            "entry": {
              "group_id": "Q1",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "I reproduced all six members on Python 3.13.16 with websockets 14.1, using my own fake CDP peer and also real headless Chromium 141. Three things go wrong. (1) When Transaction.__call__ fails to parse a reply, the exception escapes Listener.listener_loop at the `tx(**message)` call, which has no try around it. The listener dies, and the Transaction it already popped from mapper is never resolved: send() hangs forever, and neither later sends nor aclose() release it. (2) An async handler whose arity is wrong raises TypeError synchronously in the callback dispatch. The code logs it and then re-raises on purpose, which kills the listener. (3) Any exception from _receive_message other than TimeoutError, CancelledError or ConnectionClosed (JSONDecodeError, KeyError, ConcurrencyError) also kills the listener without logging anything. In cases (2) and (3) the tx stays in mapper, so an in-flight send hangs until some later send() starts a new Listener. Once the browser connection's listener is dead, every send from a flat-session tab hangs: Tab.aopen/_attach_session returns early and never revives the parent's listener, so nobody reads the shared websocket.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "SEED-L1",
