            "lens": "refute",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "Q1",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "I could not refute this group. Every member's mechanism reproduces on Python 3.13 and websockets 14.1, using a fake CDP peer, real headless Chromium 141, or both.\n\n(1) Transaction.__call__ lets any non-StopIteration exception from the generated parser or a custom generator escape. The Listener.listener_loop call `tx(**message)` has no try around it, so the listener task dies. The Transaction was already popped from mapper, so its future is never resolved, and aclose()/_fail_pending_transactions cannot release it. The send() hangs permanently.\n\n(2) In the listener_loop callback dispatch, the `raise` after logger.warning, plus the outer `except Exception: raise`, kill the listener when an async handler with the wrong arity is called.\n\n(3) A flat-session Tab's aopen/_attach_session returns early once session_id is set. Its send() restarts only the tab's own inbox Listener. When browser.connection.listener is dead, nothing reads the shared websocket, and every tab command hangs until something sends on the browser connection.\n\n(4) Any other exception from _receive_message (for example a JSONDecodeError) also kills the listener. I found no way for real Chrome to trigger this in the current code.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "SEED-L1",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Mechanism confirmed with a fake peer (probe_recv.py). A non-JSON text frame kills the listener with JSONDecodeError and the in-flight send hangs. The only trace is 'Task exception was never retrieved' at GC. Two refinements. (a) The tx stays in mapper, so aclose() fails it with ProtocolException('connection was closed'), and a later send() starts a new Listener that delivers replies still in the buffer. The hang is permanent only when the lost frame was itself the reply. (b) No real-Chrome trigger found. The ConcurrencyError route (the aopen race) is fixed on this branch by commit 31c92f1 (_open_lock), and I found no other way to get two concurrent recv() calls. Chrome caps reply nesting at about 300 levels (verified), below the json.loads RecursionError depth: about 976 on Python 3.11, about 9995 on 3.12/3.13. Chrome escapes lone surrogates (verified). Invalid UTF-8 becomes ConnectionClosed, which the loop already handles. Realism with real Chrome is low; a non-Chrome CDP endpoint or proxy that sends non-JSON frames could reach it."
