          {
            "lens": "fixsafety",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "Q1",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "Confirmed: all four listener-robustness defects are real, and each needs its own fix. (1) Transaction.__call__ (connection.py ~L112-130) catches only StopIteration. A KeyError or ValueError from a generated from_json, an exception from a custom generator, or its own explicit `raise ProtocolException('could not parse...')` escapes through `tx(**message)` in Listener.listener_loop (~L973), which is outside every try. The listener dies, and the tx was already popped from mapper, so send() hangs forever and aclose() cannot release it. (2) Callback dispatch in listener_loop (~L1000-1011) logs a handler that raises when called (e.g. wrong arity) and then re-raises, which kills the listener. (3) The receive try in listener_loop catches only TimeoutError, CancelledError and ConnectionClosed. Any other exception from _receive_message (e.g. JSONDecodeError, RuntimeError/ConcurrencyError) ends the task silently and in-flight sends hang. (4) For a flat-session tab, Connection.aopen only calls _attach_session, which returns early once session_id is set. Nothing restarts the browser connection's listener, so every tab command hangs after that listener stops. The minimal local fix has 4 hunks and passes: 9 regression tests fail (hang) on the repo and pass on the prototype, on both Python 3.13 and 3.11. 68 of the repo's own tests also pass against the prototype with real headless Chromium, and mypy shows no new errors.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "SEED-L1",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Fake websocket on py3.13: one non-JSON frame, or recv() raising RuntimeError, in front of the Browser.getVersion reply. Repo: listener task done with JSONDecodeError('Expecting value...'); send() stuck at connection.py:662 `return await tx`; tx still in mapper. Realism low: Chrome does not send non-JSON, and websockets ConcurrencyError needs a second concurrent recv(), which zendriver's Listener bookkeeping avoids. Fix hunk 3 (log, fail pending tx of the connection and its flat sessions, break) makes send raise, and the next send works. A 'log and continue' variant is unsafe: on py3.13 wait_for runs the coroutine inline, so a persistent error raised before the first suspension (ConcurrencyError) spins without yielding. probe_continue_freeze.py: 'FROZEN: event loop made no progress for 3s (heartbeat ticks 1)' vs prototype 'heartbeat ticked 50 times in ~0.5s'."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A1.1",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Fake peer answers Target.getTargetInfo with result {} -> repo: send hangs, listener exc KeyError('targetInfo'), mapper {}. Storage.getCookies with a cookie whose sameSite is 'SomeFutureValue' -> hangs, ValueError. Prototype: raises ProtocolException('could not parse the cdp response...') with __cause__ set; the same listener object keeps running and the next send works. Real-world precedent: commit f9b442b 'Fix crash in browser.cookies.get_all() on Chrome 146 when the sameParty field is missing' is exactly this class of protocol skew."
