                },
                {
                  "claim": "A browser-level command (Target.getTargets) whose parser raises kills browser.connection's listener on repo, and every later tab.evaluate hangs until something else sends on browser.connection; on the prototype both succeed",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "probe_real_chrome.py second phase: repo 'browser listener running: False exc: notThere' + 'tab.evaluate ... HUNG'; prototype 'ok -> 4'"
                },
                {
                  "claim": "Runtime.evaluate sent on the browser connection gets a CDP error reply (no Runtime domain on the browser target), so it raises ProtocolException rather than exercising the parse path",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "first probe_real_chrome.py run on repo printed 'browser.connection.send(bad custom cmd): raised ProtocolException' with listener still running"
                },
                {
                  "claim": "Chrome versions change command result shapes in ways the bundled generated cdp rejects (Chrome 146 removed Cookie.sameParty, causing a KeyError in get_cookies)",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "git show f9b442b (CHANGELOG: 'Fix crash in browser.cookies.get_all() on Chrome 146 when the sameParty field is missing'); not re-run against Chrome 146"
                },
                {
                  "claim": "Chrome never sends non-JSON text frames, so SEED-L1's JSON-decode trigger is not realistic with real Chrome",
                  "status": "assumed",
                  "how": "reasoning only; the receive-error path was demonstrated only with a fake websocket"
                }
              ],
              "realism": "high",
              "severity": "hang",
              "fix_assessment": {
                "kind": "minimal-local",
                "prototype_dir": "/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/Q1-fixsafety/pkg",
                "diff_summary": "zendriver/core/connection.py only (full diff in Q1-fixsafety/prototype.diff). 1) Transaction.__call__: add `except Exception as e:` after `except StopIteration`, building ProtocolException('could not parse the cdp response:\\n%s' % response), setting __cause__ = e, and `return self.set_exception(...)`. The trailing explicit `raise ProtocolException(...)` becomes `self.set_exception(ProtocolException(...))`. 2) Connection.aopen, flat-session branch, after `await self._attach_session(browser_connection)`: `if browser_connection.websocket is not None and (not browser_connection.listener or not browser_connection.listener.running): browser_connection.listener = Listener(browser_connection)`. 3) Listener.listener_loop receive try: add `except Exception as e:` after the ConnectionClosed clause. It logs a warning with exc_info, calls `session._fail_pending_transactions(e)` for each flat session and `self.connection._fail_pending_transactions(e)`, then `break`s; the next send() starts a new listener. 4) Listener.listener_loop callback dispatch: remove the `raise` after the per-callback logger.warning, so a faulty handler is logged and skipped.",
                "regression_test_path": "/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/Q1-fixsafety/test_q1_listener_robustness.py",
                "fails_on_repo": "yes",
                "passes_on_prototype": "yes",
                "mypy_ok": "yes",
                "risks": "No new locks, and no await between the check and the Listener() creation in hunk 2, so concurrent tab sends cannot start two readers on one websocket (no ConcurrencyError race). The check runs after _attach_lock is released, so there is no lock-ordering or re-entrancy problem. The websocket-not-None guard avoids starting a listener while the browser connection is opening (connect pending, websocket None) or after it is closed. CancelledError, KeyboardInterrupt and GeneratorExit are BaseException and stay uncaught. TimeoutError and ConnectionClosed clauses come first, so the new `except Exception` changes nothing for them. Behaviour changes callers would notice: (a) a parse failure now surfaces as ProtocolException (cause chained), not a hang. Callers that already catch ProtocolException (get_frames, _search_frames, describe_node loops, element resolve, _send_oneshot in _prepare_headless) now degrade gracefully. A custom generator that raises KeyError surfaces as ProtocolException(from KeyError), not KeyError. Setting the raw exception instead is the alternative, but it would bypass those handlers. (b) A handler that raises synchronously is logged at WARNING and the remaining handlers still run. (c) An unexpected receive error fails every in-flight command on that connection and its flat sessions with the raw exception (same style as the ConnectionClosed path). Tasks started by feed_cdp may then log 'exception never retrieved'. Replies still buffered are dropped as unknown ids when the listener restarts. Do not swap the break for continue: that freezes the event loop on py>=3.12 for persistent synchronous errors (demonstrated). Not addressed (pre-existing): a wait() already blocked on a stopped listener's idle event still hangs (setting idle on exit would fix it), and the parse-failure message embeds the full response, which may be large."
              }
            }
          }
        ]
