          {
            "lens": "refute",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "Q2",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "I tried to refute this group and could not. On real headless Chromium 141 (--headless=new, Python 3.13), a JS alert() open in the tab means DOM.getDocument and DOM.enable never get a reply. Because of that, select, find, find_all, select_all (including include_frames), xpath, wait_for, tab(...), cf_is_interactive_challenge_present and verify_cf all stay pending far past timeout=2: I watched them for 12 s, and the Chrome behaviour behind it held for more than 34 s. Nothing upstream limits the wait. Headless does not auto-dismiss the dialog. The websocket stays up (PING_TIMEOUT=900). The Target.getTargetInfo call that Connection.sleep sends does get answered, so the hang is purely in the DOM sends. The most realistic trigger is a plain script: `tab = await browser.get(page that calls alert() while loading)` returns in 0.01 s, then `await tab.select('#a', timeout=3)` hangs forever even though #a exists. Commit bd88ccd (#291) already treated exactly this 'page stuck behind a dialog' case as a bug in wait_for_ready_state, and no test asserts the current behaviour. A busy main thread that eventually finishes only makes the calls late (an 8 s busy loop made every timeout=2 call finish at about 7.6 s with the documented result); only an infinite JS loop hangs. The two side members are also real: verify_cf can run past its timeout by up to click_delay, and xpath with timeout<=0 never runs the query and otherwise spins without sleeping.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "B13.1",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Real Chromium, alert open, timeout=2: select('#nope'), select('#topp') (the element exists!), find, find_all and select_all were all still pending after 12 s. They are stuck on Transaction DOM.getDocument at tab.py:351 (Tab.select -> query_selector), :308 (Tab.find), :382 (Tab.find_all) and :423 (Tab.select_all); with include_frames=True Tab.select is stuck at :347. Also reproduced in a plain script: browser.get(alert-on-load page) then select('#a', timeout=3) was still pending after 20 s. The sleep() -> update_target call is NOT a hang point, because Target.getTargetInfo is answered while the dialog is open (verified)."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A5.1",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Same mechanism and evidence as B13.1. xpath('//nope', timeout=2) was still pending after 12 s, stuck at tab.py:473 (Tab.xpath, send(DOM.enable)), which breaks its 'It will not raise' / return-[] contract. DOM.enable gets no reply while the dialog is open (verified)."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "B13.2",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "wait_for(selector=) stuck at tab.py:1308 and wait_for(text=) at :1316 (the first query, before the while condition is ever checked). tab(selector=...) is stuck through Tab.__call__ at :1966. xpath is stuck at :473. All were still pending 12 s past timeout=2. Out of scope but worth knowing: Tab.__call__ passes (text, selector) by position into wait_for(selector, text), which swaps them. I only read the code for this, not tested."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A6.5",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "With an alert open it hangs (verified, see B13.2). With an 8 s busy main thread, wait_for(timeout=2) raised TimeoutError only after 7.61 s. That is an overshoot limited by how long the page script runs, not a hang; only an infinite loop would hang."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "B13.3",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "cf_is_interactive_challenge_present(tab, timeout=2) was stuck at cloudflare.py:120 and tab.verify_cf(timeout=2) at tab.py:1666 -> cloudflare.py:155 (verify_cf) -> :93 (cf_wait_for_interactive_challenge) -> :31 (DOM.getDocument). Both were still pending after 12 s with an alert open. With an 8 s busy page they returned False / raised TimeoutError at 8.10 s (overshoot only)."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A11.4",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Real Chromium, local page shaped like a Turnstile challenge (closed shadow root holding an iframe that mentions challenges.cloudflare.com, plus a light-DOM cf-turnstile-response input that never gets a value). verify_cf(timeout=1, click_delay=4) raised TimeoutError after 4.02 s with 1 click. (2, 0.1) took 2.01 s. With the defaults (15, 5) it took 15.03 s, because 15 is a multiple of 5 and the challenge was found at once. So the overrun is at most about click_delay plus one check_input, and it is large only for timeout/click_delay pairs that don't divide evenly or for challenges found late. It still raises the documented TimeoutError, just late. Low impact."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A5.7",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Real Chromium: xpath(\"//p[@id='topp']\", timeout=0) and timeout=-1 returned [] in 0.000 s for an element that exists, while timeout=0.001 found it; find(timeout=0) and select(timeout=0) do make one attempt. A 0.5 s xpath miss sent 1398 Tab.send calls, against 5 for select with the same timeout. The spin is limited by the timeout (default 2.5 s) and yields on every await, so the cost is wasted CDP traffic, not a hang. History: b38d12d ('simplification of xpath (#227)') removed the sleep(0.1) and moved the timeout check before the first attempt; nothing shows that was intended. Tests only use the default and 0.5 timeouts."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "Probes in /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/Q2-refute/ (chrome_behaviour.py, methods_probe.py, onload_alert.py, cf_overrun.py; output in methods_out.txt, onload_out.txt, cf_out.txt), run under `timeout` with asyncio.wait/wait_for safety nets against /home/user/zendriver/zendriver (printed zendriver.__file__), Python 3.13.16, Chromium 141.0.7390.37 headless.\n\nKey output:\n- 'select(#topp) [exists] STILL PENDING after 12.0s (timeout=2) at core/tab.py line 351'; the same for every member method, each at its own line.\n- onload_alert.py: 'browser.get returned after 0.01s' then 'select(#a, timeout=3) STILL PENDING after 20s', with the stack at Tab.select tab.py:351 on Transaction DOM.getDocument pending.\n- Busy 8 s: all timeout=2 calls done after 7.60-8.10 s with the documented outcome.\n- cf: 'verify_cf(timeout=1, click_delay=4): TimeoutError after 4.02s, clicks=1'.\n- xpath: timeout=0 -> 0 items vs timeout=0.001 -> 1 item; 1398 sends in a 0.5 s miss vs 5 for select.\n\nCode: every polling loop compares loop.time()-start_time only after an unbounded `await self.send(...)` returns, unlike wait_for_ready_state, which bd88ccd (#291) wrapped in asyncio.wait_for for exactly this dialog case.",
              "refinements": "1. A true hang needs a JS dialog (alert/confirm/prompt) open in the tab, or an infinite JS loop. Bounded busy JS only overshoots until the script yields; the result is still TimeoutError, [] or False, just late.\n2. The trigger is page state, not a caller argument. The most realistic path is a page that alerts during load or from a timer: browser.get/tab.get return right away (about 0.01-0.12 s, verified), and the next select/find/wait_for hangs, even for elements that exist.\n3. A dialog opened by the script's own element.click() or mouse_click() hangs in that click call first (verified), so in that pattern the Q2 methods are never reached.\n4. Recovery: Page.handleJavaScriptDialog returns 'No dialog is showing' unless Page.enable was sent before the dialog opened, and a late Page.enable gets no reply (verified). Navigating away with tab.get closes the dialog and DOM calls work again (verified).\n5. Keepalive never ends the wait: pongs come from the browser process and PING_TIMEOUT is 900 s. Only the mechanism is assumed; the connection itself was verified still usable after 34 s, longer than the 20 s ping_interval.\n6. Not Python-version dependent (an unbounded await on a never-completed Future); I ran 3.13 only.\n7. For A11.4, the overrun is at most click_delay plus one check_input; with the defaults and an immediate find it is about 0.03 s.\n8. For A5.7, the busy spin is limited by the timeout; the real bug is that timeout<=0 makes zero attempts.\n9. Each realism rating: the main hang is medium (pages with load or timer alerts are uncommon but real, and #77/#291 shows users hit the same thing in wait_for_ready_state), A5.7 is low-medium, A11.4 is low.",
              "chrome_claims": [
