          {
            "lens": "refute",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "Q3",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "I could not refute any member. All of them reproduce on real headless Chromium (Python 3.13.16, websockets 14.1). Q3 is really four separate defects in Browser.stop()/_stop()/_atexit_cleanup, and their realism differs a lot. (1) The atexit leak is deterministic and the most realistic. Under asyncio.run, _stop() reaches `await asyncio.to_thread(self._process.wait)` after Runner.close has already called shutdown_default_executor, gets RuntimeError('Executor shutdown has been called'), and skips _cleanup_temporary_profile. This hits every script that never calls stop(), including Ctrl+C. (2) The stop() hang needs the browser process itself to be wedged. Target.detachFromTarget (sent from Connection.aclose) and Browser.close are sent with no timeout, and both come before the terminate/kill fallback. If the caller bounds stop() with wait_for, Chrome is orphaned and the profile is left, because stop() unregistered the atexit hook first. (3) Connection.aclose catches only ProtocolException. A ConnectionClosedError escapes and aborts _stop when stop() starts before the listener has seen the socket close. (4) Concurrent stop() calls race on self._process and one of them raises AttributeError. The other call still cleans up.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "B13.5",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "p_sigstop.py ran on real Chrome after b.get('about:blank'), which already attaches a flat session, then SIGSTOP. stop() was still pending after 12 s, waiting on the 'Transaction method: Target.detachFromTarget' (Browser.stop -> _stop -> Connection.aclose). The process stayed in state T, and after cancelling, b._process was still set and the profile still existed. With no session attached (p_sigstop_nosession.py), stop() hangs instead on the Browser.close transaction. The websocket keepalive does not save it: with PING_TIMEOUT patched to 3 s, stop() finished after 33 s (ping_interval 20 + 3 + close_timeout 10) by RAISING ConnectionClosedError('keepalive ping timeout') out of aclose. The process was still SIGSTOPped and the profile still there. With the real PING_TIMEOUT=900 that bound is about 930 s, and it still never kills Chrome. Sub-claim confirmed too: Tab.close() was still pending after 13 s in its send of Target.closeTarget, outside the wait_for(future, 10) behind its documented TimeoutError. That is lower priority, since every zendriver CDP command is unbounded."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A3.4",
