              "evidence": "Scratch dir /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/Q4-reproduce/. All runs used the repo's editable zendriver; each probe prints the zendriver path, /home/user/zendriver/zendriver/__init__.py. The only patch is a spy on util._start_process that records the pid and --user-data-dir. run_probe.sh runs each probe under `timeout 120`, then checks /proc/<pid>, its ppid and the profile dir from the shell after the interpreter has exited. It then kills only the processes whose cmdline names that exact profile.\n\nCommands and key output:\n(1) `./run_probe.sh probe_cancel.py 30` (baseline): 'start completed in 0.274s', 'pid 26102 gone', 'profile ... removed'.\n(2) `./run_probe.sh probe_cancel.py 0.05` (also 0.15 and 0.22): 'start timed out after 0.051s', 'registered instances: 1', 'pid 26214 info set False connection None profile /tmp/uc_g76op5rb exists True', 'after asyncio.run: pid 26214 poll() None'. 3 s after exit: 'pid 26214 ALIVE: 26214 1 S<l 00:03 (comm chrome)', 'profile /tmp/uc_g76op5rb EXISTS', 'processes using profile: 9'.\n(3) `SLEEP=20 ./run_probe.sh probe_cancel.py 0.1`: 'pid 27365 ALIVE: 27365 1 S<l 00:20 (comm chrome)', profile EXISTS.\n(4) `./run_probe.sh probe_sigint_early.py` (SIGINT 0.1 s after the spawn, zendriver unpatched): 'KeyboardInterrupt from asyncio.run', 'registered instances: 1 [(27225, False, '/tmp/uc_9z5k9q0x')]', then 'pid 27225 ALIVE ... ppid 1', profile EXISTS.\n(5) `./run_probe.sh probe_later.py raise_ua|raise_update|cancel_ua|sigint`: each gives 'registered instances: 1', 'connection OPEN', 'profile ... exists True' after asyncio.run, then chrome ALIVE with ppid 1 and profile EXISTS after exit. Examples: 'start raised ProtocolException: simulated Target.getTargets failure', 'start raised TimeoutError after 3.00s', 'start raised CancelledError ... asyncio.run raised KeyboardInterrupt'.\n(6) `./run_probe.sh probe_fakepeer.py` (fake browser executable fakebin/fake-chrome; its websocket answers every CDP command with an error): 'start raised ProtocolException: 'Target.setDiscoverTargets' wasn't found', 'registered instances: 1', and after exit 'pid 27197 ALIVE: 27197 1 S (comm fake-chrome)', 'profile /tmp/uc_v87cucqj EXISTS'.\n\nCode read fresh: Browser.create (browser.py ~L229-232) runs `await instance.start()` and only then `asyncio_atexit.register(...)`. In Browser.start (~L571-650), `self._process = util._start_process(...)` and `util.get_registered_instances().add(self)` are followed by the test_connection loop, Connection(), set_discover_targets, update_targets, _wait_for_initial_tab and _read_headless_user_agent, with no try/except/finally. Only the `if not self.info:` branch calls `await self.stop()`. util._start_process is a plain subprocess.Popen with no start_new_session and no death signal, so Chrome outlives the parent. No other atexit, weakref.finalize or __del__ cleanup exists (Browser.__del__ is `pass`).\n\nCleanup: every Chrome or fake-chrome process I spawned was killed by its exact profile path. All 11 /tmp/uc_* profiles I created were removed (checked afterwards: none left, no processes left). No repo changes (git status clean).",
              "refinements": "1. The failed-to-connect path (`if not self.info`) does clean up. An earlier run that could not connect left no process and no profile, so the leak covers every exit except that one.\n2. The leak also happens with a fully unpatched zendriver, triggered by a plain wait_for timeout or a real SIGINT/Ctrl+C. Those are the realistic triggers. On this machine a normal start takes about 0.27 s, so a user-level timeout of 1-2 s only fires on slow or loaded machines or slow first-run profiles.\n3. In later-stage failures the leaked Browser also keeps an OPEN websocket Connection, and the registry holds it.\n4. The same leak hits the start() re-entry path `return await self.create(config=self.config)`, because it goes through the same code. I did not test that separately.\n5. Not Python-version specific: the code paths have no version-dependent behaviour. I ran Python 3.13 only.\n6. A fix also has to cover connect_existing (host and port given). There _process is None, so cleanup must not try to kill anything, but it should still discard the registry entry and close the connection.",
