            "entry": {
              "group_id": "Q4",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "Confirmed with real headless Chromium on Python 3.13. Browser.start() (zendriver/core/browser.py, Browser.start around lines 517-646) spawns Chrome with util._start_process and calls util.get_registered_instances().add(self). After that nothing catches errors: no try/except/finally, and stop() is only called on the `if not self.info` path. Browser.create (around line 196) calls asyncio_atexit.register(instance._atexit_cleanup) only after `await instance.start()` returns. So a CancelledError or any other exception after the spawn leaves three things behind: a live Chrome, the /tmp/uc_* profile, and the registry entry. The orphaned Chrome keeps running after the interpreter exits, reparented to pid 1. My refutation attempts only narrowed the claim. (1) Ctrl+C in a terminal also sends SIGINT to Chrome, because it is in the same process group (no start_new_session). Chrome exits cleanly on SIGINT, so in that case no process is orphaned, but the profile dir still leaks. (2) The time window is short on a healthy machine: start takes about 0.27 s here. (3) There is no await in start() before the spawn, so any cancellation with a positive timeout always lands after Chrome has been spawned.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "A3.3",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Reproduced: wait_for(zd.start(headless=True), 0.05) timed out. Afterwards the Chrome pid was alive, the profile existed, the Browser was still in the registry, and connection was None. After the interpreter exited, Chrome had ppid 1 and was still running 46 s later. The 'Ctrl+C orphans Chrome' part holds only when the SIGINT goes to the Python process alone (kill -INT <pid>). A terminal Ctrl+C signals the whole process group, Chrome exits (rc 0), and only the profile dir leaks. I reproduced the exception-path leak with an injected exception. A Chrome crash after /json/version leaks the profile and the registry entry, but no live process."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "B15.2",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Same root cause and same reproduction. Starts that were not cancelled cleaned up correctly (pid gone, profile removed, registry empty). The cancelled start left a live pid and the profile dir, both still there after asyncio.run returned and after the interpreter exited. Same Ctrl+C refinement as A3.3: the process survives only if the signal skips Chrome. The profile dir leaks in both cases."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "B16.4",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Same root cause. Cancelling during the test_connection retry loop leaks a live Chrome, the uc_* dir and a strong reference in the registry. An exception after the spawn also leaks them: shown with an injected RuntimeError from _read_headless_user_agent (Chrome stays alive) and with a simulated crash after /json/version that gives ConnectionRefusedError from set_discover_targets (profile and registry leak, process already gone). Its example timeouts (0.1 and 0.25) are below the ~0.27 s start time here. A healthy machine only hits this with sub-second timeouts or a hung start."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "Python 3.13.16, websockets 14.1, Chromium from the scratchpad/bin/chromium symlink. I imported the repo copy read-only (printed zendriver.__file__) and ran from the scratch dir. A spy on util._start_process recorded each pid and --user-data-dir. probe_cancel.py: baseline start took 0.274 s, and after stop the pid was gone, the profile removed and the registry empty. With T=0.05: 'timed out after 0.051s / pid 27564 alive=True profile_exists=True in_registry=True conn=None'. With T=0.3 and T=0.6 start finished and cleaned up. After the interpreter exited, `ps` showed 27564 with ppid 1 and its zygote and utility children, and the process was still alive 46 s later. probe_sigint.py, run under setsid in its own process group with SIGINT sent 30 ms after the spawn. Group mode (like a terminal Ctrl+C): KeyboardInterrupt, Chrome pid dead, profile_exists=True. Self mode (only Python signalled): Chrome alive 4.75 s later and still running with ppid 1 after exit. probe_chrome_sigint.py: a fully started headless Chrome given SIGINT exits with rc 0 in 0.10 s. probe_exc.py. Crash mode (SIGKILL Chrome right after /json/version succeeds): start raised ConnectionRefusedError, profile_exists=True, registered=1. Inject mode (RuntimeError from _read_headless_user_agent): pid alive=True, profile_exists=True, registered=1, still alive after asyncio.run. Code reading: start() has no await before util._start_process, so a positive-timeout cancel always lands after the spawn. History: the register-after-start pattern dates from the initial import (5c1a005). a51af21 (#278) refactored it into _atexit_cleanup and stop() unregistering, without touching failure paths. No test asserts the current behaviour. All Chromes I started were killed (process tree via psutil) and their profile dirs removed; ps afterwards shows no chrome processes.",
              "refinements": "1) Interactive terminal Ctrl+C does NOT orphan Chrome. Chrome is in the same foreground process group (Popen has no start_new_session/preexec_fn) and exits cleanly on SIGINT (verified). Only the /tmp/uc_* profile dir leaks, plus the registry entry, which no longer matters once the process exits. A live orphan needs the cancellation to come from inside Python (asyncio.wait_for/timeout, task.cancel, a TaskGroup sibling failing, server or app shutdown cancelling tasks, pytest-timeout) or a signal sent to the Python pid only. 2) On a healthy machine start takes about 0.27 s, so a typical user timeout (5-30 s) only fires when start hangs or is very slow. That can happen: Connection.send in set_discover_targets, update_targets or get_version has no timeout, and urlopen in test_connection uses timeout=10 per try. That is exactly when users bound start with a timeout, and retry loops then pile up orphaned Chromes. 3) Since start() has no await before the spawn, cancelling zd.start() with any positive timeout leaks every time. 4) For exceptions after the spawn: a live-process leak needs Chrome to be alive but erroring (shown only with an injected exception). A Chrome crash after /json/version leaks the profile and registry entry only. 5) Not Python-version dependent, as far as I can tell: 3.10 raises KeyboardInterrupt and then asyncio.run cancels the main task, which ends at the same CancelledError path. I only ran 3.13.",
