          {
            "lens": "fixsafety",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "Q4",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "Confirmed. Browser.start (zendriver/core/browser.py) launches Chrome with util._start_process and calls util.get_registered_instances().add(self) before it connects. The code after that point has no try/except/finally; it calls stop() only on the `if not self.info` path. Browser.create registers asyncio_atexit only after `await instance.start()` returns. So a cancel (wait_for timeout, task.cancel, Ctrl+C) or an exception from set_discover_targets, update_targets, _wait_for_initial_tab or _read_headless_user_agent leaves three things behind: the Chrome process (reparented to pid 1 after the interpreter exits), the /tmp/uc_* profile, and a permanent registry entry. The caller never gets the instance, so it cannot stop it. Minimal fix: move the post-registration body into a helper and wrap the call in `except BaseException: stop(); raise`. In attach mode (host+port) the handler must close only the connection: a plain stop() would send Browser.close and shut down the user's existing browser.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "A3.3",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Reproduced on real headless Chromium (py3.13) with my own probe: wait_for(zd.start(headless=True), 0.1) gives TimeoutError, the registry still holds 1 instance (info False, connection None), the pid is alive and /tmp/uc_* exists. After the interpreter exited, `ps` showed the chromium pid with PPID 1 and 10 processes still using the profile; I killed only those. Injected failures and hangs in update_targets, _wait_for_initial_tab and _read_headless_user_agent (both RuntimeError and wait_for cancel) all left the process alive (poll None), the profile present and registered=1."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "B15.2",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Same root cause. Confirmed with a spy on util._start_process: after the cancelled start, p.poll() is None, the user-data-dir still exists, and the process is still alive after asyncio.run() returns. Starts that complete normally clean up (poll 0, no profile left). A ProtocolException from Target.setDiscoverTargets (fake CDP peer) leaks the same way."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "B16.4",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Same root cause. A cancel during the test_connection retry loop (fake 'browser' that never listens) and a cancel after the connection is live both leak the process, the profile and the registry entry. The registry set holds a strong reference to the Browser forever."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "Real Chromium, repo code (py3.13): `T=0.1 start timed out / registered instances: 1 / pid 28194 alive True info False connection None profile /tmp/uc_z5cptu82 True / after asyncio.run pid 28194 poll None`, then `ps -o pid,ppid` showed `28194     1 S<l .../chromium`. Phase injection on repo: `phase=_read_headless_user_agent mode=hang elapsed=3.00s pid=32078 poll=None profile_exists=True registered=1 / alive after asyncio.run: [32078]` (the same for update_targets and _wait_for_initial_tab, both hang and raise). Same probes on the prototype: `phase=... poll=0 profile_exists=False registered=0 / alive after asyncio.run: []` in all 6 cases. A timeout sweep from 0.05s to 3s on the prototype left nothing behind. Regression test /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/Q4-fixsafety/tests/test_start_cleanup.py is browser-free: a fake-chrome shell script running `exec sleep 60`, plus an in-process websockets server that answers GET /json/version and fails Target.setDiscoverTargets. Repo (zendriver imported from /home/user/zendriver/zendriver/__init__.py): `3 failed in 0.56s` (`AssertionError: browser process 28988 still running`, `browser still registered`). Prototype (imported from .../Q4-fixsafety/pkg/zendriver/__init__.py): `3 passed in 1.05s`. Python 3.11 throwaway venv: untouched copy `3 failed`, prototype `3 passed`. Existing repo tests run against the prototype with real headless Chromium (test_browser.py, test_multiple_browsers.py, test_headless_user_agent.py): `27 passed`. mypy on prototype vs untouched copy: identical (332 errors, all in cdp/; 0 in core/browser.py), so no new errors. ruff format/check are clean on the prototype browser.py. Prototype diff: /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/Q4-fixsafety/fix.diff",
              "refinements": "1) Attach mode (host+port given): the triage hypothesis, plain stop() then re-raise, is unsafe. _stop sends cdp.browser.close() whenever the connection is open, so a failed attach would close the user's existing browser. I checked this with a variant prototype: test_failed_attach_does_not_close_the_existing_browser fails with `'Browser.close' in ['Target.setDiscoverTargets','Browser.close']`. The prototype closes only the connection when connect_existing. 2) Remaining gap, also present before the fix: _stop returns early when there is neither a connection nor a process (`if not self.connection and not self._process: return`), skipping _cleanup_temporary_profile. In attach mode, a cancel before the connection exists still leaves the empty uc_* dir that self.config() created via mkdtemp (verified: registry 0 but the temp dir exists on the prototype). A failure in _start_process itself also leaks that dir. Both are minor and outside the 'after Chrome is spawned' claim. 3) A second cancel arriving during cleanup interrupts stop() in its terminate/sleep loop: the process was already SIGTERMed (poll -15) and unregistered, but the profile is left. That is still much better than before (process alive, registered=1). 4) coro.close()/GeneratorExit while start() is suspended now raises RuntimeError('coroutine ignored GeneratorExit'); the process still gets SIGTERM. Before the fix close() succeeded silently and leaked the process. This happens only for start() coroutines that are closed or garbage-collected without being cancelled. 5) Suggested hardening: wrap the cleanup in `try: ... except Exception: logger.debug(..., exc_info=True)`, so an error raised inside stop() cannot replace the original exception. Replacing a CancelledError would turn wait_for's TimeoutError into that error.",
              "chrome_claims": [
                {
                  "claim": "After a cancelled zd.start the orphaned Chrome is reparented to pid 1 and keeps running after the Python interpreter exits, with its /tmp/uc_* profile left",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "Own probe real_cancel.py with wait_for(zd.start(headless=True), 0.1). After the process exited, `ps -o pid,ppid` showed PPID 1 and pgrep found 10 processes using the profile. Killed only those pids and removed the dir."
                },
                {
                  "claim": "Failures or cancels after the CDP connection is live (update_targets, _wait_for_initial_tab, _read_headless_user_agent) leak the process on repo code and are cleaned up on the prototype",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "real_phase.py patches those Browser methods to raise or hang under wait_for(…, 3) with real headless Chromium. Repo: poll=None, profile present, registered=1. Prototype: poll=0, profile removed, registered=0."
                },
                {
                  "claim": "With the prototype, Chrome receives Browser.close and exits after a failed or cancelled start; normal starts are unaffected",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "Timeout sweep 0.05s to 3s on real Chromium left no live process or profile. The 27 existing browser tests passed against the prototype."
                }
              ],
              "realism": "high",
              "severity": "leak",
              "fix_assessment": {
                "kind": "minimal-local",
                "prototype_dir": "/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/Q4-fixsafety/pkg",
                "diff_summary": "zendriver/core/browser.py, Browser.start: after `util.get_registered_instances().add(self)`, the rest of start() moves unchanged into a new `async def _connect(self, connect_existing: bool) -> None` (its trailing `return self` is removed). start() now does `try: await self._connect(connect_existing) except BaseException: if connect_existing and self.connection: await self.connection.aclose()  # do not close a browser that was not launched here; await self.stop(); raise` and then `return self`. stop() is idempotent: the second call made after the existing 'Failed to connect' path returns early, and asyncio_atexit.unregister of a callback that was never registered is a no-op. Browser.create is unchanged.",
                "regression_test_path": "/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/Q4-fixsafety/tests/test_start_cleanup.py",
                "fails_on_repo": "yes",
                "passes_on_prototype": "yes",
                "mypy_ok": "yes",
                "risks": "No locks are on the start/stop path (_update_target_info_mutex is used only in _handle_target_update), so there is no re-entrancy or lock-ordering risk. Behaviour changes callers would notice: (a) wait_for(zd.start(), T) now raises only after cleanup completes, about 0.25s later with a Chrome that honours SIGTERM (measured T=0.05 gave 0.30s; 3s hangs gave 3.3-3.4s), and up to about 3s more plus kill if Chrome ignores SIGTERM. (b) A Chrome launched by zendriver gets Browser.close on a failed start. (c) In attach mode the connection is closed but the remote browser is left running, and the instance is unregistered. (d) A user who calls `await browser.start()` directly and handles the failure gets an already-stopped instance; calling stop() again is a harmless no-op, and start() can be retried because _process is cleared. Residual risks: a second cancel during cleanup leaves the temp profile (the process is already SIGTERMed); an exception raised inside stop() would replace the original exception or CancelledError (suggest suppressing Exception from the cleanup with a debug log); GeneratorExit via coro.close() now produces RuntimeError('coroutine ignored GeneratorExit'); in attach mode a cancel before the connection exists still leaves an empty uc_* temp dir because of _stop's early return. Verified on Python 3.13 and 3.11."
              }
            }
          }
        ]
      },
