        "lenses": [
          {
            "lens": "reproduce",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "Q5",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "Confirmed on real headless Chromium 141 under Python 3.13.16. util._start_process (util.py ~L348-353) starts Chrome with stdout=PIPE and stderr=PIPE. The only reader of the stderr pipe is util._read_process_stderr, which Browser.start (browser.py ~L588-591) calls only on a failed connect. While the browser runs, nothing drains the pipe. When the 64 KiB pipe fills, the Chrome process that is logging blocks in anon_pipe_write and CDP stops answering. With --enable-logging=stderr this is the browser main process, so Runtime.evaluate, Browser.getVersion and Browser.stop() all hang. With default flags it is the network-service utility process, after about 188 navigations that hit a certificate error; Page.navigate is then never answered while Browser.getVersion still is. Draining the pipe from the test harness unblocks Chrome at once, and a control run that drains continuously never hangs, which proves the pipe is the cause. Separately, on a failed start with Chrome still alive, _read_process_stderr returns '' even though the pipe holds more than 5 KB, including 'Cannot start http server for devtools.'. Browser.start then logs 'Browser stderr: No output from browser'.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "B13.4",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "(b) Ran `probe_logging.py v1` with --enable-logging=stderr --v=1. Stderr held 43677 bytes after start, and the tab.get(data URL) + evaluate loop timed out at iteration 6 with 63816 bytes pending. The browser main thread and two child processes were in anon_pipe_write, Browser.getVersion timed out, and stop() hung in Target.detachFromTarget. (a) Ran `probe_cert.py 300 navigate` with DEFAULT flags against a self-signed https://127.0.0.1 server. Stderr grew from 5019 bytes by about 284-330 bytes per navigation (ERROR ssl_client_socket_impl.cc 'handshake failed ... net_error -202'). At iteration 188, after 605 s with 63627 bytes pending, Page.navigate got no answer within 15 s. Browser.getVersion still answered, because the blocked writer is the network-service utility process (pid 5030 in the sample lines). After the harness drained the pipe, evaluate answered again. The hang came at iteration 188 rather than the claimed 157; the mechanism is the same."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A3.5",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Ran `probe_recover.py` with --enable-logging=stderr and tab.evaluate(\"console.log('y'.repeat(100)); 1\"). Evaluate #362 timed out with 65062 bytes pending and the browser in anon_pipe_write. The harness then drained 65062 bytes, and the next evaluate('1+1') returned 2 in 0.00 s. A default-flags control (`probe_logging.py default 100`) stayed at 5073 bytes and never hung."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A9.1",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Ran `probe_logging.py get 400` with --enable-logging=stderr and tab.get(data: URL with console.error('x'.repeat(200))) + evaluate('1+1') under wait_for(10). Iteration 200 was ok with 62638 bytes pending. At iteration 203 it timed out with 63202 bytes pending and pid 32174 'chrome' in anon_pipe_write. getVersion and evaluate both timed out, and stop() hung until SIGKILL. This matches the claimed iteration 204 and about 63010 bytes."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "B16.1",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Ran `probe_logging.py evaluate 300` with evaluate(\"console.log('x'.repeat(2000)); 1+1\"). Evaluate #15 hung with 36931 bytes pending, the same numbers the finder reported. Stuck stack: Tab.evaluate tab.py:859 awaiting a pending Runtime.evaluate Transaction. The browser main thread was in anon_pipe_write. Browser.getVersion timed out, and browser.stop() did not finish within 15 s (stuck awaiting Target.detachFromTarget). The hang starts below 64 KiB here because each ~2 KB write takes its own 4 KiB pipe page, so 16 slots hold only about 37 KB. A drain-thread control (`probe_logging.py drain 100`) moved 193 KB through the pipe with no hang. With default flags and certificate errors, stderr grew 5019 -> 6297 bytes over 5 navigations."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "B13.8",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Ran `probe_stderr.py port`: the DevTools port was pre-bound (not listening) and util.free_port was patched to return it. A wrapper around util._read_process_stderr printed '5306 bytes waiting in pipe, process alive=True, returned \\'\\''. The log shows 'Timeout reading process stderr' and then 'Browser stderr: No output from browser'. A raw Popen with the same setup (`raw_port.py`) shows that the pipe content includes '[ERROR:content/browser/devtools/devtools_http_handler.cc:310] Cannot start http server for devtools.' A fake browser script that writes to stderr and keeps running gives the same empty result. A control script that writes to stderr and exits does get its stderr logged, because read(n) only returns early on EOF."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "Commands (cwd /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/Q5-reproduce, PATH including scratchpad/bin, Python /home/user/zendriver/.venv/bin/python 3.13.16, zendriver imported from /home/user/zendriver/zendriver/__init__.py, Chromium 141.0.7390.37, F_GETPIPE_SZ=65536):\n1) `timeout 120 python probe_logging.py evaluate 300` -> 'stderr pending after start 5956'; 'iteration 15: TIMEOUT after 10s; stderr pending=36931 alive=True'; stack shows Tab.evaluate tab.py:859 awaiting Runtime.evaluate; '{29019:chrome: anon_pipe_write}'; 'Browser.getVersion TIMEOUT'; 'browser.stop() did not finish within 15s' (stop() stuck awaiting Target.detachFromTarget).\n2) `probe_logging.py get 400` -> 'iteration 203: TIMEOUT ... pending=63202'.\n3) `probe_logging.py v1 100` -> start pending 43677, 'iteration 6: TIMEOUT ... pending=63816'.\n4) Controls: `probe_logging.py drain 100` -> 'no hang after 100 iterations' with drained=193871; `probe_logging.py default 100` -> pending stayed at 5073.\n5) `probe_recover.py` -> 'evaluate #362 TIMEOUT, pending=65062, browser wchan=anon_pipe_write'; after the harness drained, 'evaluate after drain -> 2 in 0.00s'; 'stop ok'.\n6) `timeout 1500 python probe_cert.py 300 navigate` (default flags, self-signed TLS) -> 'iteration 180: ok stderr pending=61639 t=568s'; 'iteration 188: navigate TIMEOUT, stderr pending=63627 after 605s'; 'Browser.getVersion answered'; stderr lines '[5030:5057:...:ERROR:net/socket/ssl_client_socket_impl.cc:902] handshake failed; ... net_error -202'; 'after draining 64 KiB, evaluate answered again'.\n7) `probe_stderr.py port|fake|exits` and `raw_port.py` -> '_read_process_stderr: 5306 bytes waiting in pipe, process alive=True, returned \\'\\''; 'LOGGED: Browser stderr: No output from browser'; the raw run showed 'Cannot start http server for devtools.' in 6187 pending bytes; with 'exits' the content is logged.\ngrep check: the only reads of _process.stderr/stdout in zendriver/ (excluding cdp/) are in util._read_process_stderr, called from browser.py ~L589 on the failure path.",
              "refinements": "1) The hang does not need a full 64 KiB. A Linux pipe has 16 page slots, and small writes merge into a page only when they fit, so lines of about 2 KB fill the pipe at about 37 KB (B16.1 hangs at #15 with 36931 bytes). 2) Which process blocks depends on who logs. With --enable-logging=stderr the browser main process blocks, so every CDP call (including Browser.getVersion) and stop() hang. With default flags and TLS errors the network-service utility process blocks, so Page.navigate and other network work hang while browser-level CDP calls still answer. 3) stdout stayed at 0 bytes in every run; the problem in practice is stderr, though stdout is undrained too. 4) The stop() hang is a consequence and sits in Browser._stop -> target.aclose / Target.detachFromTarget, which has no timeout (overlaps the separate stop() finding). 5) B13.8: read(n) on a BufferedReader blocks until n bytes or EOF, so it returns content only once Chrome has exited. Fix direction, not tested: DEVNULL for stdout, and stderr to a temp file or a drain task that keeps a bounded tail; the failure path then reads that tail.",
              "chrome_claims": [
                {
                  "claim": "Chrome's browser main thread is blocked in write(2) on the full stderr pipe when CDP stops answering (with --enable-logging=stderr)",
                  "status": "verified",
