          {
            "lens": "refute",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "Q5",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "I could not refute this group. Browser.start (browser.py) spawns Chrome through util._start_process (util.py:348-355) with stdout=PIPE and stderr=PIPE. The only reader is util._read_process_stderr, and it runs only on the connect-failure path (browser.py:589). Real headless Chromium 141 writes ERROR lines to that pipe even with zendriver's default flags. Once the pipe's 16 page slots are full, the Chrome thread that logs blocks in write(2) (wchan anon_pipe_write). Draining the pipe immediately un-wedges it, and a control run with a drain thread never hangs. Default flags are enough in a GPU-less environment: about 160 page loads that each create a WebGL context hung tab.get, evaluate and screenshot. With --enable-logging=stderr, about 10 evaluates that do console spam freeze the browser process itself, including Browser.getVersion and browser.stop(). B13.8 also holds: while Chrome is still alive, _read_process_stderr's BufferedReader.read(65536) cannot finish within 0.25 s. It returns '' and zendriver logs 'No output from browser' even though the pipe holds the exact cause. That one only affects a diagnostic message.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "B13.4",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Reproduced both variants. (a) Default flags, failing TLS handshakes (20 parallel fetches per evaluate to a self-signed https server): at round 169 (about 3400 failures, stderr pending 64401) the network-service utility process blocks (Chrome_ChildIOThread in anon_pipe_write). From then on tab.get times out, but evaluate, Browser.getVersion and stop() still work. So 'every CDP call hangs' only holds once the browser process itself logs. (b) With --enable-logging=stderr the browser main thread blocks, Browser.getVersion times out, and stop() hangs more than 40 s on Target.detachFromTarget. I did not run --v=1; --enable-logging=stderr alone is enough."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A3.5",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "--enable-logging=stderr plus 20x console.log(200 chars) per evaluate: stderr is 5890 bytes after start, and round 10 times out with 63224 pending. The browser main thread is in anon_pipe_write and Browser.getVersion times out. After one os.read drain, evaluate works again. A drain-thread control ran 60 rounds (321 KB) with no hang. One part is too narrow: 'default flags need logging enabled or a chatty build' is not true. WebGL pages in a GPU-less environment and TLS failures fill the pipe with default flags."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A9.1",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Same mechanism as A3.5. Its default-flag sources are right too: GPU errors (WebGL software fallback, about 390 B per page) and TLS handshake errors (ssl_client_socket_impl.cc:902) filled the pipe with default flags. dbus and GCM add about 5-7 KB at startup and while idle."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "B16.1",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "The exact repro holds: --enable-logging=stderr and evaluate(console.log('x'.repeat(2000))) gives 'evaluate #15 TIMEOUT; pending 36931'. This is less than 64 KiB because each roughly 2 KB write takes its own pipe page (16 slots). With 3 KB lines it blocked at #14 with 48866 pending. stop() also hangs in this state."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "B13.8",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "I held the DevTools port (bound, not listening) and pointed util.free_port at it. Chrome stays alive with 5366 bytes waiting in the pipe. _read_process_stderr returned 0 chars after exactly 0.25 s, and zendriver logged 'Browser stderr: No output from browser'. The worker thread later read those 5366 bytes, which contain '...devtools_http_handler.cc:310] Cannot start http server for devtools.', and threw them away. Mocking test_connection to return False gives the same result. Control: when Chrome exits (root without --no-sandbox, via patched is_root), stderr is captured correctly. So this hits only failures where Chrome is still alive, and the damage is diagnostic only."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "All runs used Python 3.13.16 with the repo copy (printed zendriver.__file__) and real headless Chromium 141.0.7390.37. Scripts are in /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/Q5-refute/.\n(1) p1_console.py, logging, no drain: 'started... stderr pending 5890' / 'round 10: evaluate TIMEOUT; stderr pending 63224' / browser main thread 'anon_pipe_write' / 'Browser.getVersion TIMEOUT' / 'after draining once: evaluate OK'. With a drain thread: 'no hang after 60 rounds ... drained 321490'.\n(2) p1b_stop.py: with the browser process wedged, Browser.stop() is still pending after 40 s, stuck at Target.detachFromTarget.\n(3) p8_webgl_hang.py, DEFAULT flags, headless: each nav loads a page that calls getContext('webgl'). Pending grows from 5635 to 61013 over 150 navs; 'nav 161: tab.get TIMEOUT ... pending 63933'; the blocked writer is --type=gpu-process. Afterwards evaluate, tab.get and screenshot time out, while Browser.getVersion, new tab and stop() work. The lines are 'gles2_cmd_decoder_passthrough.cc:1101 ... Automatic fallback to software WebGL has been deprecated' (159 times). The control with a drain thread ran 400 navs with no hang.\n(4) p5_default_hang.py, DEFAULT flags with TLS failures: wedges at 64401, blocked writer --type=utility (network service); evaluate and getVersion OK, tab.get times out, stop OK.\n(5) p2_default.py, DEFAULT flags: 5220 B at start. Normal pages, 404s, connection refused, DNS failures and new tabs add 0 B. console.log adds about 7 B per op (not logged). Self-signed https adds 274-346 B per navigation.\n(6) p3_idle.py, idle for 10 min: 5073 -> 6880 B.\n(7) p7_webgl.py: WebGL page load adds 393 B headless and 136 B headful under Xvfb; plain and 2d-canvas pages add 0 B.\n(8) p9_b161.py: 2 KB lines block at #15 with 36931 pending; 3 KB lines block at #14 with 48866.\n(9) p4_startfail.py portbusy: 'chrome alive=True, bytes waiting in pipe=5366' / '_read_process_stderr returned 0 chars in 0.25s' / 'LOGGED: Browser stderr: No output from browser' / the worker later read 5366 bytes containing 'Cannot start http server for devtools.'. rootsandbox: alive=False, 182 chars returned and logged.\nHistory: the pipes date from the squashed initial import 5c1a005, so there is no record that they were deliberate. The only upstream issue match is #17, which is unrelated. tests/core/test_browser.py::test_connection_error_raises_exception_and_logs_stderr only asserts the substring 'Browser stderr', so it passes with 'No output from browser'.",
              "refinements": "- Effective capacity is 16 pipe pages, not 64 KiB of bytes. With small lines Chrome blocks at about 63-64 KB. With lines just over 2 KB it blocks at about 37 KB, because each write takes its own page.\n- What hangs depends on which Chrome process logs first once the pipe is full:\n  - Browser process (--enable-logging console messages): everything, including Browser.getVersion and stop(). stop() sits on Target.detachFromTarget with no timeout; that belongs to the stop() group.\n  - GPU process (WebGL): rendering-dependent tab operations (tab.get, evaluate on that tab, screenshot). Browser.getVersion, new_tab and stop() keep working.\n  - Network service (TLS errors): navigation and network only.\n  Each process blocks only when it next logs, so the set of stuck operations grows over time.\n- Default-flag triggers are per-event ERROR logs, not volume:\n  - Each WebGL context in a GPU-less environment (Docker/CI/servers): about 390 B headless, about 136 B headful/Xvfb, so roughly 155 or roughly 440 such page loads.\n  - Each failing TLS handshake: about 170 B.\n  - console.log, normal pages, 404s, DNS failures and connection refused add nothing.\n  - Idle growth is negligible (about 1.8 KB per 10 min).\n  On a desktop with a real GPU the WebGL line is probably not emitted (assumed).\n- With --enable-logging=stderr (a common debugging flag) the hang comes within seconds.\n- stdout received 0 bytes in every run, so only stderr matters in practice. stdin=PIPE is harmless.\n- Draining fully recovers Chrome, so a drain or a file is a complete fix.\n- Python-version independent (kernel pipe semantics); I only ran 3.13.\n- Windows release Chrome does not log to stderr without --enable-logging (assumed), so Linux (and probably macOS) is the main exposure.\n- B13.8 applies only when Chrome is still alive at the failure: DevTools port race, or startup slower than about 2.5 s (default 10 x 0.25 s). The canonical root-without-sandbox and crash-at-start failures exit, hit EOF and are reported correctly. Its impact is a missing diagnostic line, which I rate cosmetic; the group's severity stays hang.",
              "chrome_claims": [
                {
                  "claim": "With zendriver's default flags Chromium writes ERROR lines to stderr (about 5 KB of dbus/GCM at startup)",
