          {
            "lens": "fixsafety",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "Q5",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "Confirmed on real headless Chromium 141 (Python 3.13). util._start_process (util.py:332-355) starts Chrome with stdout=PIPE and stderr=PIPE, and nothing reads them while Chrome runs. The only reader is _read_process_stderr (util.py:358), and it runs only on the connect-failure path in Browser.start (browser.py:588). Once the stderr pipe is full, Chrome's logging thread blocks in anon_pipe_write and CDP stops answering. This happens with --enable-logging=stderr after about 44 console.log calls, and with DEFAULT flags after about 183 navigations that fail the TLS handshake (about 288 B of ERROR log each). Separately, _read_process_stderr does a blocking read(65536) under wait_for(0.25). For a Chrome that is still alive this always times out, so Browser.start logs 'Browser stderr: No output from browser' even though Chrome wrote 'Cannot start http server for devtools.' to the pipe. A minimal local fix works: stdout=DEVNULL, plus a daemon thread that drains stderr and keeps only the last 64 KiB, which _read_process_stderr then returns. The browser-free regression tests fail on the repo code and pass on the prototype, the repo's real-browser tests pass on the prototype, and mypy reports no new errors.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "B13.4",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Default flags, repo code: I navigated (Page.navigate followed by evaluate) to https:// on a local plain-HTTP port. FIONREAD on _process.stderr went 5073 -> 61953 bytes by iteration 175, then 'iteration 183: TIMEOUT pending 64401'. With the prototype, 400 iterations ran with pending at 0 and no timeout. With --enable-logging=stderr the browser-level Browser.getVersion also timed out, and browser.stop() did not finish within 30 s; that stop() part belongs to the separate stop() finding. My byte counts differ from the finder's (288 B per navigation, not 370 B; 183 iterations, not 157), but the mechanism is the same."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A3.5",
