                },
                {
                  "fid": "A9.1",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Same root cause and the same wchan=anon_pipe_write evidence in my probe. The claim that default flags also fill the pipe over a session full of ERROR lines is reproduced too (TLS handshake failures, iteration 183)."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "B16.1",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Same mechanism: evaluate with console.log under --enable-logging=stderr hangs once the pipe fills. The exact iteration count depends on message size (44 rounds of 1 KB here). The stop() hang is part of the separate stop() finding."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "B13.8",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "I bound a socket on a port (not listening) and monkeypatched util.free_port to return it. On the repo code zd.start fails after 1.6 s and logs 'Browser stderr: No output from browser'. On the prototype the logged stderr contains '[ERROR:content/browser/devtools/devtools_http_handler.cc:310] Cannot start http server for devtools.' A browser-free test with a fake executable that writes to stderr and then sleeps gets '' from _read_process_stderr on the repo code and the message on the prototype. When the process has already exited, its stderr is read correctly on both, so that case is not a regression."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "Real Chromium runs, repo code (zendriver from /home/user/zendriver/zendriver/__init__.py, Python 3.13.16):\n(1) hang_probe.py --enable-logging=stderr: 'round 40 ok, pending 49621' / 'round 44: TIMEOUT, stderr pending 52816' / 'wchan anon_pipe_write' / 'browser getVersion TIMEOUT' / 'stop TIMEOUT'.\n(2) default_flags_probe.py 400 (default flags, TLS-failing navigations): 'iteration 175 pending 61953' / 'iteration 183: TIMEOUT pending 64401' / 'RESULT hung True'.\n(3) stderr_probe.py (port conflict): 'LOGGED: Browser stderr: No output from browser'.\nThe same probes on the prototype: 300 rounds with 'pending 0', 'stop ok in 0.4s'; 400 navigations, 'RESULT hung False'; the logged stderr contains 'Cannot start http server for devtools.'\n\nRegression tests in /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/Q5-fixsafety/tests/test_q5_stderr_pipe.py are browser-free (a fake browser script):\n- REPO (zendriver imported from /home/user/zendriver/zendriver/__init__.py): '3 failed, 2 passed in 18.06s'. The failures are test_browser_does_not_block_when_it_logs_more_than_a_pipe_holds ('Failed: the browser process blocked writing to an undrained pipe'), test_stderr_of_a_running_browser_is_read (assert ... in '') and test_start_failure_logs_stderr_of_running_browser (caplog shows 'No output from browser').\n- PROTOTYPE (zendriver imported from .../Q5-fixsafety/pkg/zendriver/__init__.py): '5 passed in 3.23s'.\n- On Python 3.11 the results are the same: untouched copy '3 failed, 2 passed', prototype '5 passed'.\n\nThe repo's tests/core/test_browser.py run on the prototype with headless Chromium: 18 passed. test_tab.py and test_connection.py: 42 passed.",
              "refinements": "Exact counts differ from the finders' numbers, but the mechanism is the same. In my runs Chrome blocked with 52-64 KiB pending (partial pages count against the 16-page pipe), not exactly 64 KiB. Default flags add about 288 B per TLS-failure navigation (finder: 370 B). Ordinary navigation and console spam without --enable-logging do not grow stderr. The stop() hang the finders mention after the pipe fills comes from the separate stop() issue: stop() sends Browser.close without a timeout. Fixing Q5 removes this trigger for it but not the underlying issue. B13.8 has a second, minor latent problem: the strict utf-8 decode in _read_process_stderr could raise UnicodeDecodeError on a read cut mid-character. The prototype decodes with errors='replace'.",
              "chrome_claims": [
                {
                  "claim": "Chrome blocks in write(2) on a full stderr pipe and stops answering CDP (page and browser target)",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "Real headless Chromium 141 with --enable-logging=stderr: evaluate timed out at round 44, /proc/<pid>/wchan read anon_pipe_write, and Browser.getVersion also timed out"
                },
                {
                  "claim": "With default flags, Chrome ERROR logging (TLS handshake failures) fills the pipe over a session",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "default_flags_probe.py: FIONREAD went 5073 -> 64401 and the navigate/evaluate timed out at iteration 183"
                },
                {
                  "claim": "On a DevTools port conflict, Chrome stays alive and writes 'Cannot start http server for devtools.' to stderr",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "The prototype's drained tail logged that exact line; the repo code logged 'No output from browser'"
                },
                {
                  "claim": "Chrome and crashpad children that inherit the stderr write end exit after browser.stop(), so the drain thread reaches EOF",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "thread_probe.py, 3 start/stop cycles: 10 processes held the pipe while running; 2 s after stop, 0 drain threads were alive, 0 processes held the write end, and the WeakKeyDictionary was empty"
                }
              ],
              "realism": "high",
              "severity": "hang",
              "fix_assessment": {
                "kind": "minimal-local",
                "prototype_dir": "/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/Q5-fixsafety/pkg",
                "diff_summary": "zendriver/core/util.py only, diff saved at /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/Q5-fixsafety/fix.diff. (1) _start_process passes stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL instead of PIPE; stdin and stderr stay PIPE. After Popen it registers _stderr_tails[process] = _StderrTail(process.stderr). (2) New class _StderrTail: a daemon thread named 'zendriver-browser-stderr' loops `while chunk := os.read(stream.fileno(), 2**16)`, appends under a threading.Lock and trims with `del self._data[:-size]`, so at most 64 KiB is kept. It closes the stream itself at EOF and catches OSError/ValueError. Its async read(n, timeout) runs `await asyncio.to_thread(self._thread.join, timeout)` (0.25 s, the same budget as before) and returns the last n bytes. (3) Module-level `_stderr_tails: weakref.WeakKeyDictionary[Popen[bytes], _StderrTail]`. (4) _read_process_stderr returns the tail decoded with errors='replace' when the process is tracked. Untracked processes still go through the old read path. browser.py is unchanged.",
                "regression_test_path": "/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/Q5-fixsafety/tests/test_q5_stderr_pipe.py",
                "fails_on_repo": "yes",
                "passes_on_prototype": "yes",
                "mypy_ok": "yes",
                "risks": "Behaviour changes: (a) browser._process.stdout is now None; it was an unread pipe. (b) browser._process.stderr is now consumed by the drain thread, so a user reading it directly would race with the thread. Both attributes are private. (c) The failure log may now contain up to 64 KiB of Chrome noise, such as dbus errors, at INFO level; before, it said 'No output' for a live Chrome. (d) Chrome's stderr is still discarded during the session apart from the tail; forwarding it to logger.debug would be an optional addition.\n\nConcurrency: one threading.Lock, held only for append/trim and the final copy. It is never held across an await, nothing else locks inside it, and no asyncio.Lock is involved, so there is no re-entrancy or lock-ordering hazard.\n\nCancel path: if Browser.start is cancelled during _read_process_stderr, the executor's join finishes within 0.25 s on its own. This is harmless.\n\nLifetime: one thread per launched browser, which exits at EOF once Chrome and its crashpad/zygote children exit; I verified this after stop(). An orphaned child that keeps the write end open keeps one daemon thread and one fd alive until it exits. The thread keeps its own reference to the stream and checks fileno() on every iteration, so the fd is not closed by GC and reused under it. Only an external process.stderr.close() racing with an in-flight read has a tiny window. If thread start fails (RuntimeError under resource exhaustion), Popen has already spawned Chrome, which is then orphaned; this edge case could be guarded.\n\nCompatibility: no new mypy errors (332 errors in both the untouched and the prototype trees, all in cdp/; util.py reports 'Success: no issues found'). The code works on Python 3.10+ (walrus; annotation-only generic subscript under `from __future__ import annotations`; tested on 3.11 and 3.13), and os.read on the pipe fd also works on Windows.\n\nAlternatives considered: DEVNULL-only would regress the exited-Chrome diagnostics that work today (test_stderr_of_an_exited_browser_is_read passes on the repo code). A temp file grows without bound under --enable-logging --v=1, and /tmp is often tmpfs."
              }
            }
          }
        ]
