        "lenses": [
          {
            "lens": "reproduce",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "Q6",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "Confirmed on Python 3.12 and 3.13, not on 3.11. In BaseRequestExpectation._request_handler (zendriver/core/expect.py, around lines 37-45) the code runs `self._remove_request_handler(); self.request_id = event.request_id; self.request_future.set_result(event)` with no done() guard. On 3.12+, Listener.listener_loop (connection.py, around lines 922-1016) drains buffered messages without yielding: wait_for no longer creates a task, and both asyncio.Queue.get on the flat-session inbox and websockets 14.1 recv return at once. It calls asyncio.create_task(callback(ev)) for every matching RequestWillBeSent before the first task runs and removes the handler. The first task resolves request_future with R1. Each later task overwrites request_id with Rn, then raises InvalidStateError, which nobody retrieves. From then on _response_handler and _loading_finished_handler match Rn. Result: .request/.value (RequestExpectation) describe R1, while .response, ResponseExpectation.value, response_body and loading_finished describe Rn. BaseFetchInterception._response_handler and DownloadExpectation._handler hit the same lost InvalidStateError on a burst; they keep the first event, so their result is still right.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "B14.3",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Real headless Chromium 141, page with 30 <img src=/imgN.png?t=..>, pattern r'.*/img\\d+\\.png\\?t=.*'. Py3.13: expect_request mismatched 5/5 (e.g. req=img0.png(27586.2), attr_id=27586.14, resp=img12.png(27586.14)) and expect_response 5/5. Py3.12: 5/5 and 4/5. 143-144 InvalidStateError('invalid state') 'Task exception was never retrieved' over 20 trials. The side claims about BaseFetchInterception/DownloadExpectation are also confirmed with the fake peer (one lost InvalidStateError each). Py3.11: 0/20 and no errors."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A2.8",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Fake peer, direct Connection: RequestWillBeSent R1(a.json) and R2(b.json) sent back-to-back. Both handler tasks ran even though the first removed the handler: .request=a.json(R1), exp.request_id=R2, .response=b.json(R2), body='body of R2', plus one lost InvalidStateError. The same pattern shows in DownloadExpectation._handler and BaseFetchInterception._response_handler (lost InvalidStateError; the first event is kept). The mismatched response is the last request in the burst, not always the last on the page (with real Chromium, img1..img29 depending on buffering)."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A10.1",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Same mechanism as the real Chromium runs: 30 images, plus 5 parallel fetches with r'.*/data\\?n=.*'. Py3.13 expect_request: req=data?n=0, resp=data?n=4, body='payload for /data?n=4' (5/5). The _loading_finished_handler match also follows the overwritten id: response_body waited on R4's loadingFinished and returned R4's body. examples/wait_for_page.py does use the broad pattern 'https://github.githubassets.com/assets/.*'."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "B15.9",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Fake CDP peer sending requestWillBeSent R1/R2, responseReceived R1/R2 and loadingFinished R1/R2 in one burst. Py3.13.16 and 3.12.3: .request url=http://x/a.json, .response url=http://x/b.json (R2), body='body of R2', lost InvalidStateError for both expect_request and expect_response. On Py3.11.17 the same script gives R1 everywhere and no errors, so the finder's suspicion that 3.10/3.11 hide the race holds for 3.11."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "B16.8",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Real Chromium page with Promise.all of 5 fetch('/data?n=i').then(r=>r.text()). Py3.13 expect_request 5/5 and expect_response 5/5: request.url=data?n=0, response.url=data?n=4, body 'payload for /data?n=4&t=..', exp.request_id equals the n=4 id. Py3.12 also 5/5. Py3.11 0/10."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "Commands (scratch dir /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/Q6-reproduce/):\n1) Real Chromium: `PATH=<scratchpad>/bin:$PATH timeout 200 /home/user/zendriver/.venv/bin/python real_chromium_probe.py`. Py3.13.16 output (run313.txt): '== request-imgs: mismatches 5/5', '== response-imgs: mismatches 5/5', '== request-fetch: mismatches 5/5', '== response-fetch: mismatches 5/5', 'request-fetch: MISMATCH req=data?n=0&t=c0(27586.125) attr_id=27586.129 resp=data?n=4&t=c0(27586.129) value=RequestWillBeSent:data?n=0&t=c0 body='payload for /data?n=4&t=c0'', 'loop exception handler calls: 143 InvalidStateError: 143 / sample: InvalidStateError('invalid state') | Task exception was never retrieved'. The same probe on a throwaway Py3.12.3 venv with a scratch copy of the package (PYTHONPATH; zendriver.__file__ printed to show the scratch copy was used): 5/5, 4/5, 5/5, 5/5 and 144 InvalidStateError (run312.txt). Py3.11.17: 0/5 in all four cases and 0 errors (run311.txt).\n2) Fake peer (websockets.asyncio.server.serve, direct Connection): `PYTHONPATH=pkg timeout 90 <py> fake_peer_probe.py`. Py3.13 and 3.12: '[request] .request url=http://x/a.json (R1)  exp.request_id=R2  .response url=http://x/b.json (R2)  body='body of R2'', 'mismatch=True  lost task errors=[\"InvalidStateError('invalid state')\"]'; also '[fetch-intercept] got P1 ... lost task errors=[InvalidStateError]' and '[download] got guid=G1 ... lost task errors=[InvalidStateError]'. Py3.11: everything R1/P1/G1 and errors=[].\n3) Handler-level test (handler_level.py): two _request_handler tasks scheduled before either runs give 'request_future -> R1  exp.request_id -> R2' and 't2 exception: InvalidStateError' on both 3.11 and 3.13. The handler is unguarded on every Python version; only the dispatch (listener_loop not yielding between buffered messages) is version-dependent.",
              "refinements": "Python-version dependent: reproduces only on Python >= 3.12 (3.12.3 and 3.13.16 tested). On 3.11, wait_for wraps each receive in a new task, so the loop yields between messages and the first handler task runs and unregisters before the next event is dispatched (0/20 real-browser trials, 0 fake-peer mismatches). Both receive paths trigger it on 3.12+: the flat-session asyncio.Queue inbox (real Chromium tab) and the owned-websocket recv (fake peer Connection). The wrong request is the last matching request in the same buffered burst, not always the page's last request. Root cause in expect.py: _request_handler mutates request_id before set_result and never checks request_future.done(). The fix should check done() before any mutation; a set_result guard alone is not enough. For BaseFetchInterception._response_handler (intercept.py ~35-42) and DownloadExpectation._handler (expect.py ~206-208), the only wrong effect is the lost InvalidStateError task: the first event is still the one returned. Unrelated observation outside the group: when page JS never reads a fetch() body, Chromium sent no Network.loadingFinished for it within 10 s, so response_body (which has no timeout) blocks.",
              "chrome_claims": [
                {
                  "claim": "Chrome delivers several matching Network.requestWillBeSent events close enough together that they are buffered and drained in one listener pass",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "Real headless Chromium 141 on Py3.13/3.12: 39/40 trials mismatched and 143-144 lost InvalidStateError tasks; the overwritten request_id came from requests in the same burst"
                },
                {
                  "claim": "Network.loadingFinished for the overwritten request arrives and response_body returns that request's body",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "Real Chromium fetch page with bodies consumed: body 'payload for /data?n=4' returned for an expectation whose .request is n=0"
                }
              ],
              "realism": "high",
              "severity": "wrong-result",
              "fix_assessment": {
