          {
            "lens": "refute",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "Q6",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "I could not refute this. On Python 3.12 and 3.13, when several Network.requestWillBeSent events that match the pattern are waiting in the inbox at once, Listener.listener_loop dispatches them back-to-back without yielding. It calls asyncio.create_task(_request_handler(ev)) once per event before the first task runs and calls _remove_request_handler(). The first task resolves request_future with R1. Each later task then overwrites self.request_id (BaseRequestExpectation._request_handler, expect.py:44) and fails on set_result with InvalidStateError, which nobody retrieves. From then on _response_handler and _loading_finished_handler match the last request (Rn). So .request/.value describe R1 while .response, ResponseExpectation.value and .response_body describe Rn. Reproduced on real headless Chromium and on a fake peer, on 3.12 and 3.13. No mismatch on 3.11.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "B14.3",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Reproduced the same mechanism with my own 8-image page on real Chromium, py3.13: 6/6 mismatched (request=img0, response=img7, exp.request_id differs from value.request_id); 42 lost InvalidStateError tasks. Not checked: its side claim that DownloadExpectation and BaseFetchInterception also produce lost InvalidStateError. My intercept probe (8 images, pattern *img*) logged 0 such errors."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A2.8",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "8 <img> tags: py3.13 6/6 and py3.12 4/4 mismatched (response usually img7, once img2 on 3.12: the burst size varies). py3.11: 0/6. The 'dispatch-time check' fix idea was not assessed."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A10.1",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "The mechanism matches what I traced and ran. The session inbox is an asyncio.Queue and Queue.get does not suspend while items are queued. On 3.12+ wait_for awaits the coroutine directly through timeouts.timeout, so it adds no task and no yield. The example (examples/wait_for_page.py) uses a broad pattern, but it only reads ResponseExpectation.value, so it gets a valid matching response that is just not the first one. Its visible symptom there is only lost-task log noise."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "B15.9",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Two scenarios. (1) Real Chromium, Promise.all of two fetches with bodies consumed: py3.13 7/8 mismatched (request a.json, response and body b.json). py3.11 0/8. (2) Fake peer burst RWBS(R1),RWBS(R2),RR(R1),RR(R2): 20/20 mismatched with 20 InvalidStateError on both 3.13 and 3.12. On 3.11: 0/20. With 20 ms gaps between frames: 0/10. This answers its open question: 3.11 hides the race."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "B16.8",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Same root cause; I reproduced it with parallel fetches instead of 5 /data requests. response_body returned the other request's body (b.json payload) for an expectation whose .request was a.json."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "Probes are in /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/Q6-refute/: real_probe.py, fake_peer.py, fake_peer_noresp.py, intercept_probe.py, debug_lf.py.\n\nCode path (read fresh):\n- BaseRequestExpectation._request_handler (expect.py:37-45) calls remove, then sets self.request_id = event.request_id, then request_future.set_result. There is no done() guard.\n- _response_handler and _loading_finished_handler (expect.py:47-65) compare against self.request_id at the moment the task runs.\n- Listener.listener_loop (connection.py:922-1016) calls asyncio.create_task(callback(*args)) per event, on a snapshot of handlers taken at dispatch time.\n- Connection._receive_message (connection.py:826) returns await self._inbox.get(). For flat-session tabs that inbox is an asyncio.Queue, which does not suspend while items are queued.\n\nResults:\n- Real headless Chromium, py3.13.16 / websockets 14.1:\n  - imgs8: 6/6 mismatched, 42 lost InvalidStateError tasks. Sample: 'request=img0.png response=img7.png exp.request_id=26855.9 req_id=26855.2 MISMATCH'.\n  - fetch2: 7/8 mismatched, body from b.json.\n  - Control with a single matching request: 0/3 mismatched, 0 errors.\n- Python 3.12.3, scratch copy: imgs8 4/4 mismatched; fake peer 20/20.\n- Python 3.11.17, scratch copy, same websockets: imgs8 0/6, fetch2 0/8, fake peer 0/20, 0 InvalidStateError. Its 20 'no websocket connection' lost-task errors come from the listener after aclose and are unrelated.\n- Fake peer with 20 ms between frames: 0/10 mismatched.\n- fake_peer_noresp.py: if the later match never gets a ResponseReceived, response_future does not resolve within 2 s, even though R1's response was dispatched, because exp.request_id is R2.\n\nRefutation attempts:\n- Nothing upstream serialises the handler tasks.\n- Keepalive or timeouts do not matter: this is a wrong result, not a wait.\n- No test asserts last-match behaviour. tests/core/test_tab.py uses exact sample_file URLs, and the docs tutorial uses '.*/user-data.json' (a single match).\n- git history (5c1a005 introduced the code, no later intent) shows self-removal meant first-match semantics, so the overwrite is not deliberate.",
              "refinements": "1. Only on Python >= 3.12. Verified: 3.11 never reproduced (real or fake peer). On 3.10 and 3.11, wait_for wraps the receive in a new task and yields once per message, so the first handler task runs and removes itself before the next event is dispatched.\n\n2. It needs at least 2 pattern-matching RequestWillBeSent events that are queued in the session inbox at the same time (same read burst). Spacing the frames 20 ms apart removes it. Exact-URL patterns never trigger it. Broad regexes plus parallel subresources or fetches trigger it almost every time (7/8 to 100%).\n\n3. ResponseExpectation.value alone still returns a genuine matching response, just not the first. The harm is visible when code pairs .request/.value with .response/.response_body, or relies on 'first match'.\n\n4. Extra consequence: if the last match in the burst never gets a response (failed, cancelled or blocked), exp.response and response_body hang forever, although the first match completed. Verified with the fake peer.\n\n5. Every burst also leaves 'Task exception was never retrieved' InvalidStateError log noise.\n\n6. The side claims about BaseFetchInterception and DownloadExpectation InvalidStateError were not reproduced. My real-Chromium intercept probe on 8 images logged 0 lost errors, so Fetch.requestPaused events apparently do not arrive in the same burst there. A done() guard would still be harmless there.\n\n7. Not this group, but noticed while testing: Chrome sent no Network.loadingFinished for a fetch whose body JS never read (within 2 s), so response_body waits forever for such fetches regardless of this race.",
              "chrome_claims": [
                {
                  "claim": "Chrome sends the requestWillBeSent events for parser-discovered <img> subresources (8 images) close enough together that they reach the tab's session inbox in one drain on py3.12/3.13",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "real_probe.py imgs8 on headless Chromium: 6/6 (3.13) and 4/4 (3.12) mismatches"
                },
                {
                  "claim": "Two fetches started together (Promise.all) produce requestWillBeSent events in the same burst",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "real_probe.py fetch2: 7/8 mismatches on py3.13"
                },
                {
                  "claim": "Chrome did not emit Network.loadingFinished for a fetch whose body was never read by JS (at least within 2 s)",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "debug_lf.py event log: RWBS and RR for a.json but no LF; LF arrived once the page called .then(r=>r.text())"
                },
                {
                  "claim": "Fetch.requestPaused events for 8 images did not arrive as a same-drain burst (no lost InvalidStateError in BaseFetchInterception)",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "intercept_probe.py on py3.13: 3 trials, 0 lost-task errors"
                },
                {
                  "claim": "On real sites with many matching subresources (e.g. github assets), bursts are as common as on my local page",
                  "status": "assumed",
                  "how": "inferred from local-page results; not tested against external sites"
                }
              ],
