          {
            "lens": "fixsafety",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "Q6",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "Confirmed on Python 3.12 and later. When several Network.requestWillBeSent events match in one burst, `expect_request`/`expect_response` pair the first matching request with the last match's response, loadingFinished and body. Each extra handler task also ends in a lost InvalidStateError. Cause: on 3.12+, Listener.listener_loop (connection.py, the create_task call at ~line 1000) dispatches a handler task for every buffered event before any of them runs. The first task removes the handler, but the tasks already created still run, and BaseRequestExpectation._request_handler (expect.py ~line 44) overwrites self.request_id each time. On Python 3.11 the listener path does not reproduce, because wait_for wraps _receive_message in a task, so the loop yields after each message. The handlers are still unsafe when they are dispatched twice, on any version. A minimal local fix in expect.py is safe. Of the two guard forms, use `request_id is not None`; the triage's `request_future.done()` early return adds a hang.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "B14.3",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Reproduced on Python 3.13 with real headless Chromium: 5/5 trials gave request img0.png with response img13/img2/img1.png, and 42 InvalidStateError task errors were lost. Also reproduced with a fake websocket. response_body uses response_future.request_id, so the body is the later request's body too. Only Python 3.12+ is affected through the Listener."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A2.8",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Same mechanism, reproduced. It applies only on Python 3.12+: on 3.11 the listener yields once per message, so the first handler task runs and removes itself before the next event is dispatched. My fake-websocket end-to-end test passes on the 3.11 repo code. The claim leaves out this version condition."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A10.1",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Reproduced (3.13, Chromium and fake peer). The DownloadExpectation._handler double dispatch also gives InvalidStateError: confirmed with a direct two-task test on both 3.11 and 3.13. I did not prototype or test BaseFetchInterception._response_handler (intercept.py); it has the same unguarded set_result by inspection."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "B15.9",
