                },
                {
                  "fid": "B16.8",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Same root cause and same outcome: request is the first match, response, body and loadingFinished are the last match, and the extra tasks raise InvalidStateError. Reproduced on 3.13 with real Chromium (image burst) and with the fake peer."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "Python 3.13.16, websockets 14.1. Regression test at /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/Q6-fixsafety/test_expect_burst.py. It uses a real Connection and Listener with websockets.connect monkeypatched to a fake websocket that queues a burst of events.\n\nRepo run (zendriver imported from /home/user/zendriver/zendriver/__init__.py):\n'request: http://test/img0.png | response: http://test/img2.png | loadingFinished: R2 | lost task errors: [InvalidStateError('invalid state'), InvalidStateError('invalid state')]', '4 failed in 0.45s'.\n\nPrototype run (PYTHONPATH=.../Q6-fixsafety/pkg, zendriver imported from .../Q6-fixsafety/pkg/zendriver/__init__.py):\n'request: http://test/img0.png | response: http://test/img0.png | loadingFinished: R0 | lost task errors: []', '4 passed in 0.42s'.\n\nPython 3.11.17, repo copy: the end-to-end burst test passes ('3 failed, 1 passed'; the failures are the direct double-dispatch and cancel-edge tests). The prototype gives '4 passed'.\n\nReal Chromium probe (probe_chromium.py: a page with 20 imgs, 5 trials):\n- repo: 'mismatches: 5/5, lost task errors: 42'\n- prototype: 'mismatches: 0/5, lost task errors: 0'\n\nThe existing test_tab.py tests matching expect/intercept/download, run on the prototype with real Chromium: '7 passed'.\n\nmypy on the prototype vs an untouched copy: identical output, 'Found 332 errors in 47 files' in both (baseline errors are mostly from cdp/), so no new errors.",
              "refinements": "1. Version scope: through Listener.listener_loop the bug only shows on Python >= 3.12. There, asyncio.wait_for no longer creates a task, and both websockets' recv() and the session asyncio.Queue.get() return buffered data without suspending. Severity is still wrong-result, and on 3.12+ it is easy to hit: any pattern that matches several resources of one page.\n2. Fix-form refinement: do not use the triage's `if self.request_future.done(): return`. A timed-out `asyncio.wait_for(exp.value, t)` cancels request_future itself (verified on 3.11 and 3.13). After that, the triage guard means no later match ever sets request_id, so `exp.response` and `response_body` hang. The repo code still resolves them, though it logs an InvalidStateError. I built that variant at .../Q6-fixsafety/alt; it fails test_response_still_resolves_after_timed_out_wait_for_request with a timeout. Instead, guard on `self.request_id is not None` and make every set_result conditional on `not future.done()`.\n3. Sibling: BaseFetchInterception._response_handler in intercept.py has the same unguarded set_result. Adding `if not self.response_future.done()` there is safe. Extra paused requests stay paused until teardown's fetch.disable either way, so that guard changes nothing users would see.\n4. A dispatch-time check in Listener (skip callbacks that are no longer registered) would change semantics for all user handlers. It is not needed here.",
              "chrome_claims": [
                {
                  "claim": "Real Chromium sends several matching Network.requestWillBeSent frames close enough together that, on Python 3.13, zendriver dispatches them before the first handler task runs, so request and response are mismatched",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "probe_chromium.py against headless Chromium serving a page with 20 <img>: 5/5 trials on repo code mismatched (img0 vs img13/img2/img1) with 42 lost InvalidStateErrors; 0/5 on the prototype"
                },
                {
                  "claim": "Each request id gets a single Network.responseReceived, so the response and loadingFinished set_result guards only matter for cancelled futures, not for normal duplicates",
                  "status": "assumed",
                  "how": "Not probed. The guards are harmless either way, and the fake-peer tests cover the cancelled-future path."
                }
              ],
              "realism": "high",
              "severity": "wrong-result",
              "fix_assessment": {
                "kind": "minimal-local",
                "prototype_dir": "/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/Q6-fixsafety/pkg",
                "diff_summary": "zendriver/core/expect.py only (full diff at .../Q6-fixsafety/prototype.diff).\n- BaseRequestExpectation._request_handler: returns early when `self.request_id is not None` (comment: matching events dispatched before the handler removed itself must not replace the tracked request), and wraps set_result in `if not self.request_future.done()`.\n- _response_handler and _loading_finished_handler: still remove themselves on a request_id match; only set_result is guarded by `if not <future>.done()`.\n- DownloadExpectation._handler: removes itself, then `if not self.future.done(): self.future.set_result(event)`.\nThe change is ruff format/check clean.",
                "regression_test_path": "/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/Q6-fixsafety/test_expect_burst.py",
                "fails_on_repo": "yes",
                "passes_on_prototype": "yes",
                "mypy_ok": "yes",
                "risks": "- Locking and cancellation: no locks are involved and the handlers contain no awaits, so there are no re-entrancy, lock-ordering or cancellation-path concerns.\n- Behaviour change: after the first match, later matching requests in the same burst are ignored (intended), and no InvalidStateError tasks are logged any more.\n- Cancelled future: if request_future was cancelled by a timed-out wait on exp.value, the first later match still sets request_id, so exp.response and response_body keep working as before.\n- Not fixed (pre-existing): handler tasks created before `reset()` that run after it can still match into the new futures. Fixing that needs a generation token.\n- Coverage gaps: intercept.py's BaseFetchInterception._response_handler is unpatched; it needs the same one-line set_result guard. The end-to-end test only fails on Python >= 3.12 (it passes on 3.11 repo code). The two direct double-dispatch tests and the cancel-edge test fail on 3.11 repo code too, so the suite still catches regressions on every supported version."
              }
            }
          }
        ]
