            "lens": "reproduce",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "Q7",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "Confirmed, deterministic, and the same on Python 3.11 and 3.13. Connection._register_handlers (connection.py:669-720) copies self.enabled_domains at the start. If it then has to auto-enable a new domain, it awaits self.send(domain.enable(), _is_update=True). That nested send still calls aopen(), and aopen() runs _register_handlers again. The nested call removes the stale auto-enabled domain (one with no handlers left, e.g. network) from self.enabled_domains. When the outer call resumes, its final loop `for ed in enabled_domains: self.enabled_domains.remove(ed)` (line 720) removes that domain a second time, outside any try, and raises ValueError: list.remove(x): x not in list. The error comes out of send -> aopen (line 458) -> _register_handlers, so the user's command is never sent. The new domain's X.enable has already gone out, so state converges to [new domain] and the next send works.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "A2.1",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Fake peer: network handler, send, remove it, add cdp.log.EntryAdded handler, send(runtime.evaluate('1+1')) raised ValueError. The peer only received ['Log.enable'] and Runtime.evaluate was never sent; enabled_domains became ['log'] and the 3rd send worked. Same with a cdp.page.LoadEventFired handler. Real headless Chromium: the same raw-send/log sequence raised ValueError. `async with tab.expect_request(URL): await tab.reload()`, then tab.add_handler(cdp.page.LoadEventFired, cb), then `await tab.reload()` raised ValueError. An HTTP hit counter stayed at 2 and no LoadEventFired arrived, so the reload never reached the browser; the next reload worked (hits=3)."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "B14.1",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Real headless Chromium: network RequestWillBeSent handler, tab.evaluate('1') OK with enabled_domains ['network'], remove it, add cdp.runtime.ConsoleAPICalled handler, tab.evaluate('2') raised ValueError; enabled_domains became ['runtime'] and evaluate('3') worked. The `async with tab.expect_response(URL): await tab.get(URL)` variant, then a LoadEventFired handler and tab.reload(), raised the same ValueError. Fake peer with the runtime handler: only Runtime.enable was sent, Runtime.evaluate was not. I did not run the side claim (two concurrent sends or a manual X.enable while level 1 is suspended). The nested level-2 call already removes the stale domain before level 1 resumes, so that path adds no new failure mode. Without a pending enable await, _register_handlers has no suspension point, so concurrent calls cannot interleave there."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "Commands (cwd = scratch dir, PATH includes scratchpad/bin for chromium):\n1) timeout 120 /home/user/zendriver/.venv/bin/python fake_peer_repro.py (py 3.13.16, websockets 14.1, zendriver from /home/user/zendriver/zendriver/__init__.py). The fake websockets server on 127.0.0.1:0 answers every request and records the methods it gets. Output: '[A2.1 log] 2nd send RAISED: ValueError: list.remove(x): x not in list', traceback send:632 -> aopen:458 -> _register_handlers:720 `self.enabled_domains.remove(ed)`, '[A2.1 log] peer saw during 2nd send: ['Log.enable'] -> Runtime.evaluate sent? False', 'enabled_domains now=['log']', '3rd send OK'. The runtime and page variants give the same result. Control (second handler also in network): '2nd send OK ... Runtime.evaluate sent? True'.\n2) Same script on Python 3.11.17 (throwaway venv, scratch copy of the package; __file__ printed from scratch pkg): identical ValueError results, so the bug does not depend on the Python version.\n3) timeout 120 .venv/bin/python chromium_repro.py (real headless Chromium plus a local http.server). 'evaluate('2') RAISED ValueError: list.remove(x): x not in list' then 'enabled_domains: ['runtime']' then 'evaluate('3') OK -> 3'. 'send(evaluate 1+1) RAISED ValueError'. expect_request block, then 'handlers after expect block: {RequestWillBeSent: 0, ResponseReceived: 0, LoadingFinished: 0}', 'enabled_domains: ['network']', '[tab.reload()] RAISED ValueError'. expect_response(tab.get) block, then '[tab.reload()] RAISED ValueError', 'enabled_domains: ['page']'.\n4) timeout 120 .venv/bin/python chromium_reload_not_sent.py: 'reload RAISED ValueError list.remove(x): x not in list', 'server hits for page before=2 after=2 LoadEventFired seen=0', 'after a 2nd reload: hits=3 LoadEventFired seen=1'.\nScripts: /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/Q7-reproduce/{fake_peer_repro.py,chromium_repro.py,chromium_reload_not_sent.py}. Every browser was stopped, no chromium process is left, the temp venv and package copy are removed, and the repo is untouched (git status clean).",
              "refinements": "- The ValueError is raised by the _register_handlers call made from aopen() (send line 632 -> aopen line 458), not the one at send line 654. Level 2 is the RH call inside the nested enable send's own aopen().\n- The trigger needs two things together: an auto-enabled domain that now has no handlers (an empty handler list is enough; remove_handlers(evt, h) leaves [] and RH pops it), and a handler for a domain that is not enabled yet, so RH awaits X.enable.\n- The public helpers expect_request and expect_response leave exactly that state: network auto-enabled with empty handler lists. So any later handler on a new domain plus any command fails once. That makes it realistic.\n- After the error, the new domain is enabled and the stale one is dropped from the list. The stale domain is never sent X.disable, but that is by design.\n- Incidental, outside this group: while building the fake peer, a deliberately malformed reply ({'result': {}} for Runtime.evaluate) made Transaction.__call__ raise inside Listener.listener_loop. That killed the listener, and the send hung until my outer timeout. It came from a malformed reply I sent myself, so its realism is low; I mention it only so other groups know about it.\n- The triage fix hypothesis (remove only an `ed` still in self.enabled_domains) would stop the crash. Recomputing against the live list after the awaits would also work. I did not prototype either.",
              "chrome_claims": [
                {
                  "claim": "On real headless Chromium, tab.evaluate after swapping a network handler for a runtime handler raises ValueError",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "chromium_repro.py run under timeout 120 printed the ValueError traceback ending at _register_handlers:720"
