          {
            "lens": "refute",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "Q7",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "I could not refute this. Connection._register_handlers (connection.py, final loop `for ed in enabled_domains: self.enabled_domains.remove(ed)`, ~line 716-720) works from a snapshot that a nested call changes. When the outer pass auto-enables a new domain it awaits `self.send(domain_mod.enable(), _is_update=True)`. That send still calls aopen(), and aopen() always runs _register_handlers (aopen ~line 458). The nested pass removes the stale auto-enabled domain from self.enabled_domains. The outer pass then tries to remove it a second time, outside any try, and gets ValueError. The exception goes up through aopen() and send() to the caller, so the user's command is never sent. Reproduced deterministically with real headless Chromium, through raw add_handler/remove_handlers and through the public expect_request helper. The state is consistent afterwards and the next send succeeds, so the failure is a one-shot exception, not a persistent breakage.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "A2.1",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Reproduced with real headless Chromium (Python 3.13). Steps: add network.RequestWillBeSent handler, evaluate, remove it, add a handler for a domain that is not enabled yet, send again. Result: ValueError list.remove(x): x not in list, traceback send -> aopen -> _register_handlers final loop. With expect_request: `async with tab.expect_request(...)` around reload, then await req.value, then add_handler(cdp.page.LoadEventFired), then tab.reload() raises the same ValueError. Page.reload never reached Chrome: 0 LoadEventFired after the failed reload, 1 after the next reload."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "B14.1",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Same root cause. Reproduced the core claim with the runtime.ConsoleAPICalled variant: the second evaluate raised ValueError. A side effect in that expression (window.__marker=42) never ran in Chrome, so the command was dropped. enabled_domains converged to ['runtime'] and the third evaluate succeeded. Not separately reproduced: the side remark that concurrent sends or a manual X.enable() during the suspension 'hit the same stale copy'. From reading the code, the manual-enable variant needs X's handler key to be iterated after the outer pass resumes, plus a concurrent X.enable(). That is contrived and does not change the verdict."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "Probe /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/Q7-refute/probe_real.py, run under `timeout 120` with real headless Chromium. zendriver was imported from /home/user/zendriver/zendriver/__init__.py on Python 3.13.16. Output: Scenario A: eval1 -> 1, enabled ['network']; eval2 RAISED ValueError list.remove(x): x not in list (tab.evaluate -> connection.send line 632 aopen -> aopen line 458 _register_handlers -> line 720 self.enabled_domains.remove(ed)); enabled after = ['runtime']; window.__marker = 'undef' (command never executed); eval3 -> 3. Scenario B (expect_request + page.LoadEventFired + reload): enabled after expect = ['network'] with all three network handler lists empty (self-removed); reload RAISED the same ValueError; LoadEventFired count 0 after the failed reload and 1 after the next reload. Scenario C (control): with any plain send between remove_handlers and add_handler, the stale domain is dropped without a nested send, and the later send with a new log handler succeeds (enabled ['log']). History: the snapshot loop and the aopen->_register_handlers call date from the root import commit 5c1a005 (code inherited from nodriver). The code comment says the loop is meant to drop unused domains; nothing suggests raising is intended. No test asserts this behaviour. tests/core/test_tab.py test_manual_disable_send and test_auto_enable_domain cover only single-domain cases. The browser was stopped in a finally block, no chromium process was left behind, and its uc_ profile dir was removed.",
              "refinements": "Trigger conditions: (1) every handler of an auto-enabled domain X is removed; expect_request/expect_response handlers remove themselves when they match, and teardown removes the rest; (2) a handler is added for a domain Y that is in neither enabled_domains nor manually_enabled_domains and is not target/storage; Y must also have an enable() command, since a domain without one raises AttributeError before any nested send; (3) the next send on that connection is the first one after both changes. If any send runs between the removal and the addition, X is dropped cleanly and the bug does not trigger. For example, awaiting response_body inside expect_response sends Network.getResponseBody, which cleans up X. The race does not depend on timing or Python version: the nested pass finishes inside the awaited call chain before the outer pass resumes, so the order is forced. Any tab command can be the trigger (evaluate, reload, navigate via browser.get on an existing tab). Internal handlers (Browser.get TargetInfoChanged, Tab.close TargetDestroyed) use the target domain, which is skipped, so zendriver cannot trigger this by itself; user handlers are required. Impact is bounded: one ValueError, the command is dropped, enabled_domains converges to the correct list, and the next send works. No hang, nothing left inconsistent.",
              "chrome_claims": [
                {
                  "claim": "The user's command is never delivered to Chrome when the ValueError is raised",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "Real headless Chromium: window.__marker stayed undefined after the failing evaluate. No LoadEventFired came after the failing reload, and one came after the next reload."
                },
                {
                  "claim": "The nested Runtime.enable / Page.enable sent by the outer _register_handlers pass reaches Chrome and succeeds even though the outer pass raises",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "ConsoleAPICalled events arrived afterwards, and LoadEventFired fired on the next reload without another enable. enabled_domains showed ['runtime'] / ['page']."
                },
                {
                  "claim": "Chrome keeps the Network domain enabled on its side after zendriver drops it from enabled_domains (zendriver never sends Network.disable here)",
                  "status": "assumed",
                  "how": "Not checked; does not affect the bug."
                }
              ],
              "realism": "medium",
              "severity": "crash",
              "fix_assessment": {
                "kind": "minimal-local",
                "prototype_dir": "",
