          {
            "lens": "fixsafety",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "Q7",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "Confirmed. Connection._register_handlers takes a copy of self.enabled_domains, then awaits send(domain_mod.enable(), _is_update=True) for any handler domain that is not enabled yet. That nested send calls aopen(), and aopen() always runs _register_handlers again (connection.py, aopen ~L458). The nested pass prunes the stale auto-enabled domain from the live list. When the outer pass resumes, its final loop calls `self.enabled_domains.remove(ed)` (~L720) for that same domain. The call is outside any try, so it raises ValueError: list.remove(x): x not in list. The error comes out of send() -> aopen(), before the user's Transaction is even created. So the user's command never reaches the wire (only Log.enable/Page.enable does), and no tx is left in mapper. The bug is deterministic and reproduces with public APIs on real headless Chromium: add_handler/remove_handlers followed by evaluate, and expect_request followed by add_handler(page.LoadEventFired) and reload. The next send succeeds because the state has converged. Minimal fix: guard the final removal with `if ed in self.enabled_domains:`.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "A2.1",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Reproduced with a fake peer for the Log/Page/Runtime domains: the user command was not sent and only Log.enable went on the wire. Reproduced on real Chromium: `async with tab.expect_request(url): await tab.reload()`, then add_handler(cdp.page.LoadEventFired), then tab.reload() raised ValueError with traceback tab.reload:841 -> send:632 -> aopen:458 -> _register_handlers:720. The trace the finder gave (outer RH snapshot, nested RH pruning network, outer cleanup removing it a second time) matches the code."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "B14.1",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Reproduced on real Chromium: add_handler(network.RequestWillBeSent), evaluate('1'), remove it, add_handler(runtime.ConsoleAPICalled), evaluate('2') -> ValueError. The prototype returns 2 with enabled_domains == [runtime]. The concurrent two-send variant also fails on repo code (one of the two gathered sends raises ValueError). The _update_manual_domain variant is covered by the same guard. It also goes through the nested pass, because RH only suspends inside the nested enable send."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "Regression tests (browser-free; an in-process websockets fake peer, plus a repo-style monkeypatched websockets.connect FakeWebSocket). Repo run (zendriver imported from /home/user/zendriver/zendriver/__init__.py): '6 failed, 2 passed in 0.38s', every failure being 'E ValueError: list.remove(x): x not in list' at /home/user/zendriver/zendriver/core/connection.py:720 via send:632 -> aopen:458. Prototype run (PYTHONPATH=.../Q7-fixsafety/pkg, zendriver imported from .../Q7-fixsafety/pkg/zendriver/__init__.py): '8 passed in 0.25s'. The 2 tests that pass on both are behaviour-preservation tests: a stale domain is still pruned without nesting, and manual-enable bookkeeping is kept. probe_unsent.py, repo: 'send raised ValueError', wire methods ['Log.enable'] (Page.bringToFront never sent), mapper empty. Prototype: 'send OK', wire ['Log.enable', 'Page.bringToFront']. Real Chromium probe_chrome.py, repo: 's1 second evaluate RAISED: ValueError' and 's2 reload RAISED: ValueError'. Prototype: 's1 second evaluate OK -> 2 [runtime]' and 's2 reload OK, enabled: [page]'. The existing tests/core/test_connection.py and tests/core/test_tab.py run against the prototype (copied tests, ZENDRIVER_TEST_BROWSERS=headless): '42 passed, 4 deselected'. Python 3.13. The bug is a plain sequential nested call with no scheduling race, so it does not depend on the Python version.",
              "refinements": "The ValueError comes from the _register_handlers call inside aopen(), the first line of send(). It is raised before the Transaction is created, so nothing is left in mapper. The only effect is that the command is not sent and the caller gets a ValueError. The nested enable failing (an error reply to Log.enable) gives the same crash, and the guard fixes that too. A stale domain is triggered only when a domain was enabled, all its handlers were removed, and a handler for a not-yet-enabled domain exists at the next send. RH with no new domain to enable never suspends, so it cannot race. Remaining design weakness (pre-existing, unchanged by the fix): a send with _is_update=True still runs a nested _register_handlers via aopen(). Bookkeeping can also drop a domain that was re-added concurrently, but the next send just re-enables it, which is harmless.",
              "chrome_claims": [
                {
                  "claim": "With repo code, real headless Chromium raises ValueError on tab.evaluate after swapping a network handler for a runtime handler",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "Ran probe_chrome.py with zendriver.start(headless=True) against the repo copy: 's1 second evaluate RAISED: ValueError list.remove(x): x not in list'."
                },
                {
                  "claim": "With repo code, expect_request followed by add_handler(page.LoadEventFired) makes tab.reload() raise ValueError",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "Ran probe_chrome.py, which serves pages from a local http.server: 's2 reload RAISED: ValueError', traceback reload:841 -> send:632 -> aopen:458 -> _register_handlers:720."
                },
                {
                  "claim": "With the prototype fix, both Chromium scenarios succeed and enabled_domains converges to the new domain",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "Same probe with PYTHONPATH=pkg: 's1 second evaluate OK -> 2 [zendriver.cdp.runtime]', 's2 reload OK, enabled: [zendriver.cdp.page]'."
                }
              ],
              "realism": "high",
              "severity": "crash",
              "fix_assessment": {
                "kind": "minimal-local",
                "prototype_dir": "/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/Q7-fixsafety/pkg",
                "diff_summary": "zendriver/core/connection.py, Connection._register_handlers final loop: `for ed in enabled_domains: self.enabled_domains.remove(ed)` becomes `for ed in enabled_domains: if ed in self.enabled_domains: self.enabled_domains.remove(ed)`, plus a 2-line comment saying a nested send (the domain.enable() above re-runs _register_handlers) or a concurrent send may already have removed it.",
                "regression_test_path": "/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/Q7-fixsafety/test_q7_repo_style.py (drop-in for tests/core/test_connection.py, same FakeWebSocket + monkeypatched websockets.connect pattern; asserts wire == ['Page.enable','Page.bringToFront'] and enabled_domains == [page]); broader suite: /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/Q7-fixsafety/test_q7_register_handlers.py",
                "fails_on_repo": "yes",
                "passes_on_prototype": "yes",
                "mypy_ok": "yes",
                "risks": "Very low. No locks are added, so there is no re-entrancy, deadlock or lock-ordering concern. RH is already re-entered through nested sends, and the guard only turns an always-failing remove into a no-op. Error and cancel paths are unchanged. The bare `except:` around the nested enable still swallows CancelledError; that is pre-existing and out of scope. The only change callers will notice: a send that used to raise ValueError now sends the command and returns its result. The final enabled_domains state is the same one the repo reached on the following send. One case is unchanged by the fix: if a stale `ed` is removed and then re-added during the suspension by a concurrent RH that saw a new handler, the outer pass still drops it from the bookkeeping, and the next send sends a redundant (idempotent) X.enable. Optional hardening is to also skip `ed` when a live handler for that domain exists, but that is not needed for this bug. mypy: 332 errors on both the prototype and an untouched copy, all in cdp/ (the exclude does not apply to out-of-repo paths), with an identical sorted diff and zero errors in connection.py, so there are no new errors. Repo connection/tab tests pass on the prototype (42 passed, headless)."
              }
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
