        "lenses": [
          {
            "lens": "reproduce",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "Q8",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "I confirmed this with my own runs, on pure Python, an in-memory Element and real headless Chromium 141. ContraDict.__getattribute__ (zendriver/core/_contradict.py, line 53) runs `if attribute in self: return self[attribute]` before any method lookup. So a stored key named clear/items/get/keys/values/update/pop/copy hides the bound dict method, and calling it raises TypeError: 'str' object is not callable. The class docstring says the opposite. Element._attrs is a ContraDict filled from DOM attributes in _make_attrs. As a result, `<br clear=all>` breaks await elem, update, query_selector, save_to_dom and remove_from_dom; an `items=` attribute breaks repr/str of the element and of any ancestor, plus Tab.get_all_urls(); a `get=` attribute breaks elem.get(), elem[...] and elem.<attr>. get_js_attributes() on any <br> also returns a ContraDict whose .clear() raises.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "A11.1",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Ran on real headless Chromium. On <br id=b clear=all>: await br, br.update(), br.query_selector('x'), br.save_to_dom() and br.remove_from_dom() all raise TypeError. The traceback ends at element.py:334 in update, at self.attrs.clear(). str(<div id=wrap>) wrapping <my-list items='[1,2]'> and repr(my-list) raise at element.py:1160 in __repr__ (self.attrs.items()). tab.get_all_urls() raises TypeError when an <a items=3 href=...> is present; absolute=False still works. On <span get=g>: s.get('id'), s['id'] and s.id all raise TypeError. br.get_js_attributes() has 'clear'='all', and .clear() on it raises. Pure check: cdict({'keys':1}).keys() raises. Controls: an element without these attribute names updates, reprs and gets normally."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "B16.15",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Ran an in-memory Element built from a cdp.dom.Node (tab=None). attributes ['clear','all'] -> attrs.clear() TypeError; ['items','[1,2]'] -> repr() TypeError; ['get','x','id','a'] -> get('id') TypeError. Control ['id','a'] gives ('a', '<div id=\"a\"></div>'). The real-Chromium run also shows the same results, including update()/await and save_to_dom on <br clear=all>."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "Python 3.13, repo copy imported (printed zendriver.__file__ = /home/user/zendriver/zendriver/__init__.py).\n\n1) Pure probe: `cd /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/Q8-reproduce && timeout 60 /home/user/zendriver/.venv/bin/python pure_probe.py`. Using ContraDict({'clear':'all','items':'3','get':'x','keys':1,'values':'v','update':'u','pop':'p','copy':'c','a':1}, silent=True): copy(), items(), get('a'), keys(), values(), update({}), pop('a') and clear() all FAIL with TypeError: 'str'/'int' object is not callable. A key set later through __setitem__ (the _make_attrs path) fails the same way. Controls: ContraDict({'a':1}).items() and .a are OK; json.dumps and dict.items(cd) are OK.\n\n2) Real Chromium probe: `PATH=.../scratchpad/bin:$PATH timeout 120 .venv/bin/python chrome_probe.py`, page served over a local http.server. Output: br.attrs = {'id': 'b', 'clear': 'all'}; FAIL await br / br.update() / br.query_selector('x') / br.save_to_dom() / br.remove_from_dom() -> TypeError: 'str' object is not callable; OK repr(br); FAIL str(wrap), FAIL repr(my-list); s.attrs = {'id':'s','get':'g'}; FAIL s.get('id'), s['id'], s.id; FAIL tab.get_all_urls(); OK tab.get_all_urls(absolute=False) -> ['http://example.com/x']; js attrs has 'clear' key: True 'all'; FAIL br.get_js_attributes().clear(). Controls: <p id=ctrl data-x=1> update/repr/get are OK.\n\n3) tb_probe.py tracebacks: element.py line 334 in update `self.attrs.clear()` TypeError; element.py line 1160 in __repr__ `self.attrs.items()` TypeError.\n\n4) inmem_probe.py: the B16.15 in-memory cases fail as described; the control passes.\n\nAll browsers I started were stopped through browser.stop() in finally blocks. The only chromium still running belonged to another agent's pytest (ppid 1069) and was left alone. The served temp dir was removed.",
              "refinements": "- remove_from_dom also breaks before doing anything, because it calls update() first. The element stays in the DOM.\n- repr(br) for <br clear=all> works, since only `items` breaks __repr__. Whether a given name breaks a given path depends on which dict method that path calls: clear -> update/await/query_selector*/save_to_dom/remove_from_dom; items -> __repr__/__str__ (for any ancestor too) and get_all_urls(absolute=True); get -> Element.get/__getitem__/__getattr__ and cloudflare's attrs.get. cloudflare.py was not exercised.\n- get_js_attributes() on any HTMLBRElement always has a 'clear' key, because the `clear` IDL property is enumerated even when the attribute is absent. So .clear() on that ContraDict always fails for <br>, and .get() fails for any element whose JS properties include 'get'.\n- The `_check_key` warning guard is never reached for stored keys, because the `in self` check comes first.\n- The behaviour predates repo history: git log on _contradict.py shows only dd9a4f8 (mypy types) and 5c1a005.\n- Fix direction is consistent with the triage hypothesis: resolve class attributes and methods (e.g. type(self) / dict methods) before falling back to keys, or read the dict directly inside Element (dict.items(self._attrs), dict.clear, dict.get).",
              "chrome_claims": [
                {
                  "claim": "<br clear=all> selected via tab.select makes await elem / elem.update() / query_selector / save_to_dom / remove_from_dom raise TypeError",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "Ran chrome_probe.py on real headless Chromium 141; traceback points to element.py:334 in Element.update (self.attrs.clear())"
                },
                {
                  "claim": "An element with an items= attribute makes repr/str of itself and its ancestors raise, and Tab.get_all_urls() raise",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "chrome_probe.py: str(#wrap) and repr(my-list) FAIL; tab.get_all_urls() FAIL with <a items=3>; traceback at element.py:1160 in __repr__"
                },
                {
                  "claim": "An element with a get= attribute makes elem.get(name), elem[name] and elem.<name> raise TypeError",
