                },
                {
                  "claim": "cloudflare challenge_iframe.attrs.get('style') raises on an iframe with a get attribute",
                  "status": "assumed",
                  "how": "Same code path (ContraDict.get shadowing) as the verified Element.get case; not exercised against cloudflare.py"
                }
              ],
              "realism": "medium",
              "severity": "crash",
              "fix_assessment": {
                "kind": "minimal-local",
                "prototype_dir": "",
                "diff_summary": "",
                "regression_test_path": "",
                "fails_on_repo": "not-run",
                "passes_on_prototype": "not-run",
                "mypy_ok": "not-run",
                "risks": "Changing __getattribute__ so class methods win changes cd.items-style attribute access for keys named after dict methods. The docstring already says those keys must be read with obj['items'], so the documented contract matches the fix. Element.__getattr__ / attrs.<name> for these names would then return the bound method instead of the value."
              }
            }
          },
          {
            "lens": "refute",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "Q8",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "I could not refute this. ContraDict.__getattribute__ (zendriver/core/_contradict.py, lines 52-58) returns self[attribute] whenever the name is a stored key, and it does this before the _check_key guard that would hand the name to dict. So a key named clear, items, get, values, keys, update, pop, copy, setdefault or popitem replaces the bound dict method with the stored string. This is the opposite of what the class docstring promises (\"won't overwrite the methods\"). Element._attrs is a ContraDict filled from the DOM attributes Chrome reports. Zendriver's own calls to self.attrs.clear() (Element.update), self.attrs.items() (Element.__repr__, Tab.get_all_urls) and self.attrs.get() (Element.get, __getitem__, __getattr__) then raise TypeError: 'str' object is not callable. I reproduced every public path in real headless Chromium, including the Google homepage HTML as served to a Chrome user agent, which contains <br clear=\"all\" id=\"lgpd\">. The bug is real and reachable, but a normal user needs an uncommon combination to hit it.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "A11.1",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Reproduced in real headless Chromium on Python 3.13: (1) <br clear=all>: br.update() and br.query_selector('x') raise TypeError; br.click(), get_position() and repr(br) still work. (2) <my-list items=...>: repr/str of the element and of an ancestor div raise TypeError; tab.get_all_urls() raises with <link items=3>, while get_all_urls(absolute=False) works. (3) <span get=g>: s.get('id'), s['id'] and s.id (deprecated __getattr__) all raise TypeError; repr and update work. (4) get_js_attributes() of a <br> contains a 'clear' key, and .clear() raises; this construction also emits a UserWarning because it is not silent. I did not exercise the cloudflare challenge_iframe.attrs.get path; an iframe with a 'get' attribute is unrealistic. Correction to 'predates repo history': the clear/items shadowing does go back to the first commit, 5c1a005. However, the Element.get crash on a 'get' attribute is a regression from d4d8117 (#289, 2026-09-26). Before that commit Element.get used getattr(self.attrs, name, None), which returns the value even when a 'get' key exists (I checked this). Element.__getitem__ already crashed before d4d8117."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "B16.15",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Same root cause, confirmed in pure Python and in real Chromium. ContraDict({k:'v'}, silent=True).<k>() raises TypeError for all ten dict method names. Element paths: clear makes update(), await elem and query_selector(_all) raise; items makes repr/str/save_to_dom raise; get makes get('id') raise. All shown as described."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "Probes are in /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/Q8-refute/ (pure_probe.py, browser_probe.py, google_probe.py), run with /home/user/zendriver/.venv/bin/python on Python 3.13.16; the output printed zendriver.__file__ as the repo copy. Pure probe: for each of clear, items, get, values, keys, copy, update, pop, setdefault and popitem, ContraDict({k:'v','a':1}, silent=True).<k>(...) gives TypeError: 'str' object is not callable. The control dict without shadow keys works, and dict.items(c) works around it. Real Chromium: CDP DOM.describeNode reports the raw attributes, e.g. #b -> ['id','b','clear','all'], #ml -> [...,'items','[1,2]'], #s -> [...,'get','g'] and feColorMatrix -> [...,'values','1 0 0 0 0']. Results: [ERR] await br (update), [ERR] br.query_selector, [ERR] repr(my-list items), [ERR] str(wrap), [ERR] s.get('id'), s['id'] and s.id, [ERR] fcm.attrs.values(), [ERR] tab.get_all_urls(), [ERR] br.get_js_attributes().clear(). Controls: [OK] br.click(), repr(br), container update and repr, tab.find, select_all('br'). One ERR line in my output ('await ml (update)') came from my probe printing str(result); update() itself works on an items-only element. Real-world page: curl of https://www.google.com/ with a Chrome UA returned HTML containing <center><br clear=\"all\" id=\"lgpd\">. I loaded it from a file in headless Chromium: select_all('br[clear]') -> [{'clear':'all','id':'lgpd'}], and iterating center.children calling query_selector_all('a') raised TypeError on the br. str(center) still worked, because clear does not break items(). In a scan of about 12 successfully fetched popular pages (google, HN, craigslist, lite.cnn, bbc, python.org, w3.org, two Wikipedia articles, mozilla and others), only Google had br[clear]; none had attributes literally named items, get, keys, update, pop or copy. History: git log -S shows the shadowing line, attrs.clear() and attrs.items() all date from the first commit, 5c1a005. No test asserts the shadowing behaviour; tests only use attrs[...] key access. browser.info (ContraDict over /json/version) has no shadowing keys (verified).",
              "refinements": "Which key breaks what: 'clear' only breaks methods that call update() on THAT element: update(), await elem, Element.query_selector(_all), set_text, save_to_dom, remove_from_dom, and the stale-node retry in Tab.query_selector(_all)(sel, _node=elem). It does not break select/find/click/repr. In practice 'clear' appears almost only on <br>, and users rarely call these methods on a <br>; a generic loop over children or select_all('*') hits it. 'items' breaks repr/str of the element and of every ancestor (because __repr__ recurses into children), save_to_dom (str(self)), and get_all_urls(absolute=True) when the attribute is on an a/link/img/script/meta element. 'get' breaks Element.get, __getitem__ and the deprecated __getattr__ for any unknown attribute name. values/keys/update/pop/copy only break user code that calls those methods on elem.attrs; zendriver itself never calls them. 'values' is common on SVG feColorMatrix/animate (verified that Chrome reports it). Matching is by exact key, so hyphenated names like data-items or data-copy are not affected. get_js_attributes() of any <br> always has a 'clear' key (HTMLBRElement.clear property, verified), but only .clear() on the result breaks. Workarounds for users: attrs['name'] and dict.items(attrs). The failure is a raised TypeError, not a hang, and it is not Python-version dependent.",
              "chrome_claims": [
                {
                  "claim": "Chrome's DOM.Node.attributes lists legacy/unknown attributes verbatim: clear on <br>, items on a custom element, get on <span>, values on SVG feColorMatrix.",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "Ran DOM.describeNode in real headless Chromium on a data: URL page"
                },
                {
