        "lenses": [
          {
            "lens": "reproduce",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "Q9",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "Confirmed. Browser.__aexit__ (browser.py:837-841) and Connection.__aexit__ (connection.py:593-599, which Tab inherits) both do `if exc_type and exc_val: raise exc_type(exc_val)` instead of returning None. I reproduced it three ways: against a fake CDP peer, against an unstarted Browser instance, and against real headless Chromium (Python 3.13.16, websockets 14.1). Any exception whose constructor needs more than one argument turns into TypeError: JSONDecodeError, UnicodeDecodeError, websockets ConnectionClosedError, and ExceptionGroup from a failing TaskGroup, so `except*` no longer matches. ProtocolException comes back as a new object with code=None and '[code: -32000]' folded into its message. KeyError('k') becomes KeyError(KeyError('k')). sys.exit(3) inside the block makes the process exit with status 1 and print '3'. A control context manager whose __aexit__ returns None passes every original exception object through unchanged. Browser.__aexit__ also never stops the browser: browser.stopped stays False and the Chrome pid is still alive after the block.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "A2.7",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Fake CDP peer answers DOM.resolveNode with error code -32000. Inside the block the ProtocolException has code=-32000. The caller of `async with conn:` gets a different object with code=None and message ending '[code: -32000]'. Real Chromium `async with tab:` + tab.send(cdp.dom.resolve_node(999999)) gives the same: 'caller sees ProtocolException code=None same_obj=False'. Connection: UnicodeDecodeError -> TypeError 'function takes exactly 5 arguments (1 given)'; ConnectionClosedError(None, None) -> TypeError 'ConnectionClosed.__init__() missing 1 required positional argument: 'sent''; ExceptionGroup -> TypeError 'BaseExceptionGroup.__new__() takes exactly 2 arguments (1 given)'; KeyError('k') -> args=(KeyError('k'),). Browser.__aexit__ behaves the same."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A3.8",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Unstarted zendriver.Browser(Config(headless=True)): ConnectionClosedError -> TypeError missing 'sent'; JSONDecodeError -> TypeError missing 'doc' and 'pos'; ProtocolException({'code':-32000,'message':'m'}) -> new instance with code=None, message 'm [code: -32000]'. Real `async with await zd.start(headless=True) as browser: json.loads('{bad')` -> TypeError, and afterwards browser.stopped=False with the Chrome pid still alive, so __aexit__ never calls stop(). On traceback loss: caught.__traceback__ only has frames ['main', '__aexit__'], but the original is still reachable through caught.__context__ (is orig: True), so it is pushed into the chained context rather than lost completely. I did not test the atexit follow-on, which is owned by another finding."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "B15.5",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "(1) json.loads('{bad') in `async with browser`/`async with conn` -> TypeError, so `except json.JSONDecodeError` does not match (also seen with real Chromium). (2) A failing asyncio.TaskGroup in the body -> `except* ValueError` MISSED, got only TypeError 'BaseExceptionGroup.__new__() takes exactly 2 arguments (1 given)' (real Chromium too). (3) probe_sysexit.py: plain sys.exit(3) -> status 3; inside `async with Browser(...)` -> prints '3', status 1; inside `async with Connection(...)` -> prints '3', status 1. (4) ProtocolException code=None. I did not test CalledProcessError or IncompleteReadError, but they fail the same way for the same multi-argument-constructor reason."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "B16.11",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Real headless Chromium, `async with await zd.start(headless=True) as browser: json.loads('{bad')` -> 'caller sees TypeError: JSONDecodeError.__init__() missing 2 required positional arguments: 'doc' and 'pos''. A TaskGroup failure in the block -> except* ValueError MISSED, got TypeError. Same result for Connection (`async with tab`/conn) through the fake peer."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "Commands (cwd = scratch dir, PATH includes scratchpad/bin for chromium):\n1) timeout 120 /home/user/zendriver/.venv/bin/python probe_fake.py  (zendriver from /home/user/zendriver/zendriver/__init__.py, python 3.13.16)\n  [Connection] real failing send -> caught ProtocolException ... same_obj=False code=None message=\"No node with given id found...[code: -32000]\"\n  [Connection]   caught.__traceback__ frames: ['main', '__aexit__'] ; caught.__context__ is orig: True\n  [Connection] raised JSONDecodeError -> caught TypeError: JSONDecodeError.__init__() missing 2 required positional arguments: 'doc' and 'pos'\n  [Connection] raised UnicodeDecodeError -> caught TypeError: function takes exactly 5 arguments (1 given)\n  [Connection] raised ConnectionClosedError -> caught TypeError: ConnectionClosed.__init__() missing 1 required positional argument: 'sent'\n  [Connection] raised ExceptionGroup -> caught TypeError: BaseExceptionGroup.__new__() takes exactly 2 arguments (1 given)\n  [Connection] raised KeyError -> caught KeyError: KeyError('k') args=(KeyError('k'),)\n  [Connection] raised ProtocolException(dict) -> code=None message='m [code: -32000]'\n  [Connection] TaskGroup failure -> except* ValueError MISSED\n  [Browser] ... the same 7 lines\n  [control] (__aexit__ returns None) -> every case same_obj=True, ProtocolException code=-32000, except* ValueError matched\n2) timeout 120 .venv/bin/python probe_real.py (real headless Chromium)\n  [real Browser] json -> caller sees TypeError: JSONDecodeError.__init__() missing 2 required positional arguments: 'doc' and 'pos'\n  [real Browser] after block: browser.stopped=False pid=7962 alive=True\n  [real Browser] taskgroup -> except* ValueError MISSED; got ['TypeError: BaseExceptionGroup.__new__() takes exactly 2 arguments (1 given)']\n  [real Tab] inside block code=-32000 / caller sees ProtocolException code=None same_obj=False\n  [real Browser] inside block code=-32000 / caller sees ProtocolException code=None same_obj=False\n3) for w in plain browser connection; do timeout 60 .venv/bin/python probe_sysexit.py $w; echo $?; done -> plain: 3 ; browser: prints '3', status 1 ; connection: prints '3', status 1\nScripts: /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/Q9-reproduce/probe_fake.py, probe_real.py, probe_sysexit.py. I stopped every browser I started (no chrome pids left) and removed the 8 empty /tmp/uc_* profile dirs my unstarted Browser configs created.",
              "refinements": "Saying the traceback is 'lost' overstates it. Because the new exception is raised inside __aexit__, the original is kept as __context__, and default printing shows it under 'During handling of the above exception, another exception occurred'. What actually changes is the type and identity of the exception the caller catches, plus its attributes, and its __traceback__ now points at __aexit__. This is not specific to a Python version (ExceptionGroup/TaskGroup cases need 3.11+; the JSONDecodeError and ProtocolException cases hit on every supported version). Connection.__aexit__ does call aclose() before re-raising. Browser.__aexit__ never calls stop(), so a separate question is whether it should. The suggested fix (just return None and let Python re-raise the original) is confirmed by my control context manager, which passes every original exception object through unchanged.",
              "chrome_claims": [
                {
                  "claim": "In real headless Chromium, a ProtocolException from DOM.resolveNode(999999) inside `async with tab:` or `async with browser:` reaches the caller with code=None",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "probe_real.py: code=-32000 inside the block, caller sees code=None, same_obj=False"
