          },
          {
            "lens": "refute",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "Q9",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "I tried to refute this and could not. Browser.__aexit__ (browser.py:837-841) and Connection.__aexit__ (connection.py:593-599, inherited by Tab) both run `raise exc_type(exc_val)` when the body raises. On Python 3.13 this turns JSONDecodeError, UnicodeDecodeError, websockets ConnectionClosedError and a TaskGroup ExceptionGroup into TypeError. Exceptions that keep their type come back as new objects with their attributes lost: ProtocolException.code, OSError.errno/filename, and KeyError args nest as (KeyError('k'),). sys.exit(3) exits with status 1. With real headless Chrome, killing the browser in the middle of a command inside `async with browser:` gives TypeError instead of ConnectionClosedError. One sub-claim is refuted: the original traceback is not lost. The new exception's __context__ is the original object, and the default traceback printer shows it under 'During handling of the above exception'. Exceptions that build from one argument and carry no extra state (ValueError, RuntimeError, TimeoutError, CancelledError) keep their type and message, and asyncio.timeout around the block still works. No test checks the current behaviour, and git history shows no intent: the line has been there since the root import 5c1a005, and dd9a4f82 only added type hints. The repo's own test wrapper, CreateBrowser.__aexit__, does the right thing (stop, then return None).",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "A2.7",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Reproduced with real Chrome on py3.13. tab.send(cdp.dom.resolve_node(node_id=cdp.dom.NodeId(999999))) gives code=-32000 with no context manager, but code=None inside `async with tab:` and inside `async with browser:`, and the exception's args become (ProtocolException,). Offline, through a Connection, UnicodeDecodeError, ConnectionClosedError(None, None) and ExceptionGroup all become TypeError, and KeyError args become (KeyError('k'),). Minor correction: the finder's snippet passes a raw int node_id, which raises AttributeError in cdp/dom.py. A NodeId(...) is needed."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A3.8",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Browser.__aexit__ confirmed for ConnectionClosedError (TypeError 'missing ... sent'), JSONDecodeError (TypeError) and ProtocolException (code=None). With real Chrome, a pending browser.connection.send at Chrome death, or a new tab.send after death, inside `async with browser:` gives TypeError. Without the context manager it is ConnectionClosedError. Refuted part: the traceback is not replaced; the original is chained as __context__. Separate root cause: __aexit__ also never calls stop(). Verified: after a normal block exit, browser.stopped is False and Chrome is still alive. At loop close, _atexit_cleanup logs 'Executor shutdown has been called'. That belongs to the atexit/cleanup finding, not this re-raise group."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "B15.5",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "All four points reproduced on py3.13.16. (1) json.loads('{bad') becomes TypeError. (2) A failing TaskGroup becomes TypeError 'BaseExceptionGroup.__new__() takes exactly 2 arguments'. (3) sys.exit(3) inside `async with Connection` prints '3' and exits 1, while a plain sys.exit(3) exits 3. (4) ProtocolException comes back with code=None and the code folded into its str. I also saw FileNotFoundError keep its type but lose errno and filename (both None)."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "B16.11",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "JSONDecodeError and TaskGroup ExceptionGroup become TypeError through both Browser and Connection __aexit__, so `except json.JSONDecodeError` and except* no longer match. 'Attributes lost' is confirmed. 'Traceback lost' is refuted: it is chained via __context__ and printed."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "All runs used py3.13.16 and the repo's zendriver (/home/user/zendriver/zendriver/__init__.py). Offline probe /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/Q9-refute/p1_offline.py, identical results for Browser and Connection: `json: JSONDecodeError -> TypeError: JSONDecodeError.__init__() missing 2 required positional arguments: 'doc' and 'pos' context_is_orig=True`; `proto: ProtocolException -> ProtocolException 'No node [code: -32000]' code=None`; `cc: ConnectionClosedError -> TypeError: ConnectionClosed.__init__() missing 1 required positional argument: 'sent'`; `unicode -> TypeError: function takes exactly 5 arguments (1 given)`; `keyerror args=(KeyError('k'),)`; `oserror: FileNotFoundError errno=None filename=None`; `group: ExceptionGroup -> TypeError: BaseExceptionGroup.__new__() takes exactly 2 arguments (1 given)`; `value: ValueError -> ValueError 'plain'`. asyncio.timeout(0.2) around `async with c/b: sleep(10)` still raises TimeoutError. The printed traceback shows the original JSONDecodeError, then 'During handling of the above exception, another exception occurred', then browser.py:841 `raise exc_type(exc_val)` TypeError. p2_sysexit.py: async-with exit status 1 versus plain status 3. Real Chrome, p3_chrome.py: (a) code=-32000 without the context manager; (b) and (c) code=None inside `async with browser` and `async with tab`; (d) after a normal `async with browser` exit, Chrome is alive and stopped=False. p4_dead.py: (f) and (g) give `TypeError: ConnectionClosed.__init__() missing 1 required positional argument: 'sent' | ctx: ConnectionClosedError` inside `async with browser`; (h) without the context manager gives ConnectionClosedError. For a tab command already pending when Chrome dies, (e) in p3, the error is ProtocolException('target session was detached') with code=None, which keeps its type. p5_example.py, the examples/wait_for_page.py pattern: 'block exited; browser.stopped = False', then 'Unhandled exception in asyncio atexit callback ... Executor shutdown has been called'.",
              "refinements": "The impact is narrower than 'everything breaks'. (1) Exceptions that build from one argument and carry no extra state (ValueError, RuntimeError, TimeoutError, CancelledError, and most exceptions zendriver raises itself) keep their type and message; asyncio.timeout and cancellation still work through the block. (2) The original traceback is kept: it is chained via __context__ and printed. An uncaught error in a script therefore shows the real cause plus a confusing extra TypeError. The real harm is to callers who catch a specific type around the block, use except*, read attributes (ProtocolException.code, OSError.errno) or depend on the sys.exit status. (3) It is only reachable when the user writes `async with browser:` or `async with tab:`. The first appears in examples/wait_for_page.py and examples/expect_download.py. The second is marked ':meta private:' and not documented, and the test suite never uses Browser.__aexit__. (4) The 'browser never stopped' part of A3.8 is a different root cause (__aexit__ has no stop) and should be tracked with the atexit-cleanup finding. (5) The bug does not depend on the Python version (3.13 tested); ExceptionGroup and TaskGroup only exist on 3.11+.",
              "chrome_claims": [
                {
                  "claim": "Chrome answers DOM.resolveNode with a nonexistent nodeId with error code -32000 'No node with given id found'",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "Real headless Chromium: p3_chrome.py case (a) printed code=-32000 without a context manager"
                },
                {
