        "lenses": [
          {
            "lens": "reproduce",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "Q10",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "Confirmed. Tab.__call__ (zendriver/core/tab.py:1952-1966) has the signature (text=None, selector=None, timeout=10) and ends with `return await self.wait_for(text, selector, timeout)`, while Tab.wait_for (tab.py:1284) is declared as wait_for(selector=None, text=None, timeout=10). Because the arguments are passed by position, the text value lands in wait_for's selector and the selector value lands in its text. On real headless Chromium, tab(text=X) and tab(X) send X to query_selector as a CSS selector. If X is not a valid selector (e.g. 'Sign up!'), DOM.querySelector raises ProtocolException at once. If it is valid but matches nothing (e.g. 'Hello needle', 'Apples'), the call polls until the timeout and raises TimeoutError, even though wait_for(text=X) finds the element in 0.00s. tab(selector=S) is sent to find_element_by_text(S) instead. It only gets the right element because DOM.performSearch also accepts CSS selectors.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "A7.1",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Own real-Chromium run: tab(text='Hello needle', timeout=2) and tab('Hello needle', timeout=2) both raised TimeoutError after 2.02s; tab.wait_for(text='Hello needle') returned <p id='p1'> in 0.00s. A trace showed that only query_selector('Hello needle') was called."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A6.8",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Both directions confirmed. The trace shows tab(text='Hello needle') calls only query_selector('Hello needle') (TimeoutError after 1.01s). tab(selector='#p1') calls only find_element_by_text('#p1'), which still returns <p id='p1'> because performSearch also takes CSS. The browser-free binding check prints the same thing: tab(selector='#p1') binds as wait_for(selector=None, text='#p1')."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A12.1",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "On the repo's tests/sample_data/groceries.html, tab(text='Apples', timeout=1) raised TimeoutError after 1.01s; wait_for(text='Apples') returned the <script> element (the first text match). tab(text='Sign up!') raised ProtocolException 'DOM Error while querying ... DOM.querySelector selector Sign up!' in 0.00s. This is an immediate exception from an invalid selector, not a process crash, so the real severity is wrong-result rather than the finder's 'crash'."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "B15.11",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Same root cause, reproduced with my own page: tab(text=...) on text that is present raises TimeoutError('time ran out while waiting') after the full timeout, while wait_for(text=...) finds it. The swapped call has been there since commit 5c1a005 (git log -S), and dd9a4f8 later added the await."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "Code: tab.py Tab.__call__ line 1966 `return await self.wait_for(text, selector, timeout)` vs Tab.wait_for line 1284 `def wait_for(self, selector=None, text=None, timeout=10)`.\n\n1) Browser-free (patched an instance's wait_for and bound the args against the real signature):\n`cd /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/Q10-reproduce && timeout 60 /home/user/zendriver/.venv/bin/python probe_args.py`\nOutput:\ntab(text='Hello needle') raw=('Hello needle', None, 10) -> wait_for bound {'selector': 'Hello needle', 'text': None, 'timeout': 10}\ntab(selector='#p1') raw=(None, '#p1', 10) -> wait_for bound {'selector': None, 'text': '#p1', 'timeout': 10}\n\n2) Real headless Chromium, Python 3.13 (`SCR=$S timeout 120 /home/user/zendriver/.venv/bin/python probe_real.py`):\n[wait_for(text='Hello needle', timeout=2)] OK in 0.00s -> <p id='p1'>\n[tab(text='Hello needle', timeout=2)] EXC in 2.02s -> TimeoutError: time ran out while waiting\n[tab('Hello needle', timeout=2) [positional]] EXC in 2.02s -> TimeoutError\n[wait_for(text='Sign up!')] OK -> <button id='b'>\n[tab(text='Sign up!')] EXC in 0.00s -> ProtocolException: DOM Error while querying command:DOM.querySelector params:{... 'selector': 'Sign up!'} [code: -32000]\n[tab(text='Apples')] EXC in 2.02s -> TimeoutError (wait_for(text='Apples') OK -> <li>)\n[tab(selector='#p1')] OK -> <p id='p1'> (calls: [('find_element_by_text', '#p1')])\n[tab(text='Hello needle') traced] calls: [('query_selector', 'Hello needle')]\n\n3) probe_real2.py on tests/sample_data/groceries.html: wait_for(text='Apples') OK -> <script>; tab(text='Apples', timeout=1) EXC in 1.01s -> TimeoutError.\n\nAll probes printed zendriver from /home/user/zendriver/zendriver/__init__.py. Every browser was stopped with browser.stop() and no chromium process was left running.",
              "refinements": "Two outcomes for tab(text=X), depending on X: if X is a valid CSS selector that matches nothing, it polls until the timeout and raises TimeoutError. If X is not a valid selector (contains '!', spaces with punctuation, etc.), query_selector raises ProtocolException at once (and disable_dom_agent is called first). If a text string happens to be a valid selector that matches an element (e.g. tab(text='button')), the call returns the CSS match, not a text match. tab(selector=S) is a text search for S; in my runs ('#p1', 'li', 'button') it still returned the right element because performSearch also accepts selectors. I did not find a selector input where it returns a wrong element. The fix the triage proposes, self.wait_for(selector=selector, text=text, timeout=timeout), is local and keeps the public signature. The docstring (\"alias to query_selector_all or find_elements_by_text\") is also inaccurate, since it returns a single element through wait_for.",
