          {
            "lens": "refute",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "Q10",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "I could not refute this. Tab.__call__(self, text=None, selector=None, timeout=10) (zendriver/core/tab.py, Tab.__call__, line 1966) ends with `return await self.wait_for(text, selector, timeout)`. Those arguments are positional, but the signature is Tab.wait_for(self, selector=None, text=None, timeout=10) (line 1284). So the text goes into the selector slot and the selector goes into the text slot. I reproduced it with real headless Chromium on Python 3.13: tab(text=...) and tab('...') run DOM.querySelector on the text. For plain words this polls until the timeout and raises TimeoutError. For text that is not a valid selector, such as 'Sign up!', it raises ProtocolException at once. tab(selector=...) runs DOM.performSearch on the selector. That returns the same element as wait_for(selector=...) when the CSS selector matches. When it does not match, it returns a wrong element (any node whose text contains the selector string) instead of waiting or raising.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "A7.1",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Real Chrome: tab(text='Hello needle', timeout=2) and positional tab('Hello needle', timeout=2) both raised TimeoutError after 2.02s, while wait_for(text='Hello needle') returned <p id=p1> in 0.00s. The claim that tab(selector=...) works 'by luck' is only partly right. It holds when the CSS selector matches; otherwise it returns a wrong element (see refinements)."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A6.8",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Same repro: tab(text='Hello needle') timed out after the full 2s; wait_for(text=...) found the <p>. tab(selector='#p1') returned the correct <p> through the text-search path (DOM.performSearch accepts CSS; verified in Chrome)."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A12.1",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Confirmed. tab(text='Apples', timeout=2) raised TimeoutError while wait_for(text='Apples') returned the <li>. Its sub-claim also holds: tab(text='Sign up!') raised ProtocolException 'DOM Error while querying' from DOM.querySelector in 0.00s. Its finder severity of 'crash' is overstated; the exception propagates to the caller like any other error, so wrong-result is the better label."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "B15.11",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Confirmed with the equivalent repro. tab(text=...) treats the text as a CSS selector and raises TimeoutError('time ran out while waiting') after the timeout, even though wait_for(text=...) finds the element at once. git blame shows the positional call has been unchanged since before the repo's shallow root (5c1a005). dd9a4f8 only added `await`."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "Own probes, run against real headless Chromium with the repo's zendriver (zendriver.__file__ = /home/user/zendriver/zendriver/__init__.py, Python 3.13.16). Scripts: /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/Q10-refute/probe_real.py, probe_sel.py, probe_sel2.py.\n\nOutput:\n- `[tab(text='Hello needle', timeout=2)] TimeoutError 2.02s`\n- `[tab('Hello needle', timeout=2) positional] TimeoutError 2.02s`\n- `[wait_for(text='Hello needle', timeout=2)] OK 0.00s -> <p id=\"p1\">`\n- `[tab(text='Apples')] TimeoutError 2.02s`, versus `wait_for(text='Apples') -> <li>Apples</li>`\n- `[tab(text='Sign up!')] ProtocolException 0.00s: DOM Error while querying command:DOM.querySelector params:{'selector': 'Sign up!'}`\n- `tab(text='Hello needle', selector='#b1')` returned the #b1 button only after 2.02s, because it first polled querySelector('Hello needle') for the whole timeout. wait_for with the same arguments returned in 0.00s.\n- When the CSS selector matches (#p1, p, li, a, button, div, title, '.wrap', 'ul > li:nth-child(2)'), tab(selector=X) returned the same element as wait_for(selector=X).\n- On a page with no <li>/<table>/<h1> elements, tab(selector='li') returned `<span>linkedin</span>` and tab(selector='table') / tab(selector='h1') returned `<p>a table of h1 stuff</p>`. wait_for(selector=...) correctly raised TimeoutError in all three cases.\n\nRefutation attempts that failed:\n- Upstream serialisation or caller paths: none. Nothing in zendriver/, tests/, examples/ or docs calls Tab.__call__, so no caller compensates.\n- Tests: none assert the current behaviour.\n- Intent in history: none. The docstring says text= means a text search and selector= means a CSS query. git blame shows line 1966 last touched by dd9a4f8 (mypy strict), which only added `await` and kept the order.\n- query_selector does not swallow errors or fall back to text matching.",
              "refinements": "1. tab(text=X) and positional tab(X) never use the text search first. If X is a valid but non-matching selector, the call always waits the full timeout and then raises TimeoutError. If X is not a valid CSS selector (contains '!', starts with a digit, and so on), it raises ProtocolException at once.\n2. tab(selector=S) is not merely 'works by luck'. If the CSS matches, it returns the same element, but through the slower find_elements_by_text path, which fetches the whole document and runs performSearch. If the CSS does not match yet, it returns a false positive: any element whose text, tag name or attribute contains S (verified: 'li' matched a <span>linkedin</span>). So code that uses tab(selector=...) to wait for an element can return immediately with the wrong element.\n3. With both arguments, the text is polled as CSS for the whole timeout before the selector is tried as text.\n4. Severity is wrong-result, not crash. Python-version independent (pure argument-order bug).",
