              "severity": "wrong-result",
              "fix_assessment": {
                "kind": "minimal-local",
                "prototype_dir": "/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/Q10-fixsafety/pkg",
                "diff_summary": "zendriver/core/tab.py Tab.__call__: `return await self.wait_for(text, selector, timeout)` -> `return await self.wait_for(selector=selector, text=text, timeout=timeout)`. One line. Signature and docstring unchanged.",
                "regression_test_path": "/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/Q10-fixsafety/test_q10_call_args.py",
                "fails_on_repo": "yes",
                "passes_on_prototype": "yes",
                "mypy_ok": "yes",
                "risks": "No locks, tasks or cancel paths are involved; the fix only changes argument forwarding, so re-entrancy and deadlock risks do not apply. The error path is unchanged: wait_for still raises asyncio.TimeoutError, now from the correct branch. mypy on the prototype and on an untouched copy gives the same 332 pre-existing errors (diff is empty after normalising paths), so the fix adds none. Changes callers could notice: (a) code that relied on the bug by passing CSS positionally, like tab('div.x'), now gets a text search. performSearch still accepts CSS, so this mostly keeps working, but a string that also appears as page text could return a different element. (b) tab(selector=S) with an invalid CSS S now raises ProtocolException from query_selector, as wait_for(selector=S) already does, where it used to succeed through text search. (c) tab(text=T, selector=S) now tries the selector first and then the text in the remaining time, following wait_for's semantics. All three changes follow the documented signature, so the fix's semantics are right. The alternative of reordering __call__'s parameters to (selector, text) would break the documented positional-text form and is not recommended."
