        "lenses": [
          {
            "lens": "reproduce",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "Q11",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "Tab.wait_for_ready_state (zendriver/core/tab.py, inner _poll, the `if ready_state == until:` line, ~1343) uses strict equality. document.readyState only moves forward (loading -> interactive -> complete), so once a page is 'complete', the default until='interactive' and until='loading' never match. The call polls every 0.1s until the timeout and then raises TimeoutError('time ran out while waiting for load page until interactive'). I reproduced this on real headless Chromium and with a browser-free instance patch. In the normal flow (browser.get(url, new_tab=True) and then wait_for_ready_state() with nothing in between), a small file:// page was already 'complete' on the first evaluate, so the default call failed every time.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "A6.1",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Own reproducer on real headless Chromium, Python 3.13.16. The page was 'complete'. default/'interactive' raised TimeoutError after 3.00s (timeout=3) and 'loading' raised TimeoutError after 2.00s (timeout=2). The control 'complete' returned True in 0.00s. Also reproduced browser-free: an instance-patched Tab.evaluate always returning 'complete' made wait_for_ready_state(timeout=2) poll 20 times and raise TimeoutError after 2.00s."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "Commands (cwd /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/Q11-reproduce, PATH prefixed with the scratchpad/bin chromium symlink):\n1) timeout 120 /home/user/zendriver/.venv/bin/python repro_chromium.py\n   zendriver from: /home/user/zendriver/zendriver/__init__.py python 3.13.16\n   readyState after 1s: complete\n   [default (interactive) on complete page] EXC in 3.00s -> TimeoutError: time ran out while waiting for load page until interactive\n   [until='interactive' on complete page] EXC in 3.00s -> TimeoutError: time ran out while waiting for load page until interactive\n   [until='loading' on complete page] EXC in 2.00s -> TimeoutError: time ran out while waiting for load page until loading\n   [until='complete' on complete page (control)] returned True in 0.00s\n   readyState immediately after get (new tab): complete\n   [get() then default wait, no sleep] EXC in 3.00s -> TimeoutError: time ran out while waiting for load page until interactive\n2) timeout 60 /home/user/zendriver/.venv/bin/python repro_nobrowser.py  (Tab.__new__(Tab), with tab.evaluate patched on the instance to return 'complete')\n   TimeoutError after 2.00s, evaluate polled 20 times: time ran out while waiting for load page until interactive\nCode read fresh: Tab.wait_for_ready_state (tab.py:1326-1355). The `ready_state == until` check is at ~1343. Its docstring says 'Waits for the page to reach a certain ready state', and 'reach' implies 'at or past'. Nothing else in zendriver/ (outside cdp/) calls it. Every call in the repo tests passes 'complete' explicitly, so the default path is never exercised there. Browser stopped in finally, the temp page dir was removed, no chromium processes were left (ps count 0), and git status in the repo is clean.",
              "refinements": "The claim holds as written. One addition: the TimeoutError is not limited to pages that were already complete before the call. If a page goes loading -> complete between two 0.1s polls, the poll never sees 'interactive' and the call times out the same way. I only argued this from the code; my runs did not trigger it, since the page was already complete. until='loading' fails on any page past loading, so it is nearly unusable after Browser.get. The impact is the full timeout spent (10s by default) followed by a spurious TimeoutError, not a hang.",
              "chrome_claims": [
                {
                  "claim": "A fast file:// page is already 'complete' immediately after Browser.get(new_tab=True) returns",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "repro_chromium.py printed 'readyState immediately after get (new tab): complete' on real headless Chromium"
                },
                {
                  "claim": "document.readyState never goes backwards on the same document",
                  "status": "assumed",
                  "how": "HTML spec semantics; I did not test a new navigation, which resets the document"
                }
              ],
              "realism": "high",
              "severity": "wrong-result",
              "fix_assessment": {
                "kind": "minimal-local",
                "prototype_dir": "",
                "diff_summary": "",
                "regression_test_path": "",
                "fails_on_repo": "not-run",
