          },
          {
            "lens": "refute",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "Q11",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "Tab.wait_for_ready_state (zendriver/core/tab.py, inner _poll, line ~1343) checks `ready_state == until`. A page that has already passed the target state never matches. The default until='interactive' (and until='loading') then polls for the whole timeout and raises TimeoutError, even though the page went past 'interactive' long before. I could not refute it. Nothing upstream reaches 'complete' later than the call: Tab.get waits for listener idle after Page.navigate, so a fast page is usually 'complete' by the time get() returns. No keepalive or close path ends the wait early. Git history and tests show no sign the equality is deliberate. I reproduced it deterministically against real headless Chromium.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "A6.1",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Reproduced with my own probe on real headless Chromium, Python 3.13.16, repo copy imported. browser.get(fast http page) gave readyState 'complete'. wait_for_ready_state(timeout=3) raised TimeoutError('...until interactive') after 3.00s, and until='loading' raised after 2.00s. until='complete' returned True in 0.00s. A file:// page loaded with tab.get gave the same result. The same call also fails on a slow-image page once that page reaches 'complete'. While that page was still 'interactive' (image delayed 4s), the default call returned True in 0.00s."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "Probe: /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/Q11-refute/probe_ready.py, run under `timeout 120`, with an outer asyncio.wait_for(…, 30) around each call. It starts its own local ThreadingHTTPServer and one headless browser, stopped in finally. Output:\n- fast http page readyState right after browser.get: complete\n- [default(interactive) fast http] EXC TimeoutError: time ran out while waiting for load page until interactive in 3.00s\n- [complete fast http] OK True in 0.00s\n- [loading fast http] EXC TimeoutError ... until loading in 2.00s\n- file page readyState right after tab.get: complete; default call EXC TimeoutError in 3.00s\n- slow page readyState right after browser.get(new_tab): interactive; default call OK True in 0.00s\n- [complete slow page] OK True in 4.09s; default call AFTER complete EXC TimeoutError in 3.00s\n- document.open() readyState transitions: complete->loading->complete\n\nHistory: git blame shows the `ready_state == until` comparison has been there since the method was added (root 5c1a005; CHANGELOG 'Add tab.wait_for_ready_state ... @3mora2'). bd88ccd4 (#291) only wrapped the loop in asyncio.wait_for and kept the equality unchanged. No commit message or comment says exact-match semantics are intended. The docstring says 'Waits for the page to reach a certain ready state', which implies 'at least'. A search of upstream cdpdriver/zendriver issues found only #77 (the hang-forever timeout bug), nothing on the equality. Tests: test_wait_for_ready_state and every other in-repo caller (tests, docs tutorial, examples/wait_for_page.py) pass 'complete', the terminal state, which the bug does not affect. No test asserts the exact-match behaviour, and none uses the default.",
              "refinements": "- Only targets earlier than the page's current state are affected. until='complete' always works, and that is what every in-repo test, doc and example uses, which explains why the suite stays green.\n- The default 'interactive' fails only if the page is already 'complete' at the first poll, or reaches 'complete' between 100ms polls. In practice that covers nearly any static or fast page after browser.get/tab.get, because Tab.get waits for listener idle (verified: readyState was already 'complete' when get() returned).\n- The default succeeds on pages whose 'interactive' phase is still in progress when the call is made (verified with a slow subresource).\n- until='loading' is almost useless today: it succeeds only if a poll lands while the new document is still loading.\n- Edge case for the fix: document.open() moves readyState backwards, from 'complete' to 'loading' (verified in Chromium). A rank-based >= check would treat that document as not yet 'interactive' until it closes, which is arguably correct.\n- Effect: the call raises TimeoutError after `timeout` (default 10s) instead of returning True at once. That is wrong-result (a spurious exception and wasted time), not a hang.",
              "chrome_claims": [
                {
                  "claim": "After browser.get(url) on a fast local http page, document.readyState is already 'complete' when get() returns",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "Real headless Chromium probe printed 'fast http page readyState right after browser.get: complete'"
                },
                {
                  "claim": "After tab.get(file://...) on a small file page, readyState is already 'complete'",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "The probe printed 'file page readyState right after tab.get: complete'"
                },
                {
                  "claim": "A page with a slow subresource sits in 'interactive' until the subresource finishes, and the default call succeeds during that window",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "The /slowpage image was delayed 4s. readyState after browser.get(new_tab) was 'interactive', the default call returned True in 0.00s, and 'complete' arrived after 4.09s"
                },
                {
                  "claim": "readyState never goes backwards for the same document",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "Refuted for document.open(): Chromium returned complete->loading->complete. pushState left it at 'complete'. A real navigation creates a new document, which starts over at 'loading' (assumed per the HTML spec, not separately probed)"
                },
                {
                  "claim": "readyState goes loading -> interactive -> complete in that order",
                  "status": "assumed",
                  "how": "HTML spec. Consistent with the observed interactive-then-complete sequence on the slow page"
                }
              ],
              "realism": "high",
              "severity": "wrong-result",
              "fix_assessment": {
                "kind": "minimal-local",
                "prototype_dir": "",
                "diff_summary": "",
                "regression_test_path": "",
                "fails_on_repo": "not-run",
                "passes_on_prototype": "not-run",
                "mypy_ok": "not-run",
                "risks": "Not assessed under this lens. Suggested fix: compare ordinal ranks {'loading':0,'interactive':1,'complete':2} with >=. The document.open() regression to 'loading' is the only non-monotonic case seen, and it is harmless with a rank-based check."
              }
            }
          },
          {
            "lens": "fixsafety",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "Q11",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "Confirmed. In Tab.wait_for_ready_state (zendriver/core/tab.py, _poll at about line 1343) the check is `ready_state == until`. A document's readyState only moves forward, so a page that is already 'complete' never equals 'interactive' or 'loading'. The default call `await tab.wait_for_ready_state()` (until='interactive') on a page that has finished loading therefore polls until the timeout and then raises TimeoutError('time ran out while waiting for load page until interactive'). I reproduced this with real headless Chromium (Python 3.13.16) and with a browser-free regression test. The fix is a minimal local one: accept the requested state or any later state. The test fails on the repo code (5 failed, 5 passed) and passes on the prototype (10 passed). mypy shows no new errors.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "A6.1",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Real Chromium, repo copy (/home/user/zendriver/zendriver/__init__.py). After browser.get(file://page.html), readyState is already 'complete'. until='interactive', until='loading' and the default each raise TimeoutError after exactly 2.00s (timeout=2). until='complete' returns at once. With the prototype all four return True in 0.00s."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "Code read fresh: Tab.wait_for_ready_state, _poll: `ready_state = await self.evaluate(\"document.readyState\"); if ready_state == until: return True`. This has been there since the method was added (git log -S). The default is until='interactive'.\n\nReal Chromium probe (/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/Q11-fixsafety/probe_real.py, run under `timeout 120`, Python 3.13.16, browser.stop() in finally).\nREPO, zendriver: /home/user/zendriver/zendriver/__init__.py:\n  readyState right after get: complete\n  until=interactive: TimeoutError(time ran out while waiting for load page until interactive) after 2.00s\n  until=loading: TimeoutError(time ran out while waiting for load page until loading) after 2.00s\n  until=complete: returned True in 0.00s\n  until=default: TimeoutError(time ran out while waiting for load page until interactive) after 2.00s\nPROTO, zendriver: .../Q11-fixsafety/pkg/zendriver/__init__.py:\n  until=interactive/loading/complete/default: returned True in 0.00s\n\nBrowser-free regression test (/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/Q11-fixsafety/test_q11_ready_state.py). It builds Tab.__new__ and monkeypatches the instance's evaluate to return a scripted sequence of readyState values; an outer asyncio.wait_for(…, 5) is the safety net.\nRepo run (zendriver imported from: /home/user/zendriver/zendriver/__init__.py): FAILED [interactive-complete], [loading-interactive], [loading-complete], test_wait_for_ready_state_default_on_complete_page, test_wait_for_ready_state_keeps_polling_on_unexpected_value -> '5 failed, 5 passed in 11.56s'.\nPrototype run (zendriver imported from: .../Q11-fixsafety/pkg/zendriver/__init__.py): '10 passed in 1.81s'.\n\nThe repo's own tests, run on the prototype with copied tests/, PYTHONPATH=pkg:rt and ZENDRIVER_TEST_BROWSERS=headless:\n- test_tab.py -k ready_state etc.: 7 passed, including test_wait_for_ready_state and test_wait_for_ready_state_times_out_when_evaluate_hangs\n- test_mouse, test_multiple_browsers, test_react_controlled_input, test_keyinputs: 11 passed",
              "refinements": "- until='loading' is also broken: it times out on any page that is no longer loading. Under the fixed meaning ('this state or a later one') it is trivially true once a document exists.\n- until='complete', the value every in-repo caller and test uses, is unaffected. That is why the existing test suite never caught this.\n- The bug does not depend on the Python version.\n- The docstring already says 'Waits for the page to reach a certain ready state' and documents `:raises asyncio.TimeoutError: If the timeout is reached before the ready state is reached`. A page that is 'complete' has reached 'interactive', so the current behaviour contradicts the documented contract. 'Reached' is also how Playwright's wait_for_load_state behaves: it resolves immediately if the state was already reached.",
              "chrome_claims": [
                {
                  "claim": "After browser.get(file_url) on a small page, document.readyState is already 'complete' at the first evaluate",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "probe_real.py against real headless Chromium printed 'readyState right after get: complete'"
                },
                {
                  "claim": "wait_for_ready_state() with default/interactive/loading raises TimeoutError after the full timeout on that page",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "probe_real.py, repo copy: TimeoutError after 2.00s for each with timeout=2; until='complete' returned in 0.00s"
                },
                {
                  "claim": "Page.navigate returns after the new document commits, so the old document's 'complete' state cannot satisfy 'interactive' under the fix",
                  "status": "assumed",
                  "how": "Not verified. If it were wrong, the same race would already exist today for until='complete' with ==, so the fix adds no new kind of race."
                }
              ],
              "realism": "high",
              "severity": "wrong-result",
              "fix_assessment": {
                "kind": "minimal-local",
                "prototype_dir": "/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/Q11-fixsafety/pkg",
                "diff_summary": "zendriver/core/tab.py, Tab.wait_for_ready_state. Before `async def _poll()` add:\n    # document.readyState only moves forward, so a later state also means\n    # the requested one was reached\n    states = (\"loading\", \"interactive\", \"complete\")\n    reached = states[states.index(until) :]\nand in _poll change `if ready_state == until:` to `if ready_state in reached:`.\nThe timeout, the error message and the evaluate-hang handling from #291 are unchanged. ruff format and ruff check are clean. Suggested CHANGELOG Unreleased/Fixed entry: '`Tab.wait_for_ready_state` no longer times out when the page is already past the requested state, such as the default `until=\"interactive\"` on a page that has finished loading'. Optionally add to the docstring that it returns immediately if the page is already in that state or a later one.",
                "regression_test_path": "/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/Q11-fixsafety/test_q11_ready_state.py",
                "fails_on_repo": "yes",
                "passes_on_prototype": "yes",
                "mypy_ok": "yes",
                "risks": "- Locks and re-entrancy: no locks or shared state are touched, so there is no re-entrancy, deadlock or lock-ordering concern.\n- Cancel and error paths: unchanged. The index is computed outside asyncio.wait_for, so the existing timeout still covers evaluate(). A ProtocolException from evaluate (for example a context destroyed during navigation) still propagates as before.\n- Unexpected values: None, '' or any other unexpected readyState value is not in `reached`, so polling continues as today. The regression test covers this.\n- Invalid `until` (behaviour change): a value outside the Literal, such as 'complet', now raises ValueError from tuple.index immediately instead of polling and then raising TimeoutError. mypy already rejects such values at the call site. If even that change is unwanted, use `reached = states[states.index(until):] if until in states else (until,)`.\n- until='loading' (behaviour change): it now returns at once on any document. Code that relied on == to detect a new navigation entering 'loading' after an old 'complete' document would return early, but that was already racy because a 100ms poll can miss the loading phase.\n- until='interactive' and other callers: 'interactive' can now be satisfied by a 'complete' document, which is the intended fix. Callers using 'complete' (all in-repo callers and tests) are unaffected; the repo tests that use it pass on the prototype.\n- mypy: the prototype and an untouched copy each report 332 pre-existing errors, all path-related in cdp/ modules, with an empty diff. Neither reports any error in core/tab.py, and `uv run mypy` on the repo itself reports no issues."
              }
            }
          }
