          {
            "lens": "reproduce",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "Q12",
              "verdict": "partially-confirmed",
              "summary": "Root cause confirmed for 2 of the 3 sites. (1) The bare `except:` in Connection._register_handlers (connection.py:709) catches the CancelledError raised at the nested `await self.send(domain_mod.enable(), _is_update=True)`. The outer send keeps going, re-sends the same X.enable (the handler took the domain back out of enabled_domains, and send's own `await self._register_handlers()` runs again), then sends the real command. A wait_for timeout or task.cancel() is then ignored: the call either returns normally later or hangs for good. Later cancels are swallowed as well. Zendriver's own timeouts are defeated too: Tab.wait_for_ready_state(timeout=2) hung for more than 15s on a real Chromium page blocked by alert() once a handler for an un-enabled domain existed. (2) The `try: await self.flash() except: pass` swallow is real, but it is in Element.mouse_click (element.py:574-577), not Element.click. Element.click calls flash(0.25) with no try and passes cancellation on correctly. (3) The `except BaseException` in UpstreamProxy.open_tunnel closes the writer and re-raises CancelledError on 3.11, 3.12 and 3.13, so it does not swallow anything.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "SEED-X1",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Fake CDP peer, Python 3.13.16 and 3.11.17, handler for cdp.network.RequestWillBeSent. If Network.enable is never answered, wait_for(conn.send(Runtime.evaluate), 1) and task.cancel() are still running 4-5s later, stuck at connection.py:654 send -> 707 _register_handlers -> 662 send (`await tx` on the second Network.enable), with task.cancelling()==1. If Network.enable answers after 2s, both calls return the result normally at 3.0s with no TimeoutError, and the peer receives Runtime.evaluate after the caller cancelled. Controls: with no handler, TimeoutError/cancel arrives at 1.00s. The same script against a scratch copy where only that line becomes `except Exception:` gives TimeoutError/cancel at 1.00s in every case. Real headless Chromium 141 with the renderer busy for 12s and a new Network/Runtime/Log handler: wait_for(tab.send(evaluate), 3) is still running at 8s and returns normally at 11.6s (control and patched copy: TimeoutError at 3.00s). Tab.wait_for_ready_state('loading', timeout=2) on an alert-blocked page with a Log handler is still running at 15s, stuck in Log.enable, and a second cancel is swallowed too. It only ended when the tab was closed (ProtocolException 'target session was detached'). Control without the handler: TimeoutError at 2.00s."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "SEED-X2",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "The location is wrong: the swallow is in Element.mouse_click (element.py:574-577), not Element.click. Real Chromium, Python 3.13: I waited until the task's cr_await chain was inside flash (mouse_click:575 -> flash:940 -> get_position:525 -> send:662) and called task.cancel(). mouse_click returned None with task.cancelled()==False and cancelling()==1. The same test on Element.click() gives task.cancelled()==True. Caller impact: `async with asyncio.timeout(0.2..0.6): while True: await el.mouse_click()` never timed out in 11 of 20 runs (still running after limit+4s, ~650-1300 clicks). In 3 of those runs a 2nd and a 3rd cancel were swallowed as well, so the task leaked until browser.stop(). The el.click() control loop raised TimeoutError on time."
                },
