              "evidence": "Commands, cwd /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/Q12-reproduce/, PATH including scratchpad/bin for chromium:\n(a) timeout 120 /home/user/zendriver/.venv/bin/python x1_fake_peer.py [delay] -> x1_out_py313.txt, x1_out_delay_py313.txt. Key lines: 'BUG-3 cancel ignored ... RESULT: STILL RUNNING 4s after start/cancel (HANG)' with chain 'connection.py:654 in send / 707 in _register_handlers / 662 in send' and 'task.cancelling() = 1'. 'BUG-5 slow Network.enable (2s), timeout 1s ... [3.003s] RESULT: returned normally ... cancel/timeout IGNORED'. Peer log: Network.enable id=1 at 0.001s, Network.enable id=3 at 1.001s (re-sent after the swallowed cancel), Runtime.evaluate at 3.003s.\n(b) Same scripts with PYTHONPATH pointing at a scratch copy whose line 709 is `except Exception:` (zendriver.__file__ printed from the copy): every BUG case gives 'raised TimeoutError'/'task cancelled' at ~1.00s (x1_out_patched_py313.txt).\n(c) Python 3.11 venv (uv, websockets 14.1) against an unmodified copy: same HANG and 'returned normally' results.\n(d) timeout 180 python x1_real_chromium.py -> x1_real_out3.txt: 'new Network handler ... 8.00s: STILL RUNNING ... later returned normally after 11.60s'. Control: '3.00s: raised TimeoutError'. Patched copy: 3.00s TimeoutError for all (x1_real_out_patched.txt).\n(e) timeout 160 python x1_ready_state.py -> x1_ready_out.txt: 'blocker=alert handler=True: wait_for_ready_state(\\'loading\\', timeout=2) 15.00s: STILL RUNNING ... (HANG); cancelling()=1' with task.print_stack showing 'wait_for=<Transaction method: Log.enable status: pending>' and chain tab.py:1351 -> 1342 _poll -> 859 evaluate -> connection.py:654 -> 707 -> 662, then 'after a 2nd cancel: done=False'. Control: '2.00s: finished with TimeoutError'.\n(f) timeout 150 python x2_real_chromium.py 20 -> x2_real_out2.txt: 'BUG Element.mouse_click() cancelled inside flash ... RESULT: returned normally (None); task.cancelled()=False cancelling()=1 -- CANCEL SWALLOWED' and 'CONTROL Element.click() ... task.cancelled() == True'. 11 of 20 'STILL RUNNING ... asyncio.timeout never fired (HANG)', 3 of them with 'after 3rd cancel: done=False'.\n(g) for py in 3.13/3.11/3.12: python -I x3_proxy.py /home/user/zendriver/zendriver/core/proxy.py -> x3_out.txt: all 'cancelled (CancelledError propagated)' plus 'upstream saw EOF', and forwarder close '0.00s; client task cancelled=True'.\nAll browsers I started were stopped (no leftover processes of mine). I removed my scratch package copies and venv. The repo is untouched (git status clean).",
              "refinements": "1) X2: the bad site is Element.mouse_click (element.py ~574-577), not Element.click. click() is fine. 2) X1 is worse than claimed. After the swallowed cancel, the handler removes the domain from enabled_domains, and send's own `await self._register_handlers()` (connection.py ~654) re-sends X.enable, so the call can hang forever even when the real command would have been answered. Every later cancel that lands in the retried enable is swallowed too. The same swallow is also reached from aopen()'s `await self._register_handlers()` (connection.py ~458). It defeats zendriver's own internal timeouts, which wrap send in wait_for: Tab.wait_for_ready_state (tab.py ~1351, hang reproduced) and Tab.get's wait_for(send(navigate)) (tab.py ~512). 3) Fix risk for the fix stage: with `except Exception`, a cancelled X.enable leaves domain_mod in enabled_domains (seen in the patched run: enabled_domains == ['zendriver.cdp.network'] after the TimeoutError). If the cancel lands before the enable is actually sent, the domain is never enabled and the handler never fires. The fix should remove the domain and re-raise on CancelledError. 4) Incidental, outside this group: (a) open_tunnel's inner-TIMEOUT path reports ProxyError('proxy closed the connection: TimeoutError()') instead of 'timed out waiting for the proxy', because TimeoutError subclasses OSError on 3.11+ (cosmetic, reproduced on 3.11/3.12/3.13). (b) When handler sets change between sends, the nested _register_handlers can crash with 'ValueError: list.remove(x): x not in list' at connection.py ~720. I hit it once by calling remove_handlers() and then adding a handler for a different domain on the same tab.",
