            "lens": "refute",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "Q12",
              "verdict": "partially-confirmed",
              "summary": "Partly confirmed. SEED-X1 is real and serious. The bare `except:` in Connection._register_handlers (line 709, around `await self.send(domain_mod.enable(), _is_update=True)` at line 707) swallows CancelledError. It then drops the domain from enabled_domains, and the next handler type in the loop, or send()'s second `_register_handlers()` call (line 654), sends Domain.enable again. So while the renderer is blocked, a single wait_for timeout or task.cancel() can never get the call out, and it hangs without limit. If the enable is only slow, the timeout is ignored and the call returns late with a normal result. I reproduced this against real headless Chromium through a documented API (expect_request + Tab.get(timeout=3) on a page with an open alert()), which hung for over 25s; without the handler the same navigation succeeds in 0.11s. SEED-X2 is real but misplaced and low-impact. The bare except is in Element.mouse_click (line 576), not Element.click: Element.click calls flash(0.25) unwrapped and raises TimeoutError correctly. In mouse_click a wait_for timeout or task.cancel() landing in the trailing flash() is turned into a normal return, after the click has already been sent. SEED-X3 is refuted: UpstreamProxy.open_tunnel's `except BaseException` closes the writer and ends with a bare `raise`, so CancelledError propagates.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "SEED-X1",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Fake CDP peer, Python 3.13 and 3.11, Network.enable and Runtime.evaluate never answered: wait_for(conn.send(evaluate), 1) is still running after 6s; task.cancel() also hangs. Stack chain: send:654 -> _register_handlers:707 -> send:662 (await tx on a second Network.enable). Controls: no handler raises TimeoutError at 1.00s; enable answered and evaluate dropped raises TimeoutError at 1.00s. Slow enable (2s) with timeout 1s returns a normal result at 3.00s; Network.enable was sent twice (t=0 and t=1). A second explicit cancel was swallowed too when the retried enable was outstanding. Real Chrome, hung renderer (verified): wait_for(tab.evaluate, 3) with a cdp.network handler is still hung after 40s; control raises TimeoutError at 3.00s. Real Chrome, open alert() (verified): wait_for_ready_state(timeout=3) with a cdp.page handler hangs for over 20s, and `async with tab.expect_request('*'): await tab.get(url, timeout=3)` hangs for over 25s stuck in aopen:458 -> _register_handlers:707 (pending tx: Network.enable). Without a handler, the same tab.get returns in 0.11s. The hang ends only when the connection closes: browser.stop() fails the pending tx with ProtocolException. This was not deliberate: the code dates from the squashed root commit 5c1a005 (nodriver origin), its comment is about tolerating errors, and no test asserts the current behaviour."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "SEED-X2",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Location correction: the bare except is in Element.mouse_click (try: await self.flash() / except: pass, line 576), not Element.click. Element.click awaits flash(0.25) unwrapped (line 432), and the real-Chrome control raised TimeoutError at 1.00s. In real Chrome, with the page making flash()'s script busy for 3s (zendriver itself not patched), wait_for(el.mouse_click(), 1) returned normally at 1.00s with no TimeoutError. With task.cancel() at 1s (stack at Element.flash:992 -> send:662), the task completed normally and the code after the call ran. The page had already received mouseup and click, so the lost signal is a cancel arriving after the real work is done. It is not a hang on its own, but a cancel during flash is silently lost and the task keeps running."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "SEED-X3",
                  "verdict": "refuted",
                  "note": "UpstreamProxy.open_tunnel's `except BaseException as e` (line 110) closes the writer, maps only OSError, IncompleteReadError and TimeoutError to ProxyError, and re-raises everything else with a bare `raise`. Against a fake upstream that accepts TCP and never replies, on Python 3.13 and 3.11, for both http and socks5: task.cancel() at 0.5s raised CancelledError at 0.50s, and wait_for(open_tunnel, 0.5) raised TimeoutError at 0.50s. Catching BaseException here is deliberate: it is for cleanup (commit d089840)."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "Scripts, all mine, in /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/Q12-refute/: fake_peer_x1.py, fake_slow_enable.py, proxy_x3.py, chrome_x1.py, chrome_x2_x1b.py, chrome_x2.py, and stackwalk.py (walks cr_await to print the full stuck chain). Key outputs, Python 3.13.16 unless noted: [control-no-handler] raised TimeoutError after 1.00s; [handler+both-dropped] still running after 6.0s, sent=['Network.enable','Network.enable'], chain send:654 -> _register_handlers:707 -> send:662; the same holds on 3.11.17 (wait_for stuck in _cancel_and_wait). Slow enable: wait_for(timeout=1) returned the result after 3.00s, sent: [(0.0,'Network.enable'),(1.0,'Network.enable'),(3.0,'Runtime.evaluate')]. Real Chrome hung renderer: Runtime.evaluate, Page.enable, Runtime.enable and Page.getFrameTree got no answer within 3s, while Target.getTargetInfo on the browser connection answered in 0.00s; [bug-hung-renderer] still running after 40s, pending tx ['Network.enable','Runtime.evaluate'], target still listed. Open alert: Runtime.evaluate and Network.enable got no answer within 3s; [control-no-handler] tab.get returned in 0.11s; [expect_request] still hung after 25.0s in Tab.get:512 -> send:632 -> aopen:458 -> _register_handlers:707 -> send:662. X2: [mouse_click wait_for] returned after 1.00s with the code after the call run; [mouse_click cancel] returned after 1.00s; [click wait_for] TimeoutError after 1.00s. X3: CancelledError or TimeoutError after 0.50s on every path. All browsers I started (pids 16523, 17113, 17541) are gone; the repo was not modified.",
              "refinements": "X1: three conditions are needed. (1) A handler exists for a domain that is not yet auto-enabled. expect_request and expect_response create this on every use, because _teardown removes the handlers and the next _register_handlers drops Network from enabled_domains, so the next send re-sends Network.enable. (2) Domain.enable is still outstanding when the timeout or cancel fires, which in practice means the renderer is blocked (open alert/confirm, JS busy loop, long synchronous work). On a responsive page Network.enable answered in 0.00s (verified), so the window is milliseconds. (3) The caller relies on a timeout or cancel; Tab.get (timeout=10) and wait_for_ready_state rely on one internally. When the renderer stays blocked, the call hangs until the connection closes, not just runs late. Websocket keepalive does not bound it: ping_timeout is 900s, and the browser process stays responsive (browser-level CDP verified; pongs assumed). It reproduces on 3.11 and 3.13 (3.10 is assumed to behave like 3.11). Getting out takes one cancel per handler type of the stuck domain plus one, because each swallowed cancel triggers a re-enable. X2: the cancel must land in the trailing flash() of mouse_click (about 2 round trips after the mouse events). The click has already been sent, so the harm is limited to a lost task.cancel() (the task keeps running) or wait_for returning instead of raising. mouse_click's docstring itself says to use click() instead. X3: no defect.",
              "chrome_claims": [
                {
                  "claim": "With a renderer stuck in while(true){}, Chrome does not answer Runtime.evaluate, Page.enable, Runtime.enable, Page.getFrameTree or Network.enable on that page session",
                  "status": "verified",
