          {
            "lens": "fixsafety",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "Q12",
              "verdict": "partially-confirmed",
              "summary": "Two of the three sites are real. In Connection._register_handlers (connection.py ~L709), a bare `except:` wraps `await self.send(domain_mod.enable(), _is_update=True)`. When a wait_for timeout or task.cancel() hits a pending Domain.enable, the CancelledError is swallowed and the domain is removed. Connection.send (L654, `await self._register_handlers()`) then sends the enable again and waits for it with no timeout left. If the enable never answers (for example a busy renderer), send() hangs forever on Python 3.11 and 3.13. SEED-X2 is real but sits in Element.mouse_click (element.py ~L574-577, `try: await self.flash() except: pass`), not Element.click, which calls flash without a try. A cancel or timeout that lands during flash is lost: mouse_click returns None, wait_for raises no TimeoutError, and the calling coroutine keeps running. SEED-X3 is refuted: UpstreamProxy.open_tunnel's `except BaseException as e` closes the writer and re-raises CancelledError.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "SEED-X1",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Fake websocket that never answers Network.enable, with a RequestWillBeSent handler, Py3.13: wait_for(conn.send(Target.setDiscoverTargets), 0.3) is still pending after 3s (task 'cancelling'). Network.enable is sent at t=0.0 and sent again at t=0.3, right when the timeout fires. Await chain: send:654 -> _register_handlers:707 -> send:662 (await tx). task.cancel() is ignored the same way. Same failures on Py3.11. Real headless Chromium: page stuck in while(true), handler for runtime.ConsoleAPICalled, wait_for(tab.send(target.get_target_info()), 2.0) is STILL RUNNING after 8s, stuck on Runtime.enable. With the prototype it raises TimeoutError at 2.00s."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "SEED-X2",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Wrong location: the bare except is in Element.mouse_click (around flash), not Element.click, which awaits flash(0.25) with no try. With a duck-typed tab whose Runtime.callFunctionOn (the flash call) never answers, task.cancel() during flash is lost: flow() runs past mouse_click and the task is not cancelled. wait_for(el.mouse_click(), 0.3) returns None, not TimeoutError, on Py3.11 and 3.13. Real Chromium (page whose styleSheets getter runs 4s of sync work after the click): repo returns None after 1.00s with no TimeoutError; prototype raises TimeoutError at 1.00s. Severity is a lost cancellation rather than a hang, because flash is the last statement."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "SEED-X3",
                  "verdict": "refuted",
                  "note": "open_tunnel's `except BaseException as e` only converts OSError/IncompleteReadError/TimeoutError and re-raises everything else. test_open_tunnel_propagates_cancellation (silent proxy, cancel during CONNECT) passes on the unmodified repo: the task ends cancelled. Unrelated cosmetic issue: on Py>=3.11 asyncio.TimeoutError is builtins.TimeoutError, a subclass of OSError, so a proxy timeout is reported as 'proxy closed the connection: TimeoutError()' and not 'timed out waiting for the proxy'. Seen on 3.11 and 3.13."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "Regression test: /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/Q12-fixsafety/tests/test_q12_cancellation.py (browser-free, fake websocket via monkeypatched websockets.connect, duck-typed Tab, asyncio.start_server proxy).\nRepo run (no PYTHONPATH): 'zendriver imported from /home/user/zendriver/zendriver/__init__.py (python 3.13.16)' -> '5 failed, 2 passed in 9.60s'. Failed: test_wait_for_timeout_during_domain_enable_is_honoured ('send() kept running after its wait_for timeout'), test_cancel_during_domain_enable_cancels_send ('send() ignored task.cancel()'), test_cancelled_domain_enable_is_retried_later, test_cancel_during_mouse_click_flash_is_not_swallowed (\"assert ['ran past the cancellation'] == []\"), test_wait_for_timeout_during_mouse_click_flash_raises (exception() is None). Passed: print, test_open_tunnel_propagates_cancellation.\nPrototype run (PYTHONPATH=.../Q12-fixsafety/pkg): 'zendriver imported from .../Q12-fixsafety/pkg/zendriver/__init__.py (python 3.13.16)' -> '7 passed in 1.05s'.\nPy3.11.17 throwaway venv: untouched copy 5 failed, 2 passed; prototype 7 passed in 1.03s.\nThe naive triage fix (bare except -> `except Exception` only) passes the two hang tests but FAILS test_cancelled_domain_enable_is_retried_later ('assert 0 == 1'). When the cancel lands before Network.enable reaches the socket (websocket.send blocked), the domain stays in enabled_domains, the enable is never sent again, and that domain's events never arrive.\nProbes: probe_x1_chain.py (repo: Network.enable sent at t=0.0 and t=0.3, still pending at 2s; prototype: one send, TimeoutError) and probe_chrome.py (real headless Chromium results above), both in the scratch dir.\nRepo core suite on the prototype with real headless Chromium (tests/core minus cloudflare/drm, copied to scratch): 107 passed.",
              "refinements": "Correct X2's location to Element.mouse_click (element.py ~L574-577); Element.click has no try around flash. X1's hang mechanism: the swallowed cancel is followed by Connection.send's own `await self._register_handlers()` (L654), which sends Domain.enable again with no timeout left. On Py3.11 wait_for blocks in _cancel_and_wait. On 3.12+ asyncio.timeout fires only once. Either way it is a permanent hang when the enable never answers, and only a late result when it eventually does. After aclose the stuck send fails with a misleading 'target session was detached'. In-library callers that rely on wait_for cancellation are hit too: Tab.get's wait_for(send(page.navigate)) and Tab.wait_for_ready_state's `while True` _poll, which would keep polling forever if its one cancel is swallowed. Connection.py L108 (Transaction.has_exception) has a bare except around a synchronous future.exception() call, not an await, so it does not swallow task cancellation and is fine. Connection.py L821 (_send_transaction) re-raises. The triage fix strategy is not enough on its own for X1: the CancelledError branch must also remove domain_mod from enabled_domains before re-raising (see fix). Separate, out of this group: open_tunnel misclassifies timeouts on Py>=3.11 because TimeoutError is an OSError subclass. Swap the isinstance order to check TimeoutError first.",
              "chrome_claims": [
                {
                  "claim": "With a renderer stuck in while(true) and a handler for an unenabled renderer domain (Runtime), wait_for(tab.send(...), 2) never returns on the repo code (still running after 8s, pending Runtime.enable)",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "probe_chrome.py against real headless Chromium via zendriver.start(headless=True), repo copy vs prototype; the prototype raises TimeoutError at 2.00s"
                },
                {
                  "claim": "wait_for(el.mouse_click(), 1.0) returns None, not TimeoutError, when the page is busy during flash",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "probe_chrome.py: page overrides Document.prototype.styleSheets with a 4s sync loop triggered by the click; repo returns None at 1.00s, prototype raises TimeoutError at 1.00s"
                },
                {
                  "claim": "The fix causes no regressions in the real-browser core suite",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "Copied tests/ to scratch and ran tests/core (except test_cloudflare and test_drm) with PYTHONPATH=prototype and ZENDRIVER_TEST_BROWSERS=headless: 107 passed"
                }
              ],
              "realism": "medium",
              "severity": "hang",
              "fix_assessment": {
                "kind": "minimal-local",
                "prototype_dir": "/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/Q12-fixsafety/pkg",
                "diff_summary": "connection.py, Connection._register_handlers: replace the bare `except:` around `await self.send(domain_mod.enable(), _is_update=True)` with two handlers. First, `except asyncio.CancelledError:`, which removes domain_mod from self.enabled_domains if present and re-raises; the enable may never have reached the browser, and a later call re-sends it, which CDP treats as idempotent. Second, `except Exception:` with the existing log and remove logic unchanged; the noqa is dropped because E722 no longer applies. element.py, Element.mouse_click: `except:  # noqa` -> `except Exception:` around `await self.flash()`. proxy.py unchanged. Full patch: /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/Q12-fixsafety/q12-fix.patch. ruff check and ruff format --check both pass.",
                "regression_test_path": "/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/Q12-fixsafety/tests/test_q12_cancellation.py",
                "fails_on_repo": "yes",
                "passes_on_prototype": "yes",
                "mypy_ok": "yes",
                "risks": "No locks added, so no lock-ordering issues. _register_handlers runs outside _open_lock, and inside _attach_session a CancelledError now leaves through `async with self._attach_lock`, which releases the lock cleanly. Behaviour changes callers will notice: CancelledError, KeyboardInterrupt, SystemExit and GeneratorExit now propagate out of send()/aopen() and mouse_click() instead of being swallowed. Remaining minor issue (not fixed, low probability): if the cancel lands in Connection.send's own `_register_handlers()` call, which runs after `self.mapper.update({tx.id: tx})`, the never-sent outer Transaction stays in mapper until aclose. aclose then calls set_exception on it, giving a 'Transaction exception was never retrieved' log. This needs aopen's _register_handlers to have failed with an Exception, or a handler added concurrently during _prepare_headless/_prepare_expert. A fuller fix would pop tx on BaseException around that call, or create tx after _register_handlers. Also, when the cancellation leaves mid-loop, the trailing cleanup of unused enabled_domains is skipped; the next _register_handlers recomputes it, so this is harmless. mypy on the prototype: 332 errors, identical to an untouched copy (all pre-existing, in cdp/), so no new errors."
              }
            }
          }
