            "lens": "refute",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "R13",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "I could not refute it. On real headless Chrome 141.0.7390.37 (Python 3.13.16, repo copy of zendriver imported), DOM.getContentQuads returns an empty list, not an error, for any element without a layout box. That covers display:none, descendants of a display:none parent, the hidden attribute, input type=hidden, content of a closed <details>, <option> inside a closed <select>, and detached nodes. Element.get_position (element.py, inside the try, `if not quads: raise Exception('could not find position ...')`) turns the empty list into a bare Exception. Its `except IndexError: return None` can never run: quads[0] is guarded, and Position() unpacking would raise ValueError, not IndexError. click() awaits the cosmetic flash(0.25) before the JS `el.click()`, so the exception escapes and the JS click is never sent. A JS click on the same element through apply() works. For elements with no tree (Tab.get_all_linked_sources, or element.create(node, tab) without a tree), click() first sets _remote_object. get_position's guard then reads self.parent, which raises RuntimeError('could not get parent since the element has no tree set'), so click() always fails on those elements. The `if not position/pos` fallbacks in mouse_click, mouse_move, mouse_drag, flash and screenshot_b64 never run for hidden elements. This is not deliberate. Upstream nodriver (its current element.py) wraps get_position in flash() with `try ... except (Exception,): return`, so click() works on hidden elements there. Upstream zendriver main has the same flash/get_position/click code as this branch, so the regression is zendriver's own. Local git history is squashed at 5c1a005, so I cannot see when the change was made. No test asserts the raising behaviour.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "A8.3",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Reproduced on real Chrome 141. `await (await tab.select('#dn')).click()` on a display:none button raises `Exception: could not find position for <button id=\"dn\" ...>`, and the onclick counter stays None. The same happens with a display:none parent, input type=file with display:none, input type=hidden, the hidden attribute, inside a closed <details>, and <option> in a closed <select>. visibility:hidden, opacity:0, a clip-based visually-hidden checkbox, a 0x0 box and display:contents all click fine, because Chrome returns quads for them. One refinement to the claim: for detached nodes Chrome 141 also returns [] (verified); I saw no 'Could not compute content quads' ProtocolException."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "B15.6",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Same root cause as A8.3, reproduced on Chrome 141. click(), mouse_click(), mouse_move(), mouse_drag((10,10)), flash(), get_position() and screenshot_b64() on a display:none element all raise a bare Exception. None of them reaches its warning-and-return branch, and screenshot_b64 does not raise the RuntimeError its docstring promises. Calling `(e)=>e.click()` through apply() on the same element does fire onclick (counter 1). tab.select() and tab.find() both return the hidden element, so normal entry points reach this path. The impact differs by method. For click() it is a real failure: the click the library would otherwise perform is lost. For mouse_*, raising is arguably better than the silent no-op the dead branches would give. For screenshot_b64 the only problem is the wrong exception type."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A8.4",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Reproduced on Chrome 141 with elements from Tab.get_all_linked_sources() (tree is None). The first and second click() calls on an <a> raise RuntimeError('could not get parent since the element has no tree set'), and the counter stays None. An <img> behaves the same. On a fresh element, the first mouse_click() works (the counter becomes 1) because the guard short-circuits on `not self._remote_object`, and the flash inside mouse_click sits in try/except. The second mouse_click(), mouse_move() and get_position() then raise RuntimeError. element.create(node, tab) built by the user, without a tree, fails the same way. Elements from select, select_all, find, find_all, xpath and .children all carry a tree, so get_all_linked_sources (tab.py) is the only built-in source of tree-less elements. Calling `await elem.update()` first makes click() work. Realism is low: this API mostly serves get_all_urls and attribute reads, and the create() docstring warns that .parent needs update()."
                }
