        "lenses": [
          {
            "lens": "reproduce",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "R14",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "Confirmed. I reproduced it with my own probes on Python 3.13.16 and the real headless Chromium. Calling print(config), str(config), repr(config) or config(), or reading config.user_data_dir, on a template Config runs mkdtemp (Config.user_data_dir getter, config.py ~L140-151, called from Config.__repr__ ~L247 and Config.__call__ ~L200). The path is stored on the template and _custom_data_dir stays False, so every deepcopy made in Browser.__init__ (browser.py ~L259) carries the same 'temporary' profile. With a printed template or a called config(), b1 starts. b2's Chromium aborts with 'Failed to create .../SingletonLock: File exists' and zd.start raises 'Failed to connect to browser'. b2's failure path then removes b1's live profile: start -> stop -> _stop:919 -> _cleanup_temporary_profile:927 rmtree. b1 keeps running and its profile directory is gone. Without the repr (control run), the two browsers get distinct dirs and both start. Separately, Browser.__init__ formats logger.debug('Session object initialized: %s' % vars(self)) (~L274) eagerly, even at WARNING level, and that materializes the instance copy's temp dir. When start() then fails before spawning Chrome (bad executable), or the connect-existing path fails, the dir is never removed. _stop returns early at `if not self.connection and not self._process: return` (~L875), before _cleanup_temporary_profile, and create() never registers the atexit cleanup. Each failed attempt leaks one empty uc_* dir.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "A3.6",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "p3_shared.py repr mode, real Chromium. Template printed, then b1 starts with udd=.../uc_syxttq0r and b2 fails with 'Failed to connect to browser'. Then 'b1 still running: True  b1 profile exists: False'. p4 rmtree spy shows the deletion comes from b2's failure path: RMTREE uc_25duiqyk via start:594 -> stop:872 -> _stop:919 -> _cleanup_temporary_profile:927. b2's stderr shows process_singleton 'SingletonLock: File exists ... Aborting now', so the Chromium here aborted rather than handing off to b1. The sub-claim that, without any failure, the first stop() deletes the other browser's profile was not exercised, because b2 always fails to start."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A9.3",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "p1_repr.py, no browser: repr, str, config() and the user_data_dir getter each change _user_data_dir from None to a new uc_* dir with custom=False, and two deepcopies then share the path. In the control run without repr, the copies are distinct. p4 shows config() alone (the docstring-recommended way to inspect args) leads to b2 failing and b1's profile being removed. Every repr of a template that is never used leaves an orphan uc_* dir (leftover list in p1). The 'crash' severity amounts to the exception raised by zd.start for b2, plus losing b1's profile."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "B16.5",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Same reproduction as A3.6 using print(config) (p3_shared.py repr): 'template _user_data_dir before: None' -> after: .../uc_syxttq0r; b2 start FAILED 'Failed to connect to browser'; b1 profile exists: False while b1 alive. Control without print: both browsers start, same udd: False. I did not test the logger.info('%s', config) variant separately, but it goes through the same __repr__."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A3.7",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "p2_leak.py with TMPDIR set to an empty scratch dir and the logger at WARNING (30). Three zd.start(browser_executable_path='/nonexistent/chrome') calls each raised FileNotFoundError and left ['uc_a_jbdoyb','uc_k_fxkixw','uc_v2rlnuak']. A held Browser instance showed config._user_data_dir already set right after __init__, from the eager debug %-format, before start() reached config(). Calling stop() after the failed start left the dir in place ('after stop(): dir exists? True') because of the _stop early return."
