          {
            "lens": "refute",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "R14",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "I tried to refute this group and could not. Its central claim holds. Config.__repr__ (repr, str, print, f-strings, logging that actually emits), Config.__call__ and a read of the Config.user_data_dir property all run mkdtemp on the template Config and leave _custom_data_dir False. After that, every deepcopy in Browser.__init__ carries the same temp path. I verified the next steps with real headless Chromium 141. The second zd.start(config) fails after 2.5 s with 'Failed to connect to browser', and its stop() runs shutil.rmtree on the profile directory the first browser is still using. The failed-start leaks are also real. A bad browser_executable_path leaks one empty uc_* directory per attempt, and so does a connect-existing start with nothing listening. Two parts of the claim are wrong or overstated. First, Browser.__init__'s eager '%s' % vars(self) debug line materialises only the browser's own deepcopy, never the template, so it causes the leaks but not the shared profile. Second, Chrome does not hand the launch over to the first browser's process: it aborts. The deleted profile also has almost no visible effect on the first browser.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "A3.6",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Reproduced with real Chromium 141, both sequentially and with asyncio.gather. After repr(config), b1 and b2 get the same uc_* directory. b2 fails with 'Failed to connect to browser', and b1's profile directory is gone while b1 is still alive. Corrections (verified): Chrome does NOT hand the launch to b1's process. b1 still had 1 tab afterwards, and b2's Chrome aborted with 'Failed to create a ProcessSingleton for your profile directory ... Aborting now to avoid profile corruption'. The variant 'without a failure, the first stop() deletes the other's profile' cannot happen with two launched browsers running at the same time, because the second launch always aborts on b1's live SingletonLock (verified). Reusing the template one browser at a time works fine."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A9.3",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "I confirmed every trigger without a browser: repr, str, an f-string, an emitted logging call, config() and reading .user_data_dir each create a uc_* directory on the template with custom=False. A lazy logger.debug('%s', config) at a disabled level does not. Reading uses_custom_data_dir and a deepcopy alone do not. With real Chromium, b2 fails because of b1's SingletonLock (verified), and the profile is gone. Note the order: b2's own failed stop() deletes it first, before b1.stop() is ever called. If the template is never used, repr alone leaves one empty orphan directory (confirmed). On 'crash' severity: the second zd.start raises, and b1 keeps working."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "B16.5",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Same mechanism, reproduced with repr. The cited intent is real: the comment in Config.__init__ says the temp dir is deferred so the config can be reused as a template, and tests/core/test_multiple_browsers_diff_userdata relies on that. No test covers repr. Git history starts at an import commit (5c1a005), so there is no history showing the behaviour is intended. One correction: the claim that Chrome's process singleton makes b2's Chrome exit is accurate, but Chrome aborts rather than forwarding to b1 (verified)."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A3.7",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Three zd.start(browser_executable_path='/nonexistent/chrome') calls leaked three uc_* directories under an isolated TMPDIR. All three were empty, and no instance was left registered. The cause is Browser.__init__'s eagerly formatted debug line (it materialises the copy's directory), plus start() raising FileNotFoundError with no cleanup. Realism is low. With no browser installed, Config() raises FileNotFoundError in find_executable before any directory exists, so only an explicit wrong path triggers this. Each leak is an empty directory."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "B16.6",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "(a) Same as A3.7, confirmed. (b) zd.start(host='127.0.0.1', port=<closed port>) raised 'Failed to connect to browser' after 2.5 s and leaked one empty uc_* directory. Browser._stop returns early because there is neither a connection nor a process, so _cleanup_temporary_profile is skipped. The directory is never used in connect-existing mode, but the successful path does clean it up on stop(). A retry loop polling for a remote or Docker Chrome would leak one empty directory per attempt. The impact is small: inodes in TMPDIR."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "Python 3.13.16, Chromium 141.0.7390.37 headless, run as root (--no-sandbox). I imported a scratch copy of the package and printed zendriver.__file__ to prove it. Browser-free (p_repr.py): every trigger set template._user_data_dir from None to a new uc_* directory with custom=False and new_dirs=1. Browser(tmpl) left template._user_data_dir None and set only the copy. After repr(template), two Browser objects had b1 dir == b2 dir == template dir. p_leak.py: '(a) bad exe: dirs leaked by 3 failed starts: 3 [[], [], []]' and '(b) connect-existing failed after 2.5s ... dirs leaked: 1 [[]]'. Real Chromium (p_shared.py): the control without repr gave two distinct directories and both started. With repr or config(), the template directory was used by b1, and b2's stderr showed 'process_singleton_posix.cc:340] Failed to create .../SingletonLock: File exists (17)' and 'Failed to create a ProcessSingleton ... Aborting now to avoid profile corruption'. Then: 'b2 start failed after 2.5s ... Failed to connect: True', 'b1 process alive: True', 'b1 profile dir exists: False', b1 tabs 1 before and after (no handoff). p_after.py, seq and gather variants: after the deletion, b1 still navigated an http page, kept localStorage and cookies across a reload, opened a new tab and created a new context. Chrome recreated the directory ('b1 profile exists at end: True'), and b1.stop() removed it. The probes are in /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/R14-refute/ (p_repr.py, p_leak.py, p_shared.py, p_after.py).",
              "refinements": "1) The Browser.__init__ debug line never touches the template. It formats vars(self) after the deepcopy, so it only feeds the failed-start leaks (A3.7/B16.6). The shared profile needs user code to touch the template: repr, str, print, an f-string, an eager '%' format, an emitted logging call, config() or reading .user_data_dir. A lazy logger.debug('%s', cfg) at a disabled level is safe. 2) Chrome does not forward the launch to the running process. Headless Chromium 141 aborts the second launch on the live SingletonLock (verified), so the visible symptom is always that the second zd.start raises after browser_connection_max_tries * timeout (2.5 s by default), with a misleading hint about root and sandbox. 3) Deleting b1's live profile is almost silent. Navigation, localStorage, cookies, new tabs and new contexts all kept working, and Chrome recreated the directory (verified). The real harm is that the second browser cannot start. 4) It only bites when two or more browsers from one template run at the same time. Sequential reuse (start, stop, start) works. 5) The leaks are empty directories, one per failed attempt. Path (a) needs an explicit nonexistent browser_executable_path. Path (b) needs host and port set with nothing listening. 6) Aside, out of scope: with a very long TMPDIR (the scratchpad path), Chromium failed to start at all in my control run. A short TMPDIR fixed it, which looks like the Unix-socket path limit for Chrome's singleton socket, not zendriver.",
              "chrome_claims": [
                {
                  "claim": "A second Chrome launched with the same --user-data-dir as a running headless Chrome aborts with 'Failed to create a ProcessSingleton ... Aborting now to avoid profile corruption' and does not open a tab in the first process",
