          {
            "lens": "fixsafety",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "R14",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "Confirmed on repo code (Python 3.13.16, real Chromium 141 plus browser-free tests). Config.__repr__ (config.py, Config.__repr__ ~l.254-255) calls getattr() on every property in the class dict. That includes user_data_dir, whose lazy getter (Config.user_data_dir ~l.140-151) runs mkdtemp, stores the path on the template and leaves _custom_data_dir False. Browser.__init__ (browser.py ~l.259) deep-copies the template, so every browser gets the same 'temporary' profile. With real Chromium the second zd.start(config) fails with 'Failed to connect to browser', and its stop() rmtree's the profile that b1 is still using. Browser.__init__'s eager `logger.debug('...%s' % vars(self))` (~l.274) runs repr on the browser's own copy, so a temp dir is created at construction even with debug logging off. start() raises FileNotFoundError at the executable check (~l.539) before anything cleans it up, so one uc_* leaks per failed call. For connect-existing, start() always calls self.config() (~l.565), which creates the dir. When the connection fails, stop() hits the early return in Browser._stop (~l.875, `if not self.connection and not self._process: return`) and skips _cleanup_temporary_profile, so that dir leaks too. The minimal local fix has two parts: (1) __repr__ reads self._user_data_dir for user_data_dir and no longer calls the getter; (2) the early-return path of _stop calls _cleanup_temporary_profile. Optionally, (3) the debug log in __init__ becomes lazy. This fixes the repr-driven sharing and both leaks. Calling config() or reading config.user_data_dir on a template still makes browsers share a profile; fixing that needs a design decision.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "A3.6",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Real Chromium, repo code: after repr(config), b1 and b2 share the same uc_ path, b2 start raises 'Failed to connect to browser', and b1's profile is gone while b1 is still alive. The prototype fixes the print/repr trigger. The config() and read-user_data_dir triggers in the claim are NOT fixed by the minimal patch (probe on the prototype: shared profile True for both). They need the design-level reset described in refinements."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A9.3",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Browser-free: _user_data_dir goes from None to a new uc_ dir after repr(), and two Browser(config) copies get an identical path. With real Chromium, the second start fails and the profile is deleted. The repr part is fixed by the prototype. The config() and read parts remain after the minimal fix (see refinements)."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "B16.5",
