                }
              ],
              "evidence": "Probes I wrote and ran on repo code (zendriver.__file__=/home/user/zendriver/zendriver/__init__.py, Python 3.13.16). Browser-free: 'after repr _user_data_dir: .../uc_2rvasaey custom: False', 'deepcopies share path: True', 'bad exe leaked: 3', 'connect-existing leaked: 1'. Real Chromium, no repr: two distinct dirs, both browsers fine. With repr: 'b1 udd /tmp/r14fs/uc_al2tl0ol', 'b2 start failed: Exception ['Failed to connect to browser']', 'b1 process alive: True b1 profile exists: False'.\n\nRegression test /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/R14-fixsafety/tests/test_r14_temp_profile.py (8 tests: 7 browser-free plus 1 real-Chromium).\nRepo run: 'zendriver under test: /home/user/zendriver/zendriver/__init__.py' -> '7 failed, 1 passed in 10.96s' (the one pass is a sanity test that a custom user_data_dir still shows in repr).\nPrototype run: 'zendriver under test: .../R14-fixsafety/pkg/zendriver/__init__.py' -> '8 passed in 1.63s'.\nRepr-only variant: '1 failed, 7 passed' (the connect-existing leak remains), which shows the _stop change is needed.\n\nThe repo tests tests/core/test_config.py, test_multiple_browsers.py and test_browser.py, run from a scratch copy against the prototype: '29 passed'.\n\nmypy (uv run --no-sync mypy --config-file pyproject.toml) on the untouched copy and on the prototype: both 'Found 332 errors in 47 files' (all in zendriver/cdp), and a diff of the path-normalised outputs is empty, so there are no new errors. The repo is unmodified (git status clean). Browsers were stopped, and the temp dirs and the /tmp/r14fs symlink were removed.",
              "refinements": "1) Which change fixes which member: the __repr__ change alone fixes A3.6/A9.3/B16.5 (print/repr/logging trigger), A3.7 and B16.6(a). The lazy debug log is then only belt-and-braces. B16.6(b) needs the _stop early-return cleanup.\n2) Residual that the minimal fix does not cover: config() (which the docstring offers as a way to inspect the args) and reading config.user_data_dir on a template still materialise one shared, non-custom temp dir. On the prototype both give 'shared profile: True'. Smallest fix, prototyped in .../R14-fixsafety/pkg2: right after the deepcopy in Browser.__init__, add `if not self.config.uses_custom_data_dir: self.config._user_data_dir = None`. It passes 8/8 of my tests and the same 29 repo tests. It changes behaviour, though: a temp dir already created on the template is orphaned (an empty uc_ leak), and a user who pre-seeded the auto temp dir would silently get a fresh one. So it is a maintainer decision, not part of the minimal patch.\n3) The browser.py line ~259 deepcopy in the group 'where' is only the propagation point. The root cause is the side-effecting getter being reached through __repr__.\n4) Optional polish: _cleanup_temporary_profile reads the lazy getter, so on the new early-return path a never-started browser creates and then deletes a uc_ dir (net zero, verified). Guarding on self.config._user_data_dir would avoid that.",
              "chrome_claims": [
                {
                  "claim": "A second Chrome launched with the same user-data-dir hands off to the first (process singleton) and exits, so zd.start raises 'Failed to connect to browser'",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "Real headless Chromium 141 under repo code after repr(config): b2 start raised 'Failed to connect to browser' while b1's pid stayed alive. The same scenario as a pytest test fails on repo and passes on the prototype."
                },
                {
                  "claim": "The failed b2's stop() deletes the profile directory b1 is still using",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "After b2's failed start, os.path.isdir(b1.config.user_data_dir) was False while b1._process.poll() was None."
                }
              ],
              "realism": "medium",
              "severity": "wrong-result",
              "fix_assessment": {
                "kind": "minimal-local",
                "prototype_dir": "/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/R14-fixsafety/pkg",
                "diff_summary": "config.py Config.__repr__: `if k == \"user_data_dir\": v = self._user_data_dir` (with a short comment), then `elif isinstance(v, property): v = getattr(self, k)`, so repr never runs mkdtemp (an unset dir prints 'user_data_dir = None'). browser.py Browser.__init__: `logger.debug(\"Session object initialized: %s\", vars(self))` (lazy; checked that it still renders the dict when DEBUG is on). browser.py Browser._stop: the early-return branch `if not self.connection and not self._process:` now runs `await self._cleanup_temporary_profile()` before `return`.",
                "regression_test_path": "/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/R14-fixsafety/tests/test_r14_temp_profile.py",
                "fails_on_repo": "yes",
                "passes_on_prototype": "yes",
                "mypy_ok": "yes",
                "risks": "No locks are involved, so there is no re-entrancy or lock-ordering concern. _cleanup_temporary_profile only awaits asyncio.sleep in its retry loop, so a cancel just stops the cleanup. Exceptions are contained: a mkdtemp or rmtree OSError inside the getter is caught by the existing try/except, so stop() of a never-started browser cannot raise (worst case about 0.75s of retries).\n\nBehaviour changes callers could notice: (a) repr(config) shows 'user_data_dir = None' instead of a freshly created path when no dir was set. (b) stop() of a never-started or failed connect-existing browser now deletes its non-custom temp dir. That dir is normally its own; it is only someone else's in the residual config()/read-sharing case, where a never-started copy's stop() could now delete a live sibling's profile (contrived: Browser(config) + stop() without start()). The pkg2 variant removes that hazard.\n\nNot fixed: config()/reading user_data_dir on a template still shares one profile, which needs the design-level reset in Browser.__init__ (see refinements)."
              }
            }
          }
        ]
      },
