          {
            "lens": "refute",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "R15",
              "verdict": "partially-confirmed",
              "summary": "Four of the five config-argument claims are real in the code, but under a refute/realism lens most of them are cosmetic. K5 is the one that matters. add_argument rejects any argument whose lowercased text contains 'lang', 'headless', 'data-dir', 'data_dir', 'no-sandbox' or 'no_sandbox'. Browser.start passes '--load-extension=<paths>' through add_argument, so an extension whose path contains one of those substrings makes Browser.create raise ValueError before Chrome is launched. Examples: a 'languagetool' folder, a 'headless-scraper' project, or a Windows user called Lang/Lange, whose %TEMP% also holds unpacked .crx files. K1 (the browser_args property differs from __call__) and K2 (user args that repeat Config-owned flags are not removed) are confirmed, but Chrome tolerates the duplicates (verified): identical boolean switches are harmless and for value switches the last one, Config's, wins. K4 (Config keeps a reference to the caller's list) is confirmed only when the caller uses Config directly and calls add_argument. Browser.__init__ deep-copies the config, so zendriver.start/Browser.create never mutate the caller's list. K3 is refuted: a duplicated --load-extension never reaches Chrome, and would be harmless if it did.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "SEED-K1",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Config.browser_args (property, config.py ~L136) returns sorted(_default_browser_args + _browser_args). With browser_args=['--disable-features=Foo'] the property shows two --disable-features entries while __call__ shows one merged entry. The property also lacks --user-data-dir, --no-sandbox and the webrtc flags, and keeps a user arg that duplicates a default. Nothing in zendriver reads it except Config.__repr__ (used by the debug log in Browser.__init__). The __init__ docstring names config() as the list passed to the browser. The property has no setter (assignment raises AttributeError). Impact is limited to display/debug output: cosmetic, low realism."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "SEED-K2",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "In Config.__call__ (~L194-227) user args are filtered only against the defaults plus --user-data-dir (and the filter is computed before extend, so duplicates within the user's own list survive too). Config's own --headless=new, --no-sandbox, --lang, --user-agent, --remote-debugging-port, --disable-webgl and webrtc flags are appended afterwards with no check. Verified duplicates: --headless=new x2, --no-sandbox x2, --disable-webgl x2, --force-webrtc-ip-handling-policy x2. Real Chromium 141 headless launched fine with --headless=new x2 and --no-sandbox x2 (verified). For duplicated value switches the last one wins (verified with --window-size and --user-agent), and Config's copy comes last, so the outcome matches Config's settings. --lang has no effect on Linux, headless or headful under Xvfb (verified). Cosmetic. Hitting the duplicate itself is common (browser_args=['--no-sandbox'] while running as root in Docker), but it does no harm."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "SEED-K3",
                  "verdict": "refuted",
                  "note": "The add_argument call in Browser.start (~L558) does mutate self.config every start, but that config is the Browser's private deepcopy (Browser.__init__ ~L258). The caller's Config is never mutated (verified), and a second Browser built from the same Config gets one --load-extension (verified). The two restart paths never launch Chrome. (1) start() after stop() leaves config.host/port and self.info set from the first run, so connect_existing=True. No process is launched and connecting to the old port fails with ConnectionRefusedError (verified). (2) start() after the process died calls create(config=self.config), which also sees host/port set, launches nothing and raises 'Failed to connect' (verified). The doubled entry exists only in internal _browser_args. Even when passed, an identical --load-extension twice loads the extension once (verified)."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "SEED-K4",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Config.__init__ (~L92) stores the caller's list itself: c._browser_args is mine. c.add_argument() therefore appends to the caller's list, and a second Config built from the same list sees the added args. With a shared BASE list, c1.add_argument('--window-size=800,600') then c2.add_argument('--window-size=1000,700') makes c1's Chrome start at 1000x700 (verified, last switch wins), which is a wrong result. Not reachable through zendriver's own entry points: zendriver.start(browser_args=mine) and Browser.create(cfg) with an extension leave the caller's list untouched (verified, thanks to the deepcopy). An empty list is replaced by a fresh []. Low realism: needs direct Config use plus add_argument plus a reused list."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "SEED-K5",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Config.add_argument (~L229-245) uses 'any(x in arg.lower() for x in [...])'. Verified rejected: --disable-features=LanguageDetectionAPI, --accept-lang=fr-FR, --enable-features=HeadlessModeX, --disk-cache-dir=/x/data_dir, and --load-extension= paths containing golang, C:\\Users\\Lang, headless-scraper. End to end with real Chromium: add_extension(.../ext/languagetool) or (.../headless-bot/ublock) makes Browser.create raise ValueError '\"--load-extension=...\" not allowed', and Chrome is never launched. The same extension loads fine when passed through constructor browser_args (verified), which is the only workaround. The blocklist dates from the initial import; the repo test asserts only that --lang=de-DE is rejected, not the substring breadth. Medium realism for extension users."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "Probes in /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/R15-refute/: probe_config.py (browser-free) and probe_browser2.py (real headless Chromium 141.0.7390.37, Python 3.13.16; zendriver.__file__ = repo copy, read-only). Key output:\n- K1: property disable-features = ['--disable-features=Foo', '--disable-features=IsolateOrigins,...'] vs __call__ ['--disable-features=IsolateOrigins,DisableLoadExtensionCommandLineSwitch,site-per-process,Foo'].\n- K2: dups {'--headless=new': 2, '--no-sandbox': 2, '--disable-webgl': 2, '--force-webrtc-ip-handling-policy': 2}; Chrome launched OK with them.\n- Last switch wins: window outer = [1000, 700] for --window-size=800,600 then 1000,700; Browser.getVersion UA = BBB for --user-agent=AAA then BBB.\n- --lang has no effect on Linux: navigator.language = en-US with --lang=fr-FR, headless and headful under xvfb-run.\n- Identical --load-extension x2: one extension target (chrome-extension://.../bg.js).\n- K3: restart after stop raised ConnectionRefusedError [Errno 111] on the old port; new chrome launched? False; internal --load-extension entries 2. Process-died restart raised 'Failed to connect'; launched? False. Caller Config mutated? False. Second Browser from the same Config: 1 --load-extension.\n- K4: c._browser_args is mine: True; caller list after add_argument = ['--mute-audio', '--window-size=800,600']; caller lists after zendriver.start / Browser.create = ['--mute-audio'] (unchanged); shared-list c1 argv ['--window-size=800,600', '--window-size=1000,700'] -> window [1000, 700].\n- K5: 'extension at .../ext/languagetool -> ValueError: \"--load-extension=...languagetool\" not allowed... | chrome launched? False'; the same extension via browser_args loads (chrome-extension://acfg.../bg.js).\nAll Chrome processes I started were stopped (none left alive); the temp profile dirs my runs created in /tmp were removed.",
              "refinements": "- K5 is the actionable member. The realistic trigger is not a user calling add_argument with odd flags but Browser.start's own --load-extension call. Case-insensitive matching means Windows profiles like C:\\Users\\Lang or Lange also block .crx extensions, which are unpacked under %TEMP%. A fix should match flag names exactly (e.g. the part before '='), not substrings, and should not route --load-extension through the user-facing validation.\n- K1 and K2 are cosmetic: Chrome tolerates the duplicates and Config's value comes last and wins. Deduping is a cleanup, not a behaviour fix.\n- K4 needs direct Config use, add_argument and a list reused across Configs or mutated later.\n- K3 should be dropped as stated. The real defect it brushes against belongs to another group: a Browser cannot be restarted after stop() or after its process dies. config.host/port (and self.info) persist from the first run, so start() takes the connect_existing path and fails with ConnectionRefusedError or 'Failed to connect' instead of relaunching.\n- Side observation, out of group: Browser.__init__ eagerly formats logger.debug('... %s' % vars(self)). That calls Config.__repr__, which reads the user_data_dir property and creates a temp profile dir. When start() then fails before launch (e.g. the K5 ValueError), the dir is never cleaned up: two empty /tmp/uc_* dirs leaked in my K5 run.",
