          {
            "lens": "fixsafety",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "R15",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "All five seeded leads reproduce on the repo code. Config.__call__ (config.py) never removes duplicate args, apart from user args that repeat the defaults. Config.browser_args returns sorted(defaults + user args) with nothing merged or removed. Config.__init__ keeps the caller's list (self._browser_args = browser_args). Config.add_argument rejects any arg that contains a blocked substring anywhere. Browser.start (browser.py) builds --load-extension through config.add_argument on every start. K5 is worse than the seed says: because Browser.start sends the extension path through add_argument, any extension whose absolute path contains 'lang', 'headless', 'data-dir' or 'no-sandbox' (case-insensitive, for example /home/lange/..., C:\\Users\\Langley\\..., or a folder named language-helper) makes zd.start() raise ValueError before Chrome is launched. I showed this with real headless Chromium. K1 to K4 are cosmetic or minor wrong-result issues: Chrome uses the last value of a repeated switch, so identical repeats change nothing at runtime.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "SEED-K1",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Config(browser_args=['--disable-features=Foo','--mute-audio','--mute-audio']).browser_args returns two --disable-features entries and two --mute-audio, while config() merges the disable-features into one. Nothing inside zendriver reads this property, so only callers who inspect it or reuse it are affected (cosmetic). It can never equal config() exactly, and making it call self() would be a mistake: the user_data_dir getter lazily creates a temp profile dir, and __repr__ calls property getters. The safe fix is to apply the same merge and duplicate removal. Separate issue, out of scope: docs/quickstart.md shows `config.browser_args = [...]`, but the property has no setter, so that line raises AttributeError."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "SEED-K2",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Browser-free: headless=True, sandbox=False, lang='de-DE', disable_webgl=True plus the same flags in browser_args gives --headless=new, --no-sandbox, --lang=de-DE, --disable-webgl and --force-webrtc-ip-handling-policy twice each. Real Chromium on the repo code: /proc/<pid>/cmdline contained --headless=new and --no-sandbox twice. The only duplicate removal is in Config.__call__, `arg not in args`, which compares user args against the defaults, user-data-dir and expert args only. Repeats among user args (for example --mute-audio twice) are kept too. No runtime effect, since Chrome uses the last value: cosmetic."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "SEED-K3",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "With util._start_process patched to capture params, two start() calls on the same Browser (host and port reset between them) gave 1 and then 2 --load-extension args. The cause is that Browser.start mutates self.config._browser_args through add_argument. The natural restart paths still hold host and port from the first start, so connect_existing=True and no new process is launched: stop()+start(), and start() after the process exited, which goes through create(config=self.config). The duplicate only reaches Chrome if the caller resets host and port, and the two copies are identical. Cosmetic, low realism. This same add_argument call is how K5 crashes start()."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "SEED-K4",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "base=['--mute-audio']; Config(browser_args=base).add_argument('--window-size=800,600') leaves base == ['--mute-audio','--window-size=800,600']. This only happens with a non-empty list, because an empty one is replaced by a new []. Browser.__init__ deep-copies the config, so the --load-extension added by Browser.start does not leak into the caller's list. Only the caller's own add_argument calls do. Minor wrong-result, for example when a base args list is shared between several Configs."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "SEED-K5",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "add_argument rejects --disable-features=LanguageDetectionAPI, --accept-lang=de-DE, --enable-features=HeadlessFoo, --load-extension=/opt/language-tools and --disk-cache-dir=/home/lange/cache with ValueError. Real Chromium: an extension at <scratch>/headless-language-ext makes zd.start() raise ValueError on the repo code. With the prototype it starts and the extension's service_worker target (chrome-extension://...) is running. This is a crash on valid input: an exception from the public start API."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "Python 3.13.16. Regression file: /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/R15-fixsafety/tests/test_r15_config_args.py (21 tests, browser-free; the start() tests patch zendriver.core.util._start_process to capture params and raise a sentinel). REPO run prints 'zendriver imported from /home/user/zendriver/zendriver/__init__.py' and ends '10 failed, 11 passed in 0.36s'. The failures cover K1, K2, K3, K4, K5 x5, and K5 through start (ValueError at config.py:241 in add_argument). PROTOTYPE run (PYTHONPATH=.../R15-fixsafety/pkg) prints 'zendriver imported from /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/R15-fixsafety/pkg/zendriver/__init__.py' and ends '21 passed in 0.25s'. The 11 that pass on the repo are guards: the import-path test, a test that the last value still wins, and 9 cases where Config-owned switches must still be rejected. The repo's own tests/core/test_config.py (copied to scratch) gives '9 passed' on both copies. Real-Chromium probes (/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/R15-fixsafety/probe/smoke_chrome.py, smoke_chrome2.py, lastwins.py): with an extension, the repo raises ValueError and the prototype gets a single --load-extension and a running service_worker. Without an extension, repo cmdline duplicates are ['--headless=new','--no-sandbox'] and prototype duplicates are []. With --window-size 800, 1000, 800, Chrome gives outerWidth 800 on both copies, which confirms Chrome uses the last value and that keep-last removal does not change the result.",
              "refinements": "K5 is the real defect: Browser.start passes the extension path through add_argument, so a 'lang' substring in a home dir or username (Lange, Langley, Slang...), or 'headless' in a folder name, breaks zd.start(). The cleanest fix for both K3 and the K5 start crash is to build --load-extension inside Config.__call__ from _extensions and stop mutating config in Browser.start. Remove duplicates keeping the LAST copy. Keeping the first copy would change results, because Chrome uses the last value of a switch: [--window-size=800, --window-size=1000, --window-size=800] would become 1000 instead of 800. Related issues outside this group's members: (a) --headless, --no-sandbox, --user-data-dir and --lang passed through browser_args at construction are never checked, so '--headless=new' with headless=False runs headless while config.headless stays False and Browser.start skips its headless-only setup; (b) the quickstart docs assign config.browser_args, which has no setter; (c) Config.__repr__ calls the user_data_dir getter and can create a temp profile dir.",
              "chrome_claims": [
                {
                  "claim": "Chrome uses the last value of a repeated switch, so identical repeats change nothing and keep-last removal leaves the outcome unchanged",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "Real headless Chromium with --window-size=800,600 / 1000,700 / 800,600: outerWidth was 800 on both the repo and the prototype; the prototype cmdline was ['--window-size=1000,700','--window-size=800,600']"
                },
                {
                  "claim": "--load-extension built in Config.__call__ still loads the extension",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "Prototype with real Chromium: an MV3 extension at a path containing 'headless-language' produced a chrome-extension:// service_worker target in Target.getTargets"
                },
                {
                  "claim": "--accept-lang is a valid Chrome switch that the substring guard wrongly blocks",
                  "status": "assumed",
                  "how": "From knowledge of Chromium switches. The browser-free test only shows that add_argument rejects it."
                }
              ],
              "realism": "medium",
              "severity": "crash",
              "fix_assessment": {
                "kind": "minimal-local",
                "prototype_dir": "/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/R15-fixsafety/pkg",
                "diff_summary": "config.py: (1) __init__: self._browser_args = list(browser_args), a copy (K4). (2) New module helper remove_duplicate_args(args) that walks args in reverse and keeps the last copy of each identical arg. __call__ now returns merge_disable_features_args(remove_duplicate_args(args)) (K2). (3) In __call__, when _extensions is set, append '--load-extension=' + ','.join(str(_) for _ in self._extensions) before --enable-unsafe-extension-debugging (K3). (4) browser_args property returns sorted(merge_disable_features_args(remove_duplicate_args(defaults + user args))) (K1). (5) add_argument checks the switch name only: name = arg.split('=', 1)[0].lstrip('-/').lower().replace('_', '-'), and rejects if name is one of ('headless', 'user-data-dir', 'no-sandbox', 'lang') (K5). browser.py: Browser.start drops its getattr(self.config, '_extensions')/add_argument('--load-extension=...') block (K3, and the K5 start crash). About 30 lines changed; ruff format and check are clean.",
                "regression_test_path": "/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/R15-fixsafety/tests/test_r15_config_args.py",
                "fails_on_repo": "yes",
                "passes_on_prototype": "yes",
                "mypy_ok": "yes",
                "risks": "All code involved is synchronous, so locks, re-entrancy and cancellation do not apply. add_argument no longer raises from start(). Behaviour changes callers could notice: (a) the caller's list is no longer aliased, so code that appended to its own list after building Config and expected the change to show up stops working (this only ever worked for non-empty lists). (b) After start(), browser.config.browser_args / _browser_args no longer contain --load-extension; it now appears only in config() output. (c) Exact-duplicate removal also applies to positional args, so a URL listed twice in browser_args now opens once; restrict removal to args starting with '-' if that matters. (d) add_argument accepts more args than before: every name the new check rejects contained one of the old blocked substrings, so nothing new is rejected. Args the old check rejected that are now accepted: anything that merely contains a blocked word, plus non-switch names such as '--data-dir'. '/'-prefixed Windows switches like /headless are still rejected. The existing test_add_argument_still_rejects_lang still passes. (e) The browser_args property output changes (merged and duplicate-free) but still differs from config() by design. mypy: 332 errors on both the untouched copy and the prototype, all in cdp/, none in core/, so no new errors."
              }
            }
