        "lenses": [
          {
            "lens": "reproduce",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "R16",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "I reproduced all four members myself, on Python 3.13.16 with websockets 14.1, importing the repo copy (zendriver/__init__.py under /home/user/zendriver). (1) When the peer closes the socket, Listener.listener_loop breaks out on ConnectionClosed but leaves connection.websocket set to a CLOSED ClientConnection. After that, Connection.closed reports False, aopen() returns without reconnecting even though the server still accepts connections, and send() raises ConnectionClosedOK. (2) aclose() called while aopen() is still inside websockets.connect is a no-op. The socket then opens and a listener task starts running after the 'close'. (3) send() cancelled by wait_for leaves a cancelled Transaction in conn.mapper. 200 timeouts left 200 entries, which only aclose cleared. (4) Connection.wait() hangs forever at connection.py Connection.wait:579 `await self.listener.idle.wait()` when the listener stops while the page is busy. This happens with the real headless Chromium after Target.closeTarget, after tab.close(), with `await tab`, and with Tab.get(url) (stuck at tab.py Tab.get:513). It also happens with a fake peer, both on listener.cancel() and on a peer-side websocket close. The same scenarios without the session going away return in about 0.1s.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "SEED-C1",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "probe_c1.py (fake peer that closes with 1001 and keeps accepting). Output: 'listener running after peer close: False', 'conn.websocket is None: False', 'conn.closed: False', 'websocket state: 3' (CLOSED). '2nd send raised: ConnectionClosedOK received 1001 (going away)...', and 'server connections after 2nd send: 1 handshakes: 1'. 'explicit aopen() returned; websocket state: 3 server connections: 1', so it never reconnects. The cause is that Connection.aopen only connects when `self.websocket is None`, and the ConnectionClosed branch of Listener.listener_loop never resets it. This is pre-existing: the same code exists at e81b66c^."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "SEED-C2",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "probe_c2.py (fake peer delays the handshake 0.5s via process_request). aopen() runs as a task; aclose() is called at 0.2s. Output: 'aclose() returned; websocket is None: True closed: True', then once aopen finishes: 'closed: False websocket state: 1 listener running: True server-side open connections: 1'. Only a second aclose() brings the server-side count to 0. Connection.aclose only acts when `self.websocket is not None` and does not take or observe _open_lock."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "SEED-C3",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "probe_c3.py (fake peer never answers Runtime.evaluate). 200 calls of asyncio.wait_for(conn.send(cdp.runtime.evaluate(...)), 0.01) gave 'timeouts: 200/200; mapper size 1s later: 200; states: {'cancelled': 200}'. A later normal send left 'mapper after another normal send: 200', and only 'mapper after aclose: 0'. Connection.send adds the tx to mapper and does `return await tx` with no cleanup on cancellation. _send_transaction pops the tx only if websocket.send itself raises."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "B14.5",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Real Chromium (probe_wait_chromium.py): a page logs to the console every 30ms with a ConsoleAPICalled handler attached, and tab.wait() runs as a task. In the control case, clearInterval makes the waiter return 'after 0.10s'. With closeTarget: 'waiter done=False after 8.00s; session_id=None listener running=False', and the stack is 'connection.py, line 579, in wait: await self.listener.idle.wait()'. `await tab` and tab.close() from the same task behave the same way. Tab.get(busy data: URL) followed by closeTarget is stuck at 'tab.py, line 513, in get: await self.wait()' after 8s (probe_tabget_chromium.py). Fake peer (probe_wait_fake.py): the control returns, while the 'cancel' and 'peer-close' variants hang with 'old listener running=False idle=False' at the same line. Every hang was ended by my outer timeout and task.cancel()."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "Commands, all run from /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/R16-reproduce/ with PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 and the chromium dir on PATH:\n- `timeout 120 /home/user/zendriver/.venv/bin/python probe_c1.py`\n- `timeout 120 ... probe_c2.py`\n- `timeout 120 ... probe_c3.py`\n- `timeout 120 ... probe_wait_fake.py`\n- `timeout 150 ... probe_wait_chromium.py`\n- `timeout 100 ... probe_tabget_chromium.py`\n\nThe fake CDP peer is fake_cdp.py in the same dir: a websockets.asyncio.server with per-method drop, handshake delay, a 30ms Page.domContentEventFired flood, and server-side close.\n\nKey hang stack: `Stack for <Task pending name='Task-35' coro=<Connection.wait() running at /home/user/zendriver/zendriver/core/connection.py:579> ...> File \".../connection.py\", line 579, in wait: await self.listener.idle.wait()`.\n\nCleanup: every browser was stopped through browser.stop() in a finally block, the repo git status is clean, and I created no temp dirs outside the scratch dir.",
              "refinements": "- B14.5: no session detach is needed. A plain browser-websocket close also hangs wait(), because the ConnectionClosed branch breaks without setting idle. Even after a reattach, the waiter stays stuck on the OLD listener's Event, since a new Listener gets a new Event. Passing a number to wait(t) is not affected because asyncio.wait_for bounds it (I only reasoned this, I did not run it).\n- C1: in practice the main visible effects are (a) Connection.closed staying False after the peer closes and (b) send() raising a websockets ConnectionClosed* exception instead of ProtocolException. Browser._stop then tries browser.close on the dead socket and logs a warning. Automatic reconnect would rarely succeed against real Chrome, because the browser websocket only closes when Chrome exits.\n- C2: in Browser._stop, an in-progress connect makes `connection.closed` read True, so aclose is skipped entirely and the socket and listener task survive the stop until Chrome dies.\n- C3: the leak is bounded by the number of commands that never get a reply, such as an awaitPromise on a promise that never resolves. A late reply pops the entry. _send_oneshot has the same shape, but I did not test it.",
              "chrome_claims": [
                {
                  "claim": "tab.wait(), `await tab` and Tab.get(url) hang forever when the tab is closed (Target.closeTarget or tab.close()) while the page is busy",
