          {
            "lens": "refute",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "R16",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "I could not refute any of the four members; each one reproduces. Only B14.5 is a hang. Connection.wait() awaits the old listener's idle Event (Connection.wait, `await self.listener.idle.wait()`, connection.py:579) with no timeout and no check that the listener is still running. If the session's Listener is cancelled while idle is clear, that Event is never set, so `await tab`, tab.wait() and Tab.get(url) on the same tab (tab.py:513) hang forever. This happens when the tab is closed, on browser.stop() from another task, or when Chrome is killed. The three seeds are real but minor and unlikely to be hit. SEED-C1: after ConnectionClosed every later send fails fast with ConnectionClosedError and never reconnects; nothing hangs. SEED-C2: the race is only reachable by calling Connection.aclose() directly while a connect is in progress. SEED-C3: the leak is small and temporary, about 1.3 KB per entry, and Chrome's late reply clears it, for example on navigation.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "SEED-C1",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Fake peer closes with 1011: listener stops, conn.websocket stays set, conn.closed reports False, three sends raise ConnectionClosedError right away, and the server sees only 1 connection. After a deliberate aclose(), the next send does reconnect (2 connections). Real Chromium with MAX_SIZE patched to 200000 and a 300 KB evaluate result (client sends 1009): Chrome stays alive and accepts a fresh Connection, but browser.connection and tab.evaluate fail with ConnectionClosedError for good. Setting websocket=None by hand restores both. Not a hang, and Browser.stop() still works. It only matters when Chrome outlives the socket: a message over 256 MiB, the 900 s ping timeout, or a dropped network link to a remote browser. Realism: low."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "SEED-C2",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "With a 0.3 s delayed connect, aclose() called during the connect returns at once (websocket is None), the in-flight send then succeeds, and the socket and listener stay open until a second aclose(). Realism is very low. Browser._stop skips connection.aclose() when closed is True (websocket is None while connecting), and for a local browser the process kill closes the late socket. A tab's aclose() racing _attach_session is cleaned up by the later browser-connection aclose, which detaches every session. Reaching it needs a user calling the documented internal aclose() at the same moment, and the result looks like the existing design where a send after aclose reconnects."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "SEED-C3",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Fake peer that never answers Runtime.evaluate: 5 sends timed out by wait_for leave 5 cancelled Transactions in the mapper, and aclose() clears them. Real Chromium: 200 timed-out never-settling evaluates leave 200 entries, about 1282 bytes each (tracemalloc). Chrome answers all of them on navigate or reload, and the mapper drops to 0 (verified). No functional effect: the listener and _fail_pending_transactions skip transactions that are already done. Small, bounded leak; realism low."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "B14.5",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Real headless Chromium 141, Python 3.13. Control (busy page that goes idle after 1 s): wait() returns after 1.11 s. Busy page (console.log every 40 ms, Runtime enabled) closed by Target.closeTarget, tab.close(), browser.stop() from another task, or killing the Chrome process: the waiter is still pending after more than 8 s, with its stack stuck at Connection.wait connection.py:579 and listener.running=False. Tab.get(url) is stuck at tab.py:513 the same way. A quiet page with no domains enabled also hangs if window.close() lands 20-80 ms after the call (2/2 at each delay). A 0 ms close raises ProtocolException, and a close at 100 ms or later returns normally. An immediate click-to-window.close() raised 0/6 times and never hung. Realism: medium."
