            "lens": "fixsafety",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "R16",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "All four members reproduce on the repo code (branch ccr-9a8a26e1-b0l7bh, HEAD 064148f), both against a fake CDP peer and, for B14.5, against real headless Chromium. (1) B14.5: Connection.wait() has no done check on the listener. When a busy listener is cancelled (session detached: Target.closeTarget, or a browser crash/websocket close) or breaks on ConnectionClosed, its idle Event is never set, so `await tab` / tab.wait() / Tab.get() hang forever. (2) SEED-C1: on ConnectionClosed, Listener.listener_loop breaks but leaves connection.websocket set. As a result `closed` stays False, aopen() never reconnects, and every later send raises ConnectionClosedOK on the dead socket. (3) SEED-C2: aclose() while aopen() is still inside websockets.connect is a no-op, so the socket and a running listener exist after the close. The same happens for a tab whose _attach_session is in progress. (4) SEED-C3: a send() cancelled by wait_for leaves its Transaction in conn.mapper until a reply or close. Each problem has a small local fix in connection.py. Each fix is independent: applying only one fix makes only its own tests pass.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "SEED-C1",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Fake peer closes the socket after its first reply. On the repo, conn.closed stays False and 3 more sends each raise 'ConnectionClosedOK received 1000 (OK)'. The peer only ever sees 1 connection, so there is no reconnect. Root cause: the ConnectionClosed branch of Listener.listener_loop (around line 939) detaches sessions and fails pending transactions but never resets connection.websocket, enabled_domains or manually_enabled_domains, unlike aclose(). With real Chromium killed by SIGKILL, browser.connection.closed is still False, so Browser._stop sends browser.close on the dead socket and logs 'Could not send the close command...'. No hang; the impact is stale state and no reconnect."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "SEED-C2",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Fake peer delays the handshake by 0.5s (async process_request). aclose() is called 0.15s into aopen() and returns at once because websocket is still None. Afterwards closed=False, the listener is running and the server holds 1 open connection, which is a leak. Root cause: aopen holds _open_lock during the connect, but the non-session branch of aclose() does not take it. The flat-session analog is also confirmed: tab.aclose() during an in-progress _attach_session is a no-op, session_id becomes 'S1' after the close and no Target.detachFromTarget is sent."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "SEED-C3",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "The fake peer drops Runtime.evaluate. After wait_for(conn.send(cdp.runtime.evaluate('1')), 0.3) raises TimeoutError, conn.mapper still holds {1: <Transaction Runtime.evaluate status: finished>}. The tx is cancelled but only popped when a reply arrives or the connection closes. This is transient retention in the normal case, because Chrome usually replies later. It grows without bound only for commands that never get a reply, such as awaitPromise on a promise that never settles. Low realism leak."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "B14.5",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Fake peer, both paths, with an event flood every 20ms: (a) the server closes the websocket, (b) the server sends Target.detachedFromTarget for the tab's session. In both, wait() is still pending 3s later, and the stack sits at Connection.wait connection.py:579 `await self.listener.idle.wait()`. Real Chromium (console.log every 30ms plus a ConsoleAPICalled handler): tab.wait() is still pending 4s after Target.closeTarget, and also 4s after a SIGKILL of the browser process this probe started. Note that update_target's reply itself clears idle, so even a quiet page has a window of at least 0.1s after the reply."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "Regression test: /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/R16-fixsafety/test_r16_connection_close_state.py (6 browser-free tests using an in-process websockets fake CDP peer; run from the scratch dir with --rootdir <scratch> -p no:cacheprovider -o asyncio_mode=auto -o asyncio_default_test_loop_scope=function).\n\nRepo, Python 3.13 (no PYTHONPATH): 'zendriver imported from /home/user/zendriver/zendriver/__init__.py' then FAILED for all 6: test_peer_close_forgets_websocket_and_reconnects, test_aclose_during_connect_leaves_connection_closed, test_cancelled_send_does_not_leave_transaction_in_mapper, test_wait_returns_when_websocket_closes_while_busy, test_wait_returns_when_session_is_detached_while_busy, test_tab_aclose_during_attach_leaves_session_detached -> '6 failed in 8.65s'. The B14.5 tests print 'Stack for <Task pending ... coro=<Connection.wait() running at /home/user/zendriver/zendriver/core/connection.py:579>' with the line 'await self.listener.idle.wait()'.\n\nPrototype (PYTHONPATH=<scratch>/pkg): 'zendriver imported from /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/R16-fixsafety/pkg/zendriver/__init__.py' -> '6 passed in 2.60s'.\n\nPython 3.11.17 venv (websockets 14.1): untouched copy '6 failed in 8.81s', prototype '6 passed in 2.72s'.\n\nEach single fix applied alone makes only its own test(s) pass: C1 alone gives 4 failed/1 passed, C2 alone 4/1, C3 alone 4/1, B14.5 alone 3 failed/2 passed.\n\nReal Chromium probes (probe_chrome_wait.py, probe_chrome_kill.py). Repo: 'tab.wait() still pending 4s after Target.closeTarget -> HANG' and 'still pending 4s after the browser died -> HANG | connection.closed = False'. Prototype: 'tab.wait() finished after close: None' and 'finished after the browser died: None | connection.closed = True'.\n\nExisting suites against the prototype with real headless Chromium: tests/core/test_connection.py, test_tab.py, test_browser.py, test_multiple_browsers.py, test_frames.py, test_headless_user_agent.py and test_proxy.py, run with -m 'not external': '84 passed, 4 deselected'. On the repo, the first five files give '68 passed'.\n\nmypy: `uv run mypy --config-file pyproject.toml <copy>/zendriver` returns the same 332 errors (all in cdp/, path-related) on an untouched copy and on the prototype. Sorted, path-normalised diff is empty, and connection.py has 0 errors in both.\n\nAll browsers I started were stopped (no chromium processes left). The temporary py3.11 venv and the test and orig copies were removed. The repo is untouched (git status clean).",
              "refinements": "1. SEED-C2 also applies to flat sessions (tabs): aclose() racing _attach_session. The fix must cover both locks, not only _open_lock.\n2. B14.5 is reachable not only via tab close but via any browser crash during `await tab` / Tab.get(): the ConnectionClosed branch detaches all sessions, which cancels their listeners. On repo code this hangs forever, as shown with SIGKILL on real Chromium.\n3. SEED-C1's most visible effect is the stale `closed` property, more than the missing reconnect. After a browser crash, Browser._stop tries to send browser.close on the dead socket.\n4. SEED-C3 is transient retention, low severity. `_send_oneshot` has the same no-pop-on-cancel pattern. The tx is also put in mapper before `_register_handlers`, whose bare `except:` appears to swallow CancelledError. That is a separate issue that I did not verify here.\n5. A pre-existing issue on Python 3.10/3.11 only, also on repo code: if a session is detached in the loop iteration where its listener's wait_for inner task has been scheduled but not started, the listener dies with ValueError('no websocket connection'), logged as 'Task exception was never retrieved'. It reproduced 2 times in a 12-iteration sweep, on both the untouched copy and the prototype. The prototype's tab-aclose lock makes the attach-then-close probe land in that window on 3.11, but this is cosmetic and not a regression.",
              "chrome_claims": [
                {
                  "claim": "On repo code, tab.wait() on a busy tab (console.log every 30ms with a ConsoleAPICalled handler) hangs at Connection.wait connection.py:579 after Target.closeTarget is sent for that tab. On the prototype it returns None.",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "probe_chrome_wait.py on real headless Chromium. Repo output: 'tab.wait() still pending 4s after Target.closeTarget -> HANG' plus the task stack. Prototype: 'tab.wait() finished after close: None'."
                },
                {
                  "claim": "On repo code, tab.wait() hangs forever when the browser process dies, and browser.connection.closed stays False. On the prototype, wait() returns and closed is True.",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "probe_chrome_kill.py: SIGKILL sent to browser._process.pid, the Chromium that this script started. Repo: 'still pending 4s after the browser died -> HANG | connection.closed = False', followed by Browser.stop's 'Could not send the close command...' warning. Prototype: 'finished after the browser died: None | connection.closed = True'."
                },
                {
                  "claim": "The prototype does not regress the existing browser test suites.",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "Copied tests/ into the scratch dir and ran with PYTHONPATH=<scratch>/pkg and ZENDRIVER_TEST_BROWSERS=headless: 84 passed, 4 deselected (external)."
                }
              ],
              "realism": "medium",
              "severity": "hang",
              "fix_assessment": {
                "kind": "minimal-local",
                "prototype_dir": "/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/R16-fixsafety/pkg",
                "diff_summary": "zendriver/core/connection.py only (full diff in /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/R16-fixsafety/prototype.diff):\n(B14.5) Listener.run: add `self.task.add_done_callback(lambda _: self.idle.set())`. A stopped listener counts as idle, so every exit path wakes wait(): break on cancel, ConnectionClosed, an exception, or a cancel before the first step. A finally inside listener_loop would miss the cancel-before-start case.\n(SEED-C1) Listener.listener_loop, ConnectionClosed branch: also set `self.connection.websocket = None` and clear enabled_domains and manually_enabled_domains, mirroring aclose(). After that, `closed` is True and the next aopen() reconnects and re-registers handlers.\n(SEED-C2) Connection.aclose: wrap the session branch in `async with self._attach_lock:` and the websocket branch in `async with self._open_lock:` (with `if self.websocket is None: return` inside). An in-progress attach or connect then finishes first and is closed or detached by this call, instead of opening after it.\n(SEED-C3) Connection.send: add `finally: self.mapper.pop(tx.id, None)` to the existing try around `return await tx`.",
                "regression_test_path": "/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/R16-fixsafety/test_r16_connection_close_state.py",
                "fails_on_repo": "yes",
                "passes_on_prototype": "yes",
                "mypy_ok": "yes",
                "risks": "Locks. asyncio.Lock is not reentrant, but aclose() is never called while the same task holds _open_lock or _attach_lock: aopen's except path only calls listener.cancel(), and __aexit__ and Browser._stop hold no locks. Lock order in tab.aclose is tab._attach_lock then browser._open_lock (via browser_connection.send -> aopen). That is the same order _attach_session uses, and the browser's aclose takes only its own _open_lock and calls the synchronous _detach_session(). So there is no inversion. A stress probe (5 concurrent senders plus 10 aclose cycles, delayed handshake) finished without a deadlock, with mapper empty and 0 server connections left.\n\nCancel paths. If aclose is cancelled while waiting for the lock, nothing changes and the lock is released (verified). The prototype raises more 'connection was closed' ProtocolExceptions in that stress run (50 vs 25), because connects that complete during a close are now closed too.\n\nBehaviour changes callers would notice:\n(a) aclose() can now block until an in-flight websockets.connect finishes (open_timeout defaults to 10s). tab.aclose() can now block until an in-flight Target.attachToTarget round trip finishes.\n(b) After a peer close, the next send tries to reconnect to the same URL. If Chrome is dead, callers get OSError/ConnectionRefusedError from websockets.connect instead of websockets ConnectionClosed. Browser._stop now skips browser.close and aclose because closed is True, which removes a spurious warning. A reconnect does not restore Target.setDiscoverTargets autodiscovery; that is a pre-existing limitation.\n(c) wait() / `await tab` now returns None when the listener stops, instead of hanging. A caller whose tab was closed carries on and gets an error on its next command. Raising ProtocolException('target session was detached') would be the alternative design.\n(d) A dead Listener's repr now shows [idle].\n\nA theoretical stale-listener clobber of a new websocket in the C1 branch cannot happen with C2's lock in place, because a reconnect cannot start before aclose finishes and a cancelled listener gets CancelledError. Not covered by these fixes: _send_oneshot's identical cancel leak, and the pre-existing 3.10/3.11 ValueError listener-death race on detach (cosmetic, reproduces on repo code)."
              }
            }
          }
        ]
      },
