          {
            "lens": "refute",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "R18",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "I tried to refute both members and could not. Both reproduce against real headless Chromium 141 on Python 3.13.16 and 3.11.17. B14.2: when the first sends on a fresh tab run concurrently in expert mode, the attachShadow override is registered twice. After the next navigation, every element.attachShadow() call throws 'RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded'. B14.6: if Tab.aclose() runs while the tab's first send is inside _prepare_headless, _prep_headless_done is left True. After reattach the override is skipped and navigator.userAgent contains 'HeadlessChrome'. A Listener is also started on the detached connection and dies with an unretrieved ValueError. Nothing upstream serialises these calls, and git history shows the check-then-await is not deliberate. Realism is the weak point: B14.2 needs the opt-in expert=True, and B14.6's UA leak needs the user to call the internal aclose() and then reuse the tab.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "B14.2",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Own probe: real Chromium, expert=True, headless, local HTTP page that calls attachShadow({mode:'closed'}). The probe counts frames on the shared websocket. With browser.get(url, new_tab=True) followed by asyncio.gather(tab.evaluate('1'), tab.evaluate('2')), addScriptToEvaluateOnNewDocument went out 2 times and the title after reload was 'ERR:RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded'. tab.set_local_storage({'a':'1','b':'2'}) gave the same result: 2 sends and a RangeError. The sequential control sent it once and the title was 'OK'. The same results came out on Python 3.11.17, so this does not depend on the Python version. Cause: the second task is parked on _attach_lock in Connection._attach_session. It wakes while the first task is still awaiting its oneshot reply in Connection._prepare_expert, before setattr(self, '_prep_expert_done', True). The double Network.setUserAgentOverride really is harmless: the UA had no 'Headless'."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "B14.6",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Own probe: headless, browser.get('about:blank', new_tab=True), then gather(tab.evaluate('1'), closer), where closer waits for tab.session_id and then awaits tab.aclose(). Result in 3 of 3 runs on 3.13 and 1 of 1 on 3.11: the first send raised ProtocolException 'target session was detached', _prep_headless_done was True after the detach, and UA after reattach plus reload contained 'HeadlessChrome'. Exactly one Listener was created while websocket was None, followed by 'Task exception was never retrieved ValueError(no websocket connection)'. A stale, never-done Runtime.evaluate Transaction (id 1) was left in tab.mapper. I did not see the 'Transaction exception was never retrieved' log itself in my runs. The sequential control (evaluate, then aclose, then reattach) is fine because _detach_session pops the flag. The _prepare_expert half of the claim is the same pattern; I checked it by reading only, not by running it."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "Scripts: /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/R18-refute/expert_probe.py and /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/R18-refute/headless_probe.py (each prints zendriver.__file__ and the Python version). expert_probe output on 3.13.16: seq addScript=1 title='OK'; gather addScript=2 title='ERR:RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded'; localstorage addScript=2 same ERR; main_tab_gather (tabs[0] after tab.get) addScript=1 title='OK'. Same gather/localstorage results on 3.11.17 from a scratch package copy. headless_probe on 3.13.16: sequential flag=None, UA has Headless=False, zombies=0; race flag=True, UA has Headless=True, zombies=1, unhandled ValueError=1, stale tx [(1,'Runtime.evaluate',False)]. Chrome scope check: an override set to 'CustomUA/1.0' on session A was gone after A detached, and the new session showed Headless=True. Close via the browser's Target.closeTarget issued after session_id was set: flag=None, zombies=0 in 3 of 3 runs. Close issued right away, before attach finished: flag=True, zombies=1 in 4 of 4 runs. Code read: Connection.send calls aopen, then _prepare_expert/_prepare_headless (flag read before any await, set after them), then `if not self.listener or not self.listener.running: self.listener = Listener(self)`, then mapper.update, then `raise ProtocolException('target session was detached')`. _send_oneshot swallows ProtocolException. _detach_session pops both flags and then fails pending transactions. git log -S '_prep_expert_done' shows 5c1a005 (fix verify_cf, #176) and e81b66c (flat sessions, #280); neither mentions concurrency, and no test asserts single injection. The repo was left untouched (git status clean).",
              "refinements": "B14.2: needs expert=True, which is off by default (Config expert=AUTO). It also needs two or more concurrent sends as the first traffic on a tab that has not been sent to yet: tabs from browser.get(new_tab=True) or create_context, or popups found through targets. browser.tabs[0] driven by browser.get(url) or tab.get(url) is safe, because Page.navigate runs the prepare step first, one call at a time (verified). The only internal trigger is Tab.set_local_storage with 2 or more items, which uses asyncio.gather. 'Open a tab, seed localStorage, reload' is a natural pattern, and it hits exactly this. The damage only shows from the next document on (reload or navigate), not on the current one. It then persists for the life of the session. B14.6: the UA leak needs a local detach in the window between sending the override and getting its reply. In practice that means a user calling Tab.aclose(), whose docstring says 'should not be called manually by users', at the same time as the tab's first send, and then reusing the same Tab. zendriver itself calls aclose() only from Browser.stop, where the browser is going away anyway. A detach from Chrome's side (target closed) hits the window only when the close lands around attach time. That happened 4 of 4 times with an early close and 0 of 3 with a late one. The target is then destroyed, so the UA leak does not matter, and what remains is a cosmetic 'Task exception was never retrieved' log plus one leaked Transaction. Unrelated to R18 and outside this group: on Python 3.11, browser.stop() in the expert probe also logged 'Task exception was never retrieved' from Listener.listener_loop, a ValueError raised while handling a CancelledError.",
              "chrome_claims": [
                {
                  "claim": "Chrome does not dedupe identical Page.addScriptToEvaluateOnNewDocument sources registered twice on one session; both copies run, and attachShadow recurses into a RangeError",
