          {
            "lens": "fixsafety",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "R18",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "Both members reproduce, browser-free with a fake flat-session peer and on real headless Chromium 141. (1) _prepare_expert and _prepare_headless check their done flag and then await, with no serialisation. Concurrent first sends on a fresh expert tab register the attachShadow wrapper more than once. The second copy wraps the first, so attachShadow calls itself and throws RangeError. (2) A session detached while the first send is preparing it keeps a stale done flag, so the reattached session never gets its UA override or expert script and HeadlessChrome leaks. The same send also starts a Listener on the detached connection, which dies with ValueError('no websocket connection'), and it leaves its Transaction in mapper. A minimal local fix works and holds up under the fix-safety checks: an asyncio.Lock around the prepare calls in send(), the done flag set only if the session is still the same and still attached, an early ProtocolException in send() when the session went away during prepare, and an idempotent expert script. The idempotent script is needed because the lock alone does not cover a first send that is cancelled mid-prepare.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "B14.2",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Fake peer, 3x gather(tab.send(Runtime.evaluate)) as the first sends: the repo sends Page.addScriptToEvaluateOnNewDocument 3 times on S1 and the prototype sends it once. The headless variant sends Network.setUserAgentOverride 3 times; that is harmless. Real Chromium, Python 3.13: expert=True, browser.get(url,new_tab=True), then navigate and test attachShadow({mode:'closed'}). On the repo, sequential sends give 'OK open=true'; gather(evaluate,evaluate) and set_local_storage({'a','b'}) both give 'ERR:RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded'. On the prototype all three give 'OK open=true'."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "B14.6",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Fake peer: the browser emits Target.detachedFromTarget instead of replying to the session's Network.setUserAgentOverride (headless case) or Page.enable (expert case). On the repo the first send raises ProtocolException('target session was detached'), the flag stays True, and the reattached session S2 gets 0 UA overrides or expert scripts. In both cases 1 Listener is created on the detached connection and dies with ValueError('no websocket connection'), and 1 Transaction is left in tab.mapper. The prototype re-prepares S2 once, creates no such Listener and leaves mapper empty. Real Chromium headless, tab.aclose() racing the tab's first evaluate: on the repo the flag stayed True in 7/7 runs and navigator.userAgent contained HeadlessChrome after reattach in 4/7, with 'Transaction exception was never retrieved' and an unretrieved Listener ValueError. On the prototype the flag was None in 7/7 runs, with no leak, 0 detached listeners and an empty mapper."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "My own scratch tests, not the finder scripts: /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/R18-fixsafety/tests/test_r18_prepare.py. It patches websockets.connect with a fake browser socket that hands out flat sessions S1, S2 and so on, and the owner is a SimpleNamespace with config.expert/headless and _headless_user_agent. There are 6 tests: 2 for concurrent first sends (expert, headless), 2 for 'prepared after reattach', and 2 for 'leaves nothing behind' (no Listener on the detached session, empty mapper).\nRepo run (no PYTHONPATH; prints 'zendriver imported from /home/user/zendriver/zendriver/__init__.py'): '6 failed in 0.38s'. The failures are 'assert 3 == 1' on calls_of(addScript/UA override,'S1'), 'assert 0 == 1' on calls_of(...,'S2'), and a Listener plus a leftover Transaction ('listener on detached session died with ValueError('no websocket connection')').\nPrototype run (PYTHONPATH=<dir>/pkg; prints '.../R18-fixsafety/pkg/zendriver/__init__.py'): '6 passed in 0.34s'.\nThe same results on Python 3.11 in a throwaway venv: pristine copy '6 failed in 0.53s', prototype '6 passed in 0.47s'.\nReal Chromium probes are in .../R18-fixsafety/probe/: chrome_expert.py (seq, gather, local_storage), chrome_headless_detach.py, chrome_cancel.py and chrome_reattach.py. Results are in the member notes.\nThe repo's tests/core, run from a scratch copy against the prototype (headless, excluding external, cloudflare, drm and proxy), gave '95 passed, 14 deselected'.",
              "refinements": "1) The lock alone is not enough for B14.2. If the first send is cancelled (wait_for timeout or task.cancel()) after its addScriptToEvaluateOnNewDocument has reached Chrome, the flag stays unset and the next send registers a second copy. On the fake peer this gives 2 registrations on S1 on both the repo and the lock-only prototype. On real Chromium the lock-only prototype gave RangeError 3/3. Making the injected script idempotent fixes it: wrap the body in `if (!Element.prototype._attachShadow) {...}`. That guard alone also fixes the gather and set_local_storage cases on real Chromium; the lock then only dedups the redundant UA override and Page.enable commands. An in-progress task awaited through asyncio.shield would be the alternative to the JS guard.\n2) I confirmed that Chrome drops session-scoped addScriptToEvaluateOnNewDocument scripts on detach. On the repo with no guard, aclose, reattach, re-register and reload gave 'OK open=true'. So re-preparing after reattach is correct and is not itself a double registration.\n3) A nearby defect is left unfixed. If the detach hits the first expert oneshot, the second _send_oneshot(page.enable()) raises ValueError('no websocket connection') out of send() instead of ProtocolException. This happens on both repo and prototype; the flag is correctly not set. Optional one-line follow-up: make _send_oneshot return None when websocket is None, so that the new check in send() raises ProtocolException.\n4) A different path is also out of scope. A detach during _register_handlers, after tx is already in mapper, makes _detach_session fail the tx and send() raise without awaiting it, which gives 'Transaction exception was never retrieved'.",
              "chrome_claims": [
                {
                  "claim": "Two registrations of the expert attachShadow script make element.attachShadow() throw RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded on the next document",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "Real headless Chromium 141, Python 3.13: gather of two evaluates and set_local_storage with two items, then navigate and test attachShadow; repo ERR:RangeError, prototype OK open=true"
                },
                {
                  "claim": "Chrome removes a flat session's addScriptToEvaluateOnNewDocument scripts when the session detaches, so re-preparing the reattached session does not double-register",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "chrome_reattach.py on the repo code (no JS guard): prepare S1, tab.aclose(), reattach via tab.get (prepares S2), reload, attachShadow -> OK open=true"
                },
                {
                  "claim": "A session detached during the first send's prepare leaves HeadlessChrome in navigator.userAgent after reattach",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "chrome_headless_detach.py with tab.aclose() racing the first evaluate: repo stale flag 7/7, HeadlessChrome leaked 4/7; prototype 0/7"
                },
                {
                  "claim": "A first send cancelled mid-prepare causes the same recursion even with the lock",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "chrome_cancel.py: cancel the first evaluate right after attach; lock-only prototype gives RangeError 3/3; with the idempotent JS guard OK 3/3"
                }
              ],
              "realism": "medium",
              "severity": "wrong-result",
              "fix_assessment": {
                "kind": "minimal-local",
                "prototype_dir": "/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/R18-fixsafety/pkg",
                "diff_summary": "zendriver/core/connection.py (diff at .../R18-fixsafety/fix.diff): (a) Connection.__init__ adds self._prepare_lock = asyncio.Lock(), like _attach_lock and _open_lock. (b) In send(), the _prepare_expert()/_prepare_headless() calls run inside `async with self._prepare_lock:`, followed by `if self.websocket is None: raise ProtocolException(\"target session was detached\")` before the Listener and Transaction are created. (c) _prepare_headless and _prepare_expert capture session_id = self.session_id first and only set the done flag when `self.websocket is not None and self.session_id == session_id`. (d) The expert script is wrapped in `if (!Element.prototype._attachShadow) { ... }` so a duplicate registration is a no-op.",
                "regression_test_path": "/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/R18-fixsafety/tests/test_r18_prepare.py",
                "fails_on_repo": "yes",
                "passes_on_prototype": "yes",
                "mypy_ok": "yes",
                "risks": "Re-entrancy: the lock covers only the _prepare_* calls. Those call only _send_oneshot, then _send_transaction, then websocket.send, and never call send() or aopen(), so the lock is never acquired twice. It is released before _register_handlers' nested send(). The nested send from aopen->_register_handlers takes the lock before the outer send reaches it, one after the other, not nested.\nLock ordering: tab._attach_lock is held while browser_connection._prepare_lock is taken (through browser_connection.send in _attach_session). Nothing is acquired while tab._prepare_lock is held, so there is no cycle.\nCancel and error paths: `async with` releases the lock. A detach fails the pending oneshot, which _send_oneshot swallows, so the lock is freed promptly. The prepare oneshots have no timeout, so concurrent sends now wait for the first send's prepare. In the repo each of them already awaited its own identical oneshot, so liveness is unchanged.\nCaller-visible change: a send whose session was detached during prepare now raises ProtocolException('target session was detached') earlier, with the same message as the existing later check. Previously, a tab with event handlers could silently reattach in _register_handlers' nested send and succeed on an unprepared session, which could also resurrect a session that a concurrent tab.aclose() had just detached. Now it raises, and the next send reattaches and prepares properly.\nRemaining small window: if another task reattaches during the prepare, the first send's command may reach the new session before that session's prepare (the waiting task runs it right after). The flag is not left stale.\nNot covered: the ValueError path from refinement 3. On a page that defines its own Element.prototype._attachShadow, the JS guard would skip the override (edge case).\nmypy on the prototype vs an untouched copy: 332 identical errors in both, all in cdp/ generated code, none in core/connection.py, so no new errors.\nThe repo was not modified, and git status is clean."
              }
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "group_id": "R19",
