          {
            "lens": "refute",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "R19",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "I could not refute the core claim. Listener.listener_loop (connection.py, the `else: asyncio.create_task(asyncio.to_thread(callback, *args))` branch, ~L1002) runs every non-coroutine-function handler in a worker thread. Connection.feed_cdp (~L556) calls `asyncio.ensure_future(self.send(...))`, which needs an event loop in the current thread, so it raises `RuntimeError: There is no current event loop in thread 'asyncio_0'` there. Fetch.continueRequest is never sent and the paused request stays paused. I reproduced this on real headless Chromium 141 and against a browser-free fake CDP peer (Python 3.13). The asyncio failure is identical on 3.11 and 3.12. It is a regression: in the zendriver 0.10.1 wheel, sync handlers ran inline on the loop thread, so feed_cdp from a sync handler or lambda worked. The 0.10.2 change ('Refactor to asynchronous handlers to avoid blocking the listening loop') moved them to to_thread and broke it. The lambda/coroutine sub-claim is also real, but the plain 'lambda returning a coroutine' and 'object with async __call__' forms never worked, even in 0.10.1. Only 'lambda: asyncio.create_task(...)' (and any other loop-bound call from a sync handler) is new breakage from the same root cause.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "A2.6",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "(a) Confirmed with real Chromium. A sync def RequestPaused handler calling conn.feed_cdp(continue_request) ran in thread asyncio_0 and raised RuntimeError 'no current event loop'. fetch('/x') stayed 'pending' at 8s and 28s, and a manual continue at 28s was accepted by Chrome and resolved it with status 200. (b) Confirmed behaviour: `lambda e: process(e)` and an AsyncCallable instance never ran and produced 'coroutine ... was never awaited' RuntimeWarnings. `lambda e: asyncio.create_task(...)` gave 'Task exception was never retrieved | RuntimeError(no running event loop)'. functools.partial(async_fn) does work. Caveat: the coroutine-returning lambda and the async __call__ object are not regressions. 0.10.1 used asyncio.iscoroutinefunction and called sync callbacks inline, discarding the return value, so they never worked. add_handler's own lambda example (print) works."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "B14.4",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "`lambda ev, t: t.feed_cdp(cdp.fetch.continue_request(...))` fails the same way on real Chromium (the paused fetch stays pending for 28s or more) and on the fake peer: the peer received only Fetch.enable and never Fetch.continueRequest. With an async handler the peer received Fetch.continueRequest. The claim that the error is 'lost' is overstated. The asyncio default handler prints a full 'Task exception was never retrieved' traceback that points at feed_cdp, plus a 'coroutine Connection.send was never awaited' warning, but only when the task is garbage-collected. zendriver's own 'exception in callback' logging never sees it. A raw tab.send(page.navigate) has no timeout, so it hangs for as long as Chrome keeps the request paused."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A12.3",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "With a sync lambda handler that calls feed_cdp, Fetch.enable, then tab.get(http://127.0.0.1/page), tab.get raised TimeoutError after 10.0s (the default timeout), so this path ends in an error, not a hang. The page never navigated, and a Runtime.evaluate sent afterwards got no reply for about 30s. The same code with an async handler returned in 0.1s with title 'OK'. The finder's observation that dispatch has been threaded since 5c1a005 is only an artefact of the squashed import. The real origin is upstream 0.10.2, which I checked against the 0.10.1 and 0.10.2 PyPI wheels."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "Probes in /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/R19-refute/ (Python 3.13.16, websockets 14.1, Chromium 141, zendriver imported from /home/user/zendriver/zendriver/__init__.py):\n- probe_fetch.py on real Chromium:\n  - async_def: 'fetch completed status=200 after 0.01s', handler thread MainThread.\n  - sync_def: 'fetch HUNG >8s; paused ids=[favicon.ico, /x] threads=[asyncio_0, asyncio_0]', 'at 8s window.__r=pending', 'at 28s window.__r=pending', 'manual continue after 28s accepted by Chrome; fetch result now=200', handler_errors=[RuntimeError(\"There is no current event loop in thread 'asyncio_0'.\") x2].\n  - sync_lambda: same hang.\n  - sync_def_threadsafe (handler uses asyncio.run_coroutine_threadsafe(conn.send(...), loop)): completes in 0.01s. This user-side workaround works today.\n- probe_nav.py: async handler, tab.get returns in 0.1s with title 'OK'. Sync lambda: 'tab.get raised TimeoutError after 10.0s', and the stack shows tab.py Tab.get at `await asyncio.wait_for(self.send(cdp.page.navigate(url)), timeout)`.\n- probe_lambda.py: 'async work that ran: {partial_async: 1}'. lambda_returns_coro, lambda_create_task and async_call_obj did not run, with RuntimeWarnings and 'RuntimeError(no running event loop)'.\n- fake_peer.py (browser-free): the async peer saw [Fetch.enable, Fetch.enable, Fetch.continueRequest, Fetch.continueRequest]. The sync peer saw only [Fetch.enable, Fetch.enable], with the full traceback in feed_cdp -> ensure_future -> get_event_loop.\n- minimal_asyncio.py: ensure_future from a to_thread worker raises 'There is no current event loop in thread asyncio_0' on 3.11.17, 3.12.3 and 3.13.16.\n- History: in the zendriver 0.10.1 vs 0.10.2 wheels, 0.10.1 ran sync callbacks inline with `callback(event, self.connection)` and 0.10.2 introduced `asyncio.create_task(asyncio.to_thread(run_callback))` (CHANGELOG 0.10.2: 'Refactor to asynchronous handlers to avoid blocking the listening loop').\n- tests/core/test_tab.py::test_sync_handlers_are_each_called uses loop.call_soon_threadsafe inside sync handlers, which shows the maintainers know sync handlers run off-loop. No test covers feed_cdp from a sync handler.\n- Every internal handler (intercept.py, expect.py, browser.py, tab.py) is async def, so library code never triggers this; only user sync handlers do.\n- examples/fetch_domain.py uses an async def handler with feed_cdp, and that works.\n- Searching upstream cdpdriver/zendriver issues for feed_cdp, 'no current event loop' and RequestPaused found nothing.",
              "refinements": "- The failure is deterministic (not a race) for any sync callable handler (def, lambda, bound method) that calls feed_cdp or any other loop-bound asyncio API (ensure_future, create_task, Event.set, Future.set_result) on every Python version from 3.10 up. 3.10 is assumed; I tested 3.11 through 3.13.\n- How long the hang lasts depends on the caller. tab.get is bounded by its default timeout=10 and raises TimeoutError. A raw tab.send(page.navigate) or runtime.evaluate(await_promise=True) waits indefinitely, because Chrome keeps the request paused (verified for 28s or more).\n- The failure is loud, not silent: asyncio's default exception handler prints the traceback at GC time, and RuntimeWarnings are emitted.\n- The 'lambda returning a coroutine' and 'async __call__ object' forms are long-standing design limitations from nodriver and 0.10.1, not regressions. The docstring advertises lambdas, but its example is a sync print lambda, which works. Treat these as lower-priority API polish (await or schedule awaitable results), separate from the feed_cdp regression.\n- Fix notes: feed_cdp needs the connection's loop when called off-loop. Nothing in the thread knows the loop, so capture it, for example when the Listener is created. Use run_coroutine_threadsafe off-loop and keep ensure_future/create_task on-loop. Alternatively, run sync handlers on the loop again, which reverts 0.10.2's non-blocking intent.",
              "chrome_claims": [
                {
                  "claim": "Chrome keeps a Fetch-paused request paused indefinitely without a continue: the fetch was still pending at 8s and 28s, and continueRequest at 28s was accepted and resolved it with status 200.",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "Ran probe_fetch.py sync_def against real headless Chromium 141 and read window.__r at 8s and 28s, then sent manual Fetch.continueRequest for each paused id."
                },
