          {
            "lens": "fixsafety",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "R19",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "Confirmed on Python 3.11 and 3.13, with a fake CDP peer and with real headless Chromium. In Listener.listener_loop, every non-coroutine handler is dispatched as asyncio.create_task(asyncio.to_thread(callback, *args)), so it runs in a worker thread 'asyncio_N' that has no event loop. Connection.feed_cdp is just asyncio.ensure_future(self.send(cdp_obj)). Called from that thread, it raises RuntimeError('There is no current event loop in thread ...'). The error is lost in an unretrieved to_thread task, Connection.send is never awaited, and Fetch.continueRequest is never sent, so the paused request hangs. A sync callable that returns a coroutine (a lambda calling an async function, or an object with an async __call__, for which inspect.iscoroutinefunction is False) has its coroutine dropped with only a RuntimeWarning. The worker-thread dispatch itself is deliberate (CHANGELOG 0.10.2: 'Refactor to asynchronous handlers to avoid blocking the listening loop'; test_sync_handlers_are_each_called uses loop.call_soon_threadsafe), so the right minimal fix keeps the thread and makes feed_cdp thread-safe. It also awaits awaitables that sync handlers return.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "A2.6",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "(a) Confirmed: a sync RequestPaused handler that calls feed_cdp raises 'no current event loop in thread asyncio_0', and with real Chromium the page fetch('/x') hung for 8s. (b) Confirmed for `lambda e: process(e)` and for an object with an async __call__: the coroutine is never awaited and Python prints a RuntimeWarning. Upstream nodriver had the same limitation, because it also never awaited returned coroutines, so this half is a long-standing gap rather than a regression. The `lambda e: asyncio.create_task(...)` sub-case is real ('no running event loop' in the thread). It remains after the minimal fix because only running sync handlers on the loop thread would fix it, which is a design change."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "B14.4",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Same root cause as A2.6(a). Reproduced with the feed_cdp call inside the handler, on 3.11 and 3.13: RuntimeError from ensure_future in thread asyncio_0, 'Task exception was never retrieved ... to_thread()', 'coroutine Connection.send was never awaited', and the request stays paused. The async-handler variant works. The secondary note (asyncio.Event.set / create_task from a sync handler is unsafe) is accurate but follows from the deliberate thread design; the existing tests already use call_soon_threadsafe for this."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A12.3",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Same root cause and same reproduction: feed_cdp -> asyncio.ensure_future in a worker thread -> RuntimeError, so the paused request is never continued. Verified with the connection passed as the second handler argument (event, connection), which is the form this member describes."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "Code read fresh: zendriver/core/connection.py, Listener.listener_loop (callback dispatch at ~line 999-1002: `if inspect.iscoroutinefunction(callback): asyncio.create_task(callback(*args)) else: asyncio.create_task(asyncio.to_thread(callback, *args))`), and Connection.feed_cdp (~line 556: `asyncio.ensure_future(self.send(cdp_obj))`). The docstring calls feed_cdp the non-async way to answer RequestPaused.\n\nBrowser-free regression test: /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/R19-fixsafety/tests/test_r19_sync_handlers.py. A fake websockets peer answers every command and emits Fetch.requestPaused on Fetch.enable; the tests wait for Fetch.continueRequest.\n\nRepo, py3.13:\n  zendriver imported from: /home/user/zendriver/zendriver/__init__.py\n  Failed: Fetch.continueRequest never sent; handler ran in threads ['asyncio_0', 'asyncio_0']\n  Task exception was never retrieved ... coro=<to_thread()> exception=RuntimeError(\"There is no current event loop in thread 'asyncio_0'.\")\n  RuntimeWarning: coroutine 'Connection.send' was never awaited\n  RuntimeWarning: coroutine '...process' was never awaited\n  4 failed, 3 passed\n\nPrototype, py3.13:\n  zendriver imported from: .../R19-fixsafety/pkg/zendriver/__init__.py\n  7 passed in 0.55s\n\nThe same counts on py3.11.17 (throwaway venv): repo 4 failed, 3 passed; prototype 7 passed.\n\nReal headless Chromium (probe_chrome.py: local http.server, sync handler calls tab.feed_cdp(continue_request), Fetch.enable, evaluate fetch('/x') with an 8s wait_for):\n  repo: 'page fetch HUNG: request stayed paused; handler threads ['asyncio_0', 'asyncio_0']', plus 'RuntimeError: There is no current event loop in thread asyncio_0'\n  prototype: 'fetch completed, status = 200'\n\nThe repo's tests/core/test_connection.py and tests/core/test_tab.py, run against the prototype (headless, -m 'not external'): 43 passed, 4 deselected.",
              "refinements": "1) The hang is browser-side: the request stays paused. No Python task is stuck. The only Python-side traces are the unretrieved to_thread task exception and the never-awaited warning. 2) The worker-thread dispatch is intended (0.10.2 changelog, and an existing test uses call_soon_threadsafe), so the fix should not move sync handlers back onto the loop. Doing that would be a design change: blocking sync handlers would block the listener again. 3) The minimal fix leaves one sub-claim of A2.6 unfixed: `lambda e: asyncio.create_task(...)` and other loop APIs called from a sync handler still fail in the thread (verified on the prototype: 'RuntimeError: no running event loop'). That needs either a doc note in add_handler (sync handlers run in a worker thread; use loop.call_soon_threadsafe) or the design change. 4) feed_cdp on a connection that has never created a listener, called from a non-loop thread, still raises as before. Example: a handler on browser.connection calling feed_cdp on a tab that was never opened. Within the documented pattern the handler's own connection always has a listener, because that listener dispatched the event.",
              "chrome_claims": [
                {
                  "claim": "With real headless Chromium, a sync Fetch.requestPaused handler that calls tab.feed_cdp(continue_request) leaves the request paused, and a page fetch() never completes on the repo code",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "probe_chrome.py against a local http.server, under timeout 120 with an 8s asyncio.wait_for: repo printed 'page fetch HUNG' plus the RuntimeError; the prototype printed 'fetch completed, status = 200'. The browser was stopped with browser.stop() and no leftover processes were found."
                },
                {
                  "claim": "Chromium sends one Fetch.requestPaused per intercepted request, and the handler is invoked once per event",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "The probe recorded the handler thread names; the handler ran twice (2 paused requests) in both the repo and prototype runs."
                }
              ],
              "realism": "high",
              "severity": "hang",
              "fix_assessment": {
                "kind": "minimal-local",
                "prototype_dir": "/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/R19-fixsafety/pkg",
                "diff_summary": "zendriver/core/connection.py only (full diff in /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/R19-fixsafety/fix.diff).\n(1) Connection.feed_cdp: the on-loop path is unchanged. Only when asyncio.get_running_loop() raises (the case that currently always fails) does it take the loop from self.listener.task.get_loop() and call loop.call_soon_threadsafe(asyncio.ensure_future, self.send(cdp_obj)). With no listener it falls through to the old ensure_future, which raises the same error as before.\n```\nloop = None\ntry:\n    asyncio.get_running_loop()\nexcept RuntimeError:\n    # synchronous event handlers run in a worker thread (see\n    # Listener.listener_loop), which has no event loop of its own\n    if self.listener and self.listener.task:\n        loop = self.listener.task.get_loop()\nif loop is not None:\n    loop.call_soon_threadsafe(asyncio.ensure_future, self.send(cdp_obj))\nelse:\n    asyncio.ensure_future(self.send(cdp_obj))\n```\n(2) New module-level helper `async def _call_sync_handler(handler, *args)`. It runs `result = await asyncio.to_thread(handler, *args)` and then awaits the result if inspect.isawaitable(result). listener_loop's sync branch now does asyncio.create_task(_call_sync_handler(callback, *args)) in place of create_task(asyncio.to_thread(...)). Sync handlers still run off-loop.\nruff format and ruff check pass.",
                "regression_test_path": "/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/R19-fixsafety/tests/test_r19_sync_handlers.py",
                "fails_on_repo": "yes",
                "passes_on_prototype": "yes",
                "mypy_ok": "yes",
                "risks": "Re-entrancy and locks: the worker thread only builds the send() coroutine object and schedules it. It does not wait for the result and holds no lock. send() runs on the loop thread and takes _open_lock/_attach_lock there as usual, so there is no new lock ordering and no deadlock. Reading listener.task concurrently is a benign race: any listener of the connection belongs to the same loop.\n\nErrors: call_soon_threadsafe(ensure_future, ...) creates the task on the loop. A failing send therefore still shows up as 'Task exception was never retrieved', the same as the on-loop path (covered by test_feed_cdp_error_is_logged_like_on_loop_path). run_coroutine_threadsafe would have hidden the error in an unread concurrent future. If the loop is already closed, call_soon_threadsafe raises RuntimeError in the handler thread, which matches today's error class.\n\nBehaviour changes callers would notice: (a) feed_cdp from sync handlers now works. (b) An awaitable returned by a sync handler is now awaited on the loop. This covers a lambda calling an async function and an object with an async __call__. A handler that returns a never-completing awaitable leaves one pending task. That task does not block the listener, the same as a hanging async handler. Handlers returning non-awaitables are unaffected.\n\nNot fixed by design: loop APIs such as create_task or Event.set called directly inside a sync handler still fail or are unsafe in the worker thread. Fixing that means running sync handlers on the loop, an API/behaviour change that would undo the 0.10.2 non-blocking intent. Recommend a docstring note in add_handler and feed_cdp instead.\n\nmypy: 332 errors on both the prototype and an untouched copy (same set after normalising line numbers), with 0 in core/connection.py, so no new errors."
              }
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
