        "lenses": [
          {
            "lens": "reproduce",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "R20",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "Confirmed in real headless Chromium 141 (Python 3.13). Element.send_keys(str) calls KeyEvents.from_text(text, KeyPressEvent.CHAR) (zendriver/core/element.py, Element.send_keys ~line 721). That maps both '\\n' and '\\r' to SpecialKeys.ENTER. KeyEvents._get_key_and_text (CHAR branch) then calls KeyEvents.conv_to_str (zendriver/core/keys.py ~line 167), which returns '\\n'. So the payload is Input.dispatchKeyEvent(type='char', text='\\n'). Chrome turns that into keypress charCode 10, which inserts nothing and does not submit the form. The result: line breaks are silently dropped from textarea and contenteditable input, and 'query\\n' or 'query\\r' never submits. A raw char '\\r' (charCode 13) works correctly. The class's own SPECIAL_KEY_CHAR_MAP maps ENTER to '\\r', and the DOWN_AND_UP path uses that map and works. CRLF works only by accident: grapheme treats '\\r\\n' as one grapheme, so it skips the ENTER mapping and goes out as raw text '\\r\\n'.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "A10.4",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "My own Chromium run reproduced every claimed symptom. send_keys('line1\\nline2\\rline3') on a textarea gives the value 'line1line2line3'. send_keys('abc\\n') and send_keys('abc\\r') on a form input give submits=0 (keypress charCode 10). send_keys('a\\nb') on a contenteditable gives 'ab'. The controls behave as claimed: raw char '\\r' gives textarea 'a\\nb' and input submits=1, while raw char '\\n' gives 'ab' and submits=0. send_keys('abc\\r\\n') submits (charCode 13). SpecialKeys.ENTER through DOWN_AND_UP submits, and from_mixed_input(['a\\nb']) through DOWN_AND_UP keeps the newline. Only the CHAR path, which is the send_keys(str) default, is broken. Note: '\\r' alone is also broken, because it goes through the same ENTER mapping."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "All runs used PATH=<scratchpad>/bin:$PATH under `timeout 120 /home/user/zendriver/.venv/bin/python ...` (Python 3.13.16, Chrome/141.0.7390.37, zendriver imported from /home/user/zendriver/zendriver/__init__.py).\n(1) Browser-free: /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/R20-reproduce/probe_payloads.py printed: 'a\\nb' -> ['a','\\n','b']; 'a\\rb' -> ['a','\\n','b']; 'a\\r\\nb' -> ['a','\\r\\n','b']; payload for '\\n' = {'type_':'char','text':'\\n','key':None,...}; ENTER DOWN_AND_UP texts = [('keyDown','\\r'),('keyUp','\\r')]; SPECIAL_KEY_CHAR_MAP[ENTER]='\\r'.\n(2) Chromium: /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/R20-reproduce/probe_chromium.py (page.html: a form whose onsubmit counts submits, plus an input, a textarea and a contenteditable, all with keypress logging). Key lines: `send_keys input 'abc\\n' -> submits:0, log ...keypress charCode 10`; `send_keys input 'abc\\r' -> submits:0, charCode 10`; `send_keys input 'abc\\r\\n' -> submits:1, charCode 13`; `send_keys textarea 'line1\\nline2\\rline3' -> ta:\"line1line2line3\"`; `send_keys contenteditable 'a\\nb' -> ce:\"ab\"`; `raw char '\\r' into textarea -> ta:\"a\\nb\"`; `raw char '\\n' into textarea -> ta:\"ab\"`; `raw char '\\r' into input -> submits:1`; `raw char '\\n' into input -> submits:0`; `'abc' + SpecialKeys.ENTER -> submits:1`.\n(3) The prototype with conv_to_str returning '\\r' for ENTER (probe_mixed.py, run with PYTHONPATH set to the prototype and the import path printed) gave textarea 'line1\\nline2\\nline3', input 'abc\\n' submits:1, input 'abc\\r' submits:1, contenteditable 'a<div>b</div>'. The repo copy gave 'line1line2line3', submits:0 and 'ab'.",
              "refinements": "Two points the claim slightly understates. First, '\\r' alone is also dropped: from_text sends '\\r' through the same ENTER→conv_to_str path, which produces '\\n'. Second, contenteditable elements also lose the line break ('a\\nb' becomes 'ab'). The bug is confined to the CHAR path: send_keys(str), and from_text(..., KeyPressEvent.CHAR) when passed explicitly. send_keys(SpecialKeys.ENTER) and from_mixed_input / from_text with DOWN_AND_UP use SPECIAL_KEY_CHAR_MAP ('\\r') and work. CRLF works by accident because grapheme groups '\\r\\n' into a single grapheme that goes out unmapped.",
              "chrome_claims": [
                {
                  "claim": "Chrome ignores Input.dispatchKeyEvent(type='char', text='\\n'): it fires keypress charCode 10, inserts nothing in a textarea, input or contenteditable, and does not trigger implicit form submission",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "Real headless Chromium 141.0.7390.37, both through send_keys and through raw cdp.input_.dispatch_key_event(type_='char', text='\\n'); the keypress log showed charCode 10, textarea 'ab', submits 0"
