              "group_id": "R20",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "I could not refute this. Element.send_keys(str) builds its events with KeyEvents.from_text(text, KeyPressEvent.CHAR), which maps both '\\n' and '\\r' to SpecialKeys.ENTER. The CHAR branch of KeyEvents._get_key_and_text then calls KeyEvents.conv_to_str(ENTER), and conv_to_str returns '\\n' (keys.py, around line 167). The class's own SPECIAL_KEY_CHAR_MAP and the DOWN_AND_UP path (_build_action_data) use '\\r' for ENTER. Verified on real headless Chrome 141.0.7390.37: Input.dispatchKeyEvent(type='char', text='\\n') fires only a keypress with charCode 10. It inserts nothing and does not submit the form, so send_keys('abc\\n') and send_keys('abc\\r') never submit, and a textarea or contenteditable loses every line break. '\\r' alone is a regression: the old char-per-char send_keys path sends a raw '\\r', which does submit (checked by emulating that path).",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "A10.4",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Reproduced on Chrome 141 headless with Python 3.13, against the repo copy of zendriver. On an <input> in a form, send_keys('abc\\n') gives 0 submits and send_keys('abc\\r') gives 0 submits. send_keys('abc\\r\\n') gives 1 submit, and send_keys(SpecialKeys.ENTER) gives 1 submit. On a textarea, send_keys('line1\\nline2\\rline3') gives 'line1line2line3'. On a contenteditable, send_keys('a\\nb') gives 'ab'. Raw char '\\r' gives 'a\\nb' in the textarea and 'a<div>b</div>' in the contenteditable, and it submits the input's form. Raw char '\\n' gives 'ab' and no submit. Payloads printed without a browser: 'abc\\n' and 'abc\\r' both end in ('char','\\n'), and 'abc\\r\\n' ends in ('char','\\r\\n') because CRLF is one grapheme. All parts of the claim hold."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "Probe scripts: /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/R20-refute/payloads.py builds the payloads without a browser. /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/R20-refute/probe.py drives real Chromium with page.html: a form with onsubmit and an input, plus a textarea, a contenteditable and a capture-phase event log. Both printed zendriver.__file__ = /home/user/zendriver/zendriver/__init__.py (repo copy, Python 3.13.16). Payload output: 'abc\\n' -> [...('char','\\n')]; 'abc\\r' -> [...('char','\\n')]; 'abc\\r\\n' -> [...('char','\\r\\n')]; SpecialKeys.ENTER DOWN_AND_UP -> keyDown/keyUp with text '\\r' and VK 13. Chromium output, all verified: \"send_keys input 'abc\\n' -> submits 0, events keypress charCode 10\"; \"send_keys input 'abc\\r' -> submits 0, keypress charCode 10\"; \"send_keys input 'abc\\r\\n' -> submits 1\"; \"send_keys input SpecialKeys.ENTER -> submits 1, keydown 'Enter', keypress 'Enter' 13\"; \"raw char '\\r' -> submits 1\"; \"raw char '\\n' -> submits 0\"; \"OLD path list('abc\\r') -> submits 1\"; \"OLD path list('abc\\n') -> submits 0\"; \"textarea 'line1\\nline2\\rline3' -> 'line1line2line3'\"; \"textarea 'a\\r\\nb' -> 'a\\nb'\"; \"contenteditable 'a\\nb' -> 'ab'\"; \"raw char contenteditable '\\r' -> 'a<div>b</div>'\". Refutation attempts that failed: (1) No test asserts that '\\n' maps to char '\\n'. tests/core/test_keyinputs.py only sends Enter through from_mixed_input/SpecialKeys, which use DOWN_AND_UP and '\\r'. (2) Nothing in history shows '\\n' was chosen on purpose. History is squashed at 5c1a005, so the KeyEvents rewrite PR is not visible. The '\\n' in conv_to_str contradicts SPECIAL_KEY_CHAR_MAP[ENTER]='\\r' in the same class, while SPACE and TAB match that map, so it looks like an oversight. (3) CHANGELOG says send_keys 'is still backwards compatible with passing a string', but '\\r' behaviour regressed. (4) The send_keys docstring tells users that sending \\n or \\r\\n 'work wonders', so users are steered toward the input that fails. Internal callers never pass a newline (the only one is tab.py's body.send_keys('thisisunsafe')), so the bug only reaches user code.",
              "refinements": "(a) A bare '\\n' did not work before the KeyEvents rewrite either: the old char-per-char path also sent char '\\n', as my emulation shows. So the '\\n' part is long-standing; only '\\r' is a new regression, and I checked that only by emulating the old path. (b) CRLF '\\r\\n' works by accident: it is one grapheme, so _handle_string_key_lookup misses it, and it goes out as raw char '\\r\\n', which Chrome treats as Enter (verified). (c) Only the str -> CHAR path is affected. send_keys(SpecialKeys.ENTER) and from_mixed_input(...) (DOWN_AND_UP) send keyDown with '\\r' and work (verified). (d) Note for the fix: switching the CHAR path to char '\\r' fixes implicit form submission and line breaks in textarea and contenteditable (verified with raw char '\\r'). But a char event never fires keydown, and the keypress has an empty key. JS handlers listening for keydown with key==='Enter', common in SPA search boxes, would still not react. Sending Enter as a keyDown/keyUp pair even in CHAR mode would cover those handlers too: SpecialKeys.ENTER produced keydown 'Enter', keypress 13 and a submit (verified). (e) The logic is pure Python, so the Python version does not matter. Realism: 'query\\n' to submit and multi-line textarea input are common automation idioms, and the docstring recommends \\n. Workarounds exist (SpecialKeys.ENTER, '\\r\\n') but are not obvious.",
              "chrome_claims": [
