            "lens": "reproduce",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "R21",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "Confirmed with my own runs, browser-free and with real headless Chromium. HTTPApi._request (zendriver/core/browser.py, HTTPApi._request, about line 1133) calls the module-level urllib.request.urlopen. Its default opener's ProxyHandler reads http_proxy/HTTP_PROXY from the environment, so GET http://127.0.0.1:<port>/json/version goes to the proxy whenever no_proxy does not cover 127.0.0.1. Every test_connection() attempt then fails. Browser.start() stops Chrome and raises 'Failed to connect to browser ... running as root ... sandbox=False', even though Chrome is up and answers direct requests on that port.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "A4.2",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Reproduced on Python 3.13.16 / websockets 14.1. Browser-free: with http_proxy or HTTP_PROXY pointing at a dummy proxy that answers 502 and no no_proxy, HTTPApi.get('version') raised HTTPError 502. The proxy logged 'GET http://127.0.0.1:<port>/json/version HTTP/1.1' and the local DevTools server got nothing. no_proxy=localhost alone does not help, because the host is 127.0.0.1. Only no_proxy=127.0.0.1, or no proxy var at all, works. End-to-end: zd.start(headless=True, browser_connection_max_tries=3) failed after 1.3s with 'Failed to connect to browser'; the proxy saw 4 /json/version requests. On attempts 2 to 4, a proxy-less request (ProxyHandler({})) to the same port returned 'Chrome/141.0.7390.37'. The control run without http_proxy started fine and evaluated a page."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "Commands, all run from /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/R21-reproduce with zendriver imported from /home/user/zendriver/zendriver/__init__.py on Python 3.13.16:\n\n1) Browser-free, one fresh process per scenario: `for s in baseline http_proxy HTTP_PROXY https_only all_proxy no_proxy_127 no_proxy_localhost; do timeout 60 /home/user/zendriver/.venv/bin/python probe_httpapi2.py $s; done`\n   [baseline] OK\n   [http_proxy] FAIL HTTPError: HTTP Error 502: Bad Gateway; proxy saw=['GET http://127.0.0.1:35539/json/version HTTP/1.1']; devtools saw=[]\n   [HTTP_PROXY] FAIL HTTPError 502; proxy saw=['GET http://127.0.0.1:34871/json/version HTTP/1.1']\n   [https_only] OK; [all_proxy] OK (urllib does not use all_proxy for http)\n   [no_proxy_127] OK\n   [no_proxy_localhost] FAIL HTTPError 502; proxy saw=['GET http://127.0.0.1:46683/json/version HTTP/1.1']\n   Pitfall: my first attempt ran every scenario in one process and every scenario passed. urllib caches its global opener on the first urlopen and reads http_proxy only then, so a baseline-first run masks the bug. In real use the env var is set before the process starts, so the bug shows.\n\n2) Real Chromium (PATH=.../scratchpad/bin:$PATH): `timeout 120 .venv/bin/python probe_start.py proxy` printed 'start FAILED after 1.3s: Exception: Failed to connect to browser ... One of the causes could be when you are running [as root]'. The proxy saw 4x 'GET http://127.0.0.1:46109/json/version' (Chrome itself also sent its own clients2.google.com request through the env proxy). `probe_start.py control` printed 'start OK after 0.3s', 'page eval: hi', proxy saw [].\n\n3) `timeout 120 .venv/bin/python probe_start_listening.py proxy` wraps HTTPApi.get to also make a ProxyHandler({}) request to the same port at each attempt. Result: ['direct FAIL URLError', 'direct OK: Chrome/141.0.7390.37', 'direct OK: Chrome/141.0.7390.37', 'direct OK: Chrome/141.0.7390.37'], while zendriver's own requests all went to the proxy, so Chrome was listening when start() gave up.\n\nNo Chrome processes were left over (pgrep on my private user_data_dir found nothing), the temp profile and venv dirs were removed, and the repo is untouched (git status clean).",
              "refinements": "(a) The finder's websocket side note also reproduces. probe_ws.py runs a fake CDP websocket peer on 127.0.0.1 with http_proxy set and no no_proxy. Under the locked websockets 14.1, Connection.send(cdp.page.enable()) worked and the proxy saw nothing. Under websockets 15.0.1 (pyproject allows >=14.0; I put it on PYTHONPATH from a throwaway --target dir), the send failed with InvalidProxyMessage and the proxy saw 'CONNECT 127.0.0.1:33407 HTTP/1.1'. So with websockets >=15, fixing only HTTPApi still leaves start() broken behind such a proxy, at Connection.aopen's websockets.connect (zendriver/core/connection.py, Connection.aopen, about line 440). (b) A fix cannot just pass proxy=None to websockets.connect: probe_ws_kw.py shows websockets 14.1 raises TypeError: create_connection() got an unexpected keyword argument 'proxy', while 15.0.1 accepts it. It needs a version check or a websockets>=15 floor. (c) no_proxy=localhost does not cover the 127.0.0.1 that zendriver hard-codes as config.host (browser.py, about line 534), so even users with a typical 'localhost' exclusion hit this. (d) https_proxy alone and all_proxy alone do not trigger it for the http:// DevTools URL in urllib.",
              "chrome_claims": [
                {
                  "claim": "Chrome was started and listening on the DevTools port while start() reported 'Failed to connect to browser'",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "probe_start_listening.py made a proxy-less urllib request (ProxyHandler({})) to the same host:port at each test_connection attempt; attempts 2-4 returned Browser 'Chrome/141.0.7390.37' while zendriver's requests hit the dummy proxy and got 502"
                },
                {
                  "claim": "start() works normally without http_proxy in the environment",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "probe_start.py control: start OK after 0.3s, tab.evaluate returned 'hi'"
                }
              ],
              "realism": "medium",
              "severity": "crash",
              "fix_assessment": {
                "kind": "minimal-local",
                "prototype_dir": "",
                "diff_summary": "",
                "regression_test_path": "",
                "fails_on_repo": "not-run",
                "passes_on_prototype": "not-run",
                "mypy_ok": "not-run",
                "risks": "Not assessed under this lens. The ProxyHandler({}) opener idea is consistent with probe_start_listening.py, where that opener reached Chrome directly while http_proxy was set. Under websockets>=15 the CDP websocket also needs proxy=None, which websockets 14.x rejects with TypeError."
              }
            }
          },
          {
            "lens": "refute",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "R21",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "I could not refute this. HTTPApi._request in zendriver/core/browser.py (the urlopen call at about line 1133) uses the global urllib opener, which proxies from the environment. With http_proxy or HTTP_PROXY set and no_proxy not listing the literal 127.0.0.1, GET http://127.0.0.1:<port>/json/version goes to the proxy. I reproduced this with real headless Chromium 141: zendriver.start() raised 'Failed to connect to browser ... running as root' after 1.4s. During those same attempts, a direct GET to Chrome's port returned 200 with webSocketDebuggerUrl. Nothing serialises or bypasses this upstream, no test or doc asserts the current behaviour, and git history (shallow, starting at 5c1a005) shows no sign that it is deliberate.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "A4.2",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Reproduced with real Chromium on Python 3.13 with websockets 14.1. The env proxy received 'GET http://127.0.0.1:<port>/json/version' on every test_connection attempt and start() raised the misleading sandbox/root error. Browser.start calls stop() on this path, so nothing leaked. Lowercase http_proxy and uppercase HTTP_PROXY both trigger it. https_proxy alone does not affect HTTPApi, because the request is plain http. The finder's side note is also confirmed: websockets 17.3 (what pip installs today under websockets>=14.0) routes the ws:// CDP socket through the env proxy with CONNECT 127.0.0.1:<port>, even when only https_proxy is set."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "I wrote my own probes in /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/R21-refute/.\n(1) bypass_semantics.py, run on Python 3.11.17, 3.12.3 and 3.13.16 with the same result on all three. proxy_bypass('127.0.0.1:9222') is False when no_proxy is unset, 'localhost', '127.0.0.0/8', '127.*' or '::1,localhost'. It is True only for '127.0.0.1', 'localhost,127.0.0.1', '.0.0.1' or '*'.\n(2) httpapi_probe.py, browser-free: HTTPApi against a fake /json/version server, with the env proxy pointed at a fake proxy.\n- Reject proxy (lower or upper case, no_proxy unset, 'localhost' or '127.0.0.0/8'): HTTPError 502 at 0.0s. The proxy saw the absolute-form request and the devtools server saw nothing.\n- https_proxy only, or no_proxy='localhost,127.0.0.1': OK, direct.\n- Blackhole proxy: TimeoutError after 10.0s per attempt.\n- Same-host forwarding proxy: OK.\n(3) chrome_probe.py with real headless Chromium (PATH to scratchpad/bin), repo venv (websockets 14.1).\n- reject/http_proxy/no_proxy unset/tries=3: FAIL 'Failed to connect to browser' after 1.4s, 4 proxied /json/version requests. Inside the proxy, a direct origin-form GET to the same port returned 'HTTP/1.1 200 OK' with webSocketDebuggerUrl.\n- reject/HTTP_PROXY/no_proxy=localhost: same failure.\n- https_only: OK in 0.7s.\n- no_proxy=localhost,127.0.0.1: OK.\n- Faithful same-host forwarder (HTTP/1.1, origin-form, like cntlm/px): OK.\n- Blackhole proxy with tries=1: FAIL after 21.0s, so the default max_tries=10 means about 110s before the error. It is a slow failure, not a hang.\n(4) A throwaway venv with websockets 17.3 and a scratch copy of the package (zendriver.__file__ printed to show the scratch copy was imported).\n- Forwarding proxy: HTTPApi succeeded, then the proxy got 'CONNECT 127.0.0.1:<port>' and start() raised InvalidProxyStatus.\n- https_proxy only: start() raised InvalidProxyMessage.\n- In both cases the Chrome process leaked, because start() does not stop the browser when Connection fails. I killed only those leaked processes, after checking their environ for my scratch PYTHONPATH, and removed their /tmp/uc_* profiles.\nwebsockets/proxy.py get_proxy prefers ws, then socks, then https, then http for ws:// URIs, and uses urllib.request.proxy_bypass.",
              "refinements": "Exact trigger: an HTTP-scheme env proxy (http_proxy or HTTP_PROXY) is set. ALL_PROXY is ignored by urllib (assumed from source, not run). https_proxy alone does not trigger the HTTPApi part.\n\nno_proxy has to be missing the literal '127.0.0.1' (or '*' or '.0.0.1'). The common 'no_proxy=localhost' does NOT protect, and neither do curl/Go-style CIDR entries such as '127.0.0.0/8'; Python ignores both (verified on 3.11 to 3.13). That widens the reach beyond 'no_proxy unset'.\n\nIt fails only when the proxy cannot serve the client's loopback, which covers a remote corporate proxy or one that denies loopback destinations (assumed). A same-host forwarding helper such as cntlm or px works for the HTTP leg (verified with a faithful fake forwarder). Even then, websockets>=15 fails on the CONNECT for the ws leg unless that helper allows CONNECT to arbitrary loopback ports.\n\nThe result is a fast failure (about 1.4s) with a misleading 'running as root / sandbox' message. A blackholing proxy instead stalls start() for about 10s per attempt, about 110s with defaults.\n\nMacOS: urllib reads system proxy settings when no env vars are set. The default macOS bypass list ('*.local, 169.254/16') does not match 127.0.0.1 in _proxy_bypass_macosx_sysconf (logic verified on Linux; real macOS behaviour assumed). Windows registry proxies: assumed.\n\nFix scope: (a) bypass only loopback hosts (127.0.0.0/8, ::1, localhost) rather than dropping proxies for every host. connect_existing to a remote DevTools host might legitimately need the proxy. (b) Without a matching proxy=None for loopback in Connection.aopen's websockets.connect (connection.py about line 440), start() still fails for every user on websockets>=15, which is the default pip resolution because pyproject only pins >=14.0. On that path start() also leaks the Chrome process, which is a separate cleanup bug.\n\nWorkaround for users: add 127.0.0.1 to no_proxy.",
              "chrome_claims": [
                {
                  "claim": "Chrome's DevTools HTTP endpoint was up and answering /json/version (200 with webSocketDebuggerUrl) while zendriver's proxied request failed, so start() aborts even though Chrome is ready.",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "Real headless Chromium 141: the fake proxy made a direct HTTP/1.1 origin-form GET to the same port and got 'HTTP/1.1 200 OK' with webSocketDebuggerUrl."
                },
                {
                  "claim": "Headless Chromium on Linux itself uses the env http_proxy for its own traffic, so the env proxy is effective for the browser and the user plausibly wants it there.",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "The fake proxy logged 'GET http://clients2.google.com/time/1/current?...' from the Chromium zendriver launched."
                },
                {
                  "claim": "Chrome's DevTools HTTP server closes HTTP/1.0 requests without a response, returns 404 for an absolute-form request-URI, and keeps the connection alive despite 'Connection: close'. A naive proxy that forwards absolute-form or HTTP/1.0 would also fail; real proxies send origin-form HTTP/1.1.",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "Raw socket requests to a running headless Chromium (chrome_http10.py)."
                },
                {
                  "claim": "A remote corporate proxy cannot reach the client machine's 127.0.0.1:<port> and returns an error (502/403) or times out.",
                  "status": "assumed",
                  "how": "Simulated with a 502-returning fake proxy and a blackhole fake proxy. No real corporate proxy was tested."
                },
                {
                  "claim": "Same-host proxy helpers (cntlm, px) forward the loopback request successfully; squid's default ACL denies to_localhost.",
                  "status": "assumed",
                  "how": "Only a faithful fake forwarder was run, and it succeeded. Real cntlm, px and squid were not tested."
                }
              ],
              "realism": "medium",
              "severity": "crash",
              "fix_assessment": {
                "kind": "minimal-local",
                "prototype_dir": "",
                "diff_summary": "",
                "regression_test_path": "",
                "fails_on_repo": "not-run",
                "passes_on_prototype": "not-run",
                "mypy_ok": "not-run",
                "risks": "A blanket ProxyHandler({}) also drops the proxy for connect_existing to a remote DevTools host, which may need one; prefer bypassing loopback only. Fixing HTTPApi alone leaves start() failing (and leaking Chrome) for users on websockets>=15, because websockets.connect in Connection.aopen also reads env proxies, including https_proxy-only setups."
              }
            }
          },
          {
            "lens": "fixsafety",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "R21",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "Confirmed. HTTPApi._request (zendriver/core/browser.py, class HTTPApi, about line 1133) calls urllib.request.urlopen. urlopen's default ProxyHandler sends GET http://127.0.0.1:<port>/json/version to the http_proxy/HTTP_PROXY from the environment. On Linux, urllib only bypasses the proxy when no_proxy matches, and it never exempts loopback on its own. Every Browser.test_connection attempt then fails, and start() stops Chrome and raises 'Failed to connect to browser ... running as root ... sandbox=False'. A minimal local fix works: give HTTPApi its own opener built with ProxyHandler({}). The fix is incomplete for websockets>=15, which pyproject allows (websockets>=14.0) and which a fresh pip install resolves to. There, websockets.connect in Connection.aopen also takes ws/socks/https/http proxies from the environment by default, so start() still fails even with the HTTPApi fix. Fully fixing it also needs proxy=None on that connect call, gated on the parameter existing, because websockets 14.1 raises TypeError on it.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "A4.2",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Reproduced end to end with real headless Chromium 141 on Python 3.13 and websockets 14.1, repo code. A dummy proxy answered 502, http_proxy pointed at it, and no_proxy was removed. zendriver.start(headless=True, browser_connection_max_tries=3) failed after 1.4s with 'Failed to connect to browser'. The proxy recorded 4x 'GET http://127.0.0.1:46735/json/version HTTP/1.1'. With the HTTPApi opener fix, start() succeeded in 0.3s and the proxy saw no /json/version request. The finder's side note about websockets>=15 is also confirmed. With websockets 15.0.1 plus the HTTPApi fix, start() raised InvalidProxyStatus 'proxy rejected connection: HTTP 502' after the proxy got 'CONNECT 127.0.0.1:55683'."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "Code: HTTPApi._request runs `urllib.request.urlopen(request, timeout=10)` in an executor (browser.py, HTTPApi._request). On Python 3.13, urllib.request.proxy_bypass_environment returns False when 'no' is missing from the proxies, so loopback is not exempt.\n\nRegression test /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/R21-fixsafety/test_r21_http_env_proxy.py. It is browser-free: a threaded fake DevTools server and a fake proxy that answers 502, run with monkeypatched env, parametrized over http_proxy and HTTP_PROXY.\n- REPO (no PYTHONPATH): 'zendriver imported from /home/user/zendriver/zendriver/__init__.py', 'E urllib.error.HTTPError: HTTP Error 502: proxy cannot reach loopback' (x2), '2 failed in 1.29s'.\n- PROTOTYPE (PYTHONPATH=.../R21-fixsafety/pkg): 'zendriver imported from .../R21-fixsafety/pkg/zendriver/__init__.py', '2 passed in 2.23s'.\n\nCompanion test /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/R21-fixsafety/test_r21_ws_env_proxy.py. It runs Connection.aopen against a fake ws peer, parametrized over http_proxy and https_proxy.\n- websockets 15.0.1 + repo: InvalidProxyStatus x2, failed.\n- websockets 15.0.1 + pkg (HTTPApi fix only): ws tests still fail, '2 failed, 2 passed'.\n- websockets 15.0.1 + pkg2: '4 passed'.\n- websockets 14.1 + pkg2: '4 passed'.\n- websockets 14.1 + repo: the ws tests pass and the HTTP tests fail. On 14.x the ws path is not affected.\n\nE2E probe /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/R21-fixsafety/e2e_probe.py with real Chromium:\n- repo: failed.\n- pkg on ws 14.1: OK.\n- pkg on ws 15.0.1 (py3.12): failed at websocket CONNECT.\n- pkg2 on ws 14.1 and ws 15.0.1: OK.\n\nExisting repo tests on pkg2, run from a scratch copy with ZENDRIVER_TEST_BROWSERS=headless (test_browser, test_connection, test_multiple_browsers, test_proxy): '28 passed'.",
              "refinements": "1. The trigger is narrower than 'behind a proxy'. urllib uses only http_proxy/HTTP_PROXY for the http:// DevTools URL; https_proxy and all_proxy are ignored (urllib registers all_open but never calls it for http). HTTP_PROXY is also ignored when REQUEST_METHOD is set. So on websockets 14.x, an environment with only https_proxy is not affected.\n2. On websockets>=15, the CDP websocket picks the first of ws, socks, https, http from the environment via websockets.uri.get_proxy. A very common setup with only https_proxy therefore breaks start() through Connection.aopen, even with the HTTPApi fix. websockets 15.0.1 is what a fresh `pip install zendriver` picks; uv.lock pins 14.1, so CI does not see it. A full fix also needs proxy=None there.\n3. Test-isolation gotcha for whoever lands the test: urlopen builds its global opener once, on its first call in the process, and keeps the environment's proxies from that moment. A regression test in the real suite would silently pass on unfixed code if an earlier test had already called urlopen. My test sets monkeypatch.setattr(urllib.request, '_opener', None) to prevent that. The fix itself does not depend on this.\n4. Separate bug, not R21: when start() fails at the websocket stage (seen with websockets 15 and a proxy), Browser.start raises without stopping the browser. The Chromium process tree and its /tmp/uc_* profile leak; I had to kill the process group I started. This probably belongs to whichever group owns start-failure cleanup.\n5. Chromium also sends its own traffic (clients2.google.com time check) through the env proxy. That is the browser's own traffic and out of scope.",
              "chrome_claims": [
                {
                  "claim": "With http_proxy set to a proxy that cannot reach loopback and no no_proxy, zendriver.start() on repo code fails with 'Failed to connect to browser' although Chrome is listening",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "e2e_probe.py with real headless Chromium 141.0.7390.37 on py3.13 and websockets 14.1. Failed after 1.4s, and the proxy logged 4x GET http://127.0.0.1:<port>/json/version"
                },
                {
                  "claim": "With the HTTPApi ProxyHandler({}) fix, start() succeeds under the same env on websockets 14.1",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "e2e_probe.py with PYTHONPATH=pkg: 'start OK after 0.3s', and the proxy saw no DevTools request"
                },
                {
                  "claim": "On websockets 15.0.1, the CDP websocket also goes through the env proxy, so the HTTPApi-only fix is not sufficient",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "Throwaway py3.12 venv with websockets 15.0.1 and pkg: start raised InvalidProxyStatus, and the proxy logged 'CONNECT 127.0.0.1:55683'. pkg2 (adds proxy=None) started OK"
                },
                {
                  "claim": "start() failing at the websocket stage leaks the Chrome process",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "After the failed ws15 probe, the chromium process tree and its /tmp/uc_* profile were still alive; I killed only that process group and removed that profile"
                }
              ],
              "realism": "medium",
              "severity": "crash",
              "fix_assessment": {
                "kind": "minimal-local",
                "prototype_dir": "/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/R21-fixsafety/pkg (HTTPApi fix only); /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/R21-fixsafety/pkg2 (HTTPApi fix + websocket proxy=None)",
                "diff_summary": "pkg, browser.py HTTPApi.__init__: add `self._opener = urllib.request.build_opener(urllib.request.ProxyHandler({}))` with a 2-line comment. In HTTPApi._request, replace `urllib.request.urlopen(request, timeout=10)` with `self._opener.open(request, timeout=10)`.\n\npkg2, additionally connection.py at module level after PING_TIMEOUT: `_NO_PROXY: dict[str, Any] = ({\"proxy\": None} if \"proxy\" in inspect.signature(websockets.asyncio.client.connect).parameters else {})`, and `**_NO_PROXY` in the websockets.connect(...) call in Connection.aopen. This is feature detection: {} on 14.1 and {'proxy': None} on 15.0.1, both checked. Passing proxy=None unconditionally raises on 14.1: 'TypeError: BaseEventLoop.create_connection() got an unexpected keyword argument proxy'. Alternative: bump pyproject to websockets>=15 and pass proxy=None directly.",
                "regression_test_path": "/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/R21-fixsafety/test_r21_http_env_proxy.py (companion for websockets>=15: /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/R21-fixsafety/test_r21_ws_env_proxy.py)",
                "fails_on_repo": "yes",
                "passes_on_prototype": "yes",
                "mypy_ok": "yes",
                "risks": "No locks, re-entrancy or cancel-path changes. HTTPApi holds no lock. The opener is per instance and OpenerDirector.open is already used from executor threads by the global urlopen. Error behaviour is unchanged: build_opener keeps HTTPErrorProcessor/HTTPDefaultErrorHandler, so HTTPError and URLError are raised exactly as before and test_connection still catches them.\n\nBehaviour changes callers could notice:\n(a) Code that relied on urllib.request.install_opener(...) to affect zendriver's DevTools requests is no longer honoured. Negligible.\n(b) A user who reaches a remote browser (Browser host != loopback) only through an HTTP proxy loses proxied discovery. On websockets 14.x the CDP socket never used a proxy, so that setup could not work end to end anyway. With pkg2 on websockets>=15, it would also stop proxying the ws. If that matters, apply the bypass only for loopback hosts, or keep honouring an explicit no-bypass option. I consider unconditional direct connection the right default for a DevTools endpoint.\n\nmypy: pkg and the final pkg2 produce output identical to an untouched copy (332 pre-existing errors, all outside core/). A first pkg2 variant that read websockets.__version__ added a new attr-defined error ('does not explicitly export __version__'); the inspect.signature feature detection avoids it. ruff check and format pass on both changed files. Existing browser tests on pkg2: 28 passed.\n\nThe new test must reset urllib.request._opener (a private attribute, via monkeypatch). Otherwise it can pass on unfixed code when an earlier test in the session already called urlopen."
              }
            }
          }
        ]
      },
