        "lenses": [
          {
            "lens": "reproduce",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "R22",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "All three members reproduce with my own scripts, both against a fake CDP peer and against real headless Chromium 141.0.7390.37. A1.5: a successful session reply that is read in the same burst as Target.detachedFromTarget gets thrown away, and the caller gets ProtocolException('target session was detached'). This only happens on Python 3.12 and later; 3.11 is fine. A1.6: cancelling or timing out a fresh tab's first command while _attach_session is waiting for the Target.attachToTarget reply leaves a session attached in Chrome that is never detached, one per cancel. This happens on 3.11 and on 3.13. B14.7: if detachedFromTarget is read in the same burst as the attach reply, it is dropped because the session is not registered yet. _attach_session then registers the dead session, and that send and every later send on the tab hang forever. Chrome answers commands on the dead session with an error that has no sessionId, and the browser listener drops it. This only happens on Python 3.12 and later. On real Chrome, A1.5 and B14.7 both trigger 100% of the time when the event loop is busy for 200 ms right after the writes, and 0% when it is idle.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "A1.5",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Fake peer, py3.13 and py3.12: reply and detach in one burst gave 'peer sent successful evaluate replies: 20; caller success=0 ProtocolException=20 example=target session was detached'. A 50 ms gap gave success=20. On py3.11 the same burst gave success=20, so this needs 3.12 or later. Real Chromium 141 on py3.13, 6 trials per cell: Page.close sent through the tab's own session gave ok x6 with an idle loop and 'PE:target session was detached' x6 with the loop blocked 200 ms. Runtime.evaluate racing a browser-level Target.closeTarget gave the same split (ok x6 / PE x6). Raw frames read by the browser listener show that Chrome sent the successful reply first, then Inspector.detached, then Target.detachedFromTarget. For example: {\"id\": 105, \"result\": {\"result\": {\"type\": \"number\", \"value\": 2...}}, \"sessionId\": \"45BF...\"}. Mechanism, read fresh: Connection._detach_session sets self._inbox = None and fails the pending txs, which drops a reply already queued by Connection._receive_message's put_nowait."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A1.6",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Real Chromium 141, on both py3.13 and py3.11. Tab from browser.get(new_tab=True) (tab.session_id None, Chrome attached=False). I ran asyncio.wait_for(tab.evaluate('1+1'), 1e-6) 3 times, plus 2 deterministic variants: ensure_future, then sleep(0), then cancel. Every one left tab.session_id None, but Chrome emitted Target.attachedToTarget for 5 sessions. A later evaluate attached a 6th session. tab.aclose() detached only the 6th, so 'orphan sessions (attached, never detached): [5 ids]', and Target.getTargetInfo(...).attached was still True after tab.aclose(). An explicit Target.detachFromTarget on each orphan succeeded, after which attached was False. This proves Chrome still held them. Cause: in Connection._attach_session, the cancel lands on 'await browser_connection.send(attach_to_target)'. The tx is cancelled, the later reply is skipped because tx.done(), and the sessionId is never recorded."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "B14.7",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Fake peer, py3.13 and py3.12: the peer sent the attach reply and then detachedFromTarget(S1) with no yield in between. Result: 'first send: HANG ... tab.session_id=S1 bc._sessions=[S1] bc.mapper=[] tab.mapper=[2]'. The peer's error reply {id:2, error:-32001 'Session with given id not found.'} carried no sessionId. The stuck task's stack is at Connection.send 'return await tx'. The second send also hangs (tab.mapper=[2,3]). With a 0.2 s delay both sends succeed. On py3.11 the burst case correctly raises 'target session was detached', so this needs 3.12 or later. Real Chromium 141, py3.13: a raw second client confirmed Chrome answers an unknown session with {\"id\":1,\"error\":{\"code\":-32001,\"message\":\"Session with given id not found.\"}} and no sessionId. I then ran a fresh tab's first tab.send(evaluate) racing a browser-level Target.closeTarget. With an idle loop: 8/8 ProtocolException('target session was detached'), which is correct. With the loop blocked 200 ms: 8/8 HANG, with tab.session_id set and the dead id registered in browser.connection._sessions (they pile up). The stuck stack is at Connection.send -> self._prepare_headless() waiting on Network.setUserAgentOverride (headless). A later send on the same tab also hung (3 s probe)."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "All scripts are my own, in /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/R22-reproduce/. fakepeer.py is an in-process websockets server: attach gives a unique S<n>; dead sessions are answered Chrome-style with no sessionId; detach can go in the same burst or after a delay. make_tab builds a Connection whose _owner.connection is the browser Connection. Commands were run from that dir under timeout 120/300, with PATH=<scratchpad>/bin:$PATH for Chromium.\n(1) /home/user/zendriver/.venv/bin/python repro_a15.py (py3.13.16, zendriver from /home/user/zendriver/zendriver/__init__.py) printed 'delay=0.0: peer sent successful evaluate replies: 20; caller success=0 ProtocolException=20 ... example='target session was detached'' and 'delay=0.05: ... caller success=20 ProtocolException=0'. On py3.12.3, using a scratch venv and PYTHONPATH copy, zendriver.__file__ printed from the copy, the output was the same. On py3.11.17 both cases gave success=20.\n(2) repro_b147.py on py3.13 printed 'first send: HANG (not done after 3s) tab.session_id=S1 bc._sessions=[SessionID('S1')] bc.mapper=[] tab.mapper=[2]'. The last peer frames were {'id': 1, 'result': {'sessionId': 'S1'}}, {'method': 'Target.detachedFromTarget', ...S1}, {'id': 2, 'error': {'code': -32001, ...}}. The stack was 'File .../connection.py, line 662, in send: return await tx'. Then 'second send: HANG ... tab.mapper=[2, 3]'. delay=0.2 gave 'first send: success / second send: success'. py3.12 was the same. py3.11 gave 'first send: ProtocolException 'target session was detached''.\n(3) repro_a16.py on real Chromium (py3.13 and py3.11) printed 'new tab session_id: None chrome attached flag before: False', then 'wait_for(1e-06): TimeoutError; tab.session_id after = None' x3 and 'cancel #N: CancelledError; tab.session_id after = None' x2, then 'orphan sessions (attached, never detached): [5 SessionIDs]', 'chrome attached flag after tab.aclose(): True', 'explicit Target.detachFromTarget(...): succeeded' x5 and 'chrome attached flag after explicit detach of orphans: False'.\n(4) repro_b147_real.py printed '(a) chrome reply to unknown session: {\"id\":1,\"error\":{\"code\":-32001,\"message\":\"Session with given id not found.\"}}', then '(b) block=0: results=[ProtocolException:target session was detached x8]' and '(b) block=0.2: results=['hang' x8]'. Each hang showed a stack at 'connection.py line 641, in send: await self._prepare_headless()' (pending Network.setUserAgentOverride) and 'later send on the same tab: HANG'.\n(5) repro_a15_real.py printed 'mode=page.close block=0: ok x6', 'block=0.2: PE:target session was detached x6', and the same for mode=evaluate+closeTarget. The raw frames show the successful reply before Target.detachedFromTarget. All browsers were stopped with browser.stop(), I checked that no repro process remained, the scratch venvs and package copy were removed, and git status in the repo is clean.",
              "refinements": "1) A1.5 and B14.7 depend on the Python version. Both reproduce on 3.12 and 3.13 but not on 3.11, where asyncio.wait_for wraps each recv in a task, so the session listener and _attach_session get to run between frames. A1.6 does not depend on the version.\n2) On real Chrome the trigger is ordinary: the event loop is busy for about 200 ms (sync CPU work, time.sleep, a blocking handler) while the target is going away, so Chrome's frames pile up and are read in one burst. With an idle loop, Chrome's frames came in separate reads and zendriver behaved correctly in all trials.\n3) The B14.7 hang has two causes that work together. (a) The detach is dropped because the session is not registered yet. (b) Error replies without a sessionId for a command sent on a session go to the browser listener. There, 'message[\"id\"] in self.connection.mapper' misses, so the reply is silently dropped, even though _next_message_id says ids are shared so such replies could be matched. Fixing (b), by looking the id up in the session mappers, would turn this hang and similar ones into a clear 'Session with given id not found' error. The dead sessions also stay in browser.connection._sessions.\n4) In the headless config the first stuck await is in _prepare_headless (_send_oneshot), not the user's command.\n5) Separate from this group: after a detach, Connection.send can create a new Listener for an already detached connection (send checks the listener again after _prepare_headless, whose _send_oneshot swallows ProtocolException). That listener then dies with 'Task exception was never retrieved ... ValueError(no websocket connection)' from _receive_message. I saw this in the block=0 real-Chrome runs and the 3.11 runs.\n6) A1.6: the leak lasts as long as the browser websocket (my assumption, not tested). The next send attaches a new session and works.",
              "chrome_claims": [
                {
                  "claim": "Chrome answers a command sent with an unknown or dead sessionId with an error reply that has no sessionId field",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "A raw websockets client on the browser ws of Chromium 141.0.7390.37 received {\"id\":1,\"error\":{\"code\":-32001,\"message\":\"Session with given id not found.\"}}"
                },
                {
                  "claim": "For a session command that makes the target go away (Page.close through its own session, or evaluate racing Target.closeTarget), Chrome sends the successful reply with sessionId before Target.detachedFromTarget",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "I wrapped browser.connection.websocket.recv to log frames. The order was reply {id, result, sessionId}, then Inspector.detached, then Target.detachedFromTarget"
                },
                {
                  "claim": "A session created by Target.attachToTarget stays attached in Chrome when the client cancelled before reading the reply, and is not removed by tab.aclose()",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "Target.getTargetInfo(...).attached was True after tab.aclose(). An explicit Target.detachFromTarget on each orphan id (taken from Target.attachedToTarget events) succeeded, after which attached was False"
                },
                {
                  "claim": "When attachToTarget races a closeTarget, Chrome sends the attach reply and then Target.detachedFromTarget for the new session",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "Indirect: in the real-Chrome run with the loop blocked, the tab ended up registered with a session id that Chrome then answered with 'Session with given id not found', and the idle-loop runs got 'target session was detached'. I did not capture raw frames for this run."
                },
                {
                  "claim": "Orphaned sessions are released only when the browser websocket closes",
                  "status": "assumed",
                  "how": "Not tested directly. I only showed they survive tab.aclose() and go away when explicitly detached"
                }
              ],
              "realism": "medium",
              "severity": "hang",
              "fix_assessment": {
                "kind": "minimal-local",
                "prototype_dir": "",
                "diff_summary": "",
                "regression_test_path": "",
                "fails_on_repo": "not-run",
