          {
            "lens": "refute",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "R22",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "I could not refute any of the three members. Each one reproduced against real headless Chromium 141 through zendriver's public API. A1.5 and B14.7 need Python 3.12 or later: they reproduced on 3.12.3 and 3.13.16 and never on 3.11.17. A1.6 does not depend on the Python version. Both 3.12-only races need a structural condition. On 3.12+, asyncio.wait_for runs the coroutine inline, and websockets 14.1 Assembler.get returns buffered frames without suspending. So when the reply and the Target.detachedFromTarget frame are read in the same chunk, the browser Listener handles both in one task step. In A1.5 that step discards the reply already queued in session._inbox. In B14.7 it drops the detach, because the session is not yet in _sessions. For page targets Chrome sends the two frames 4-6 ms apart, so they only coalesce when the event loop is busy. For OOPIF removal the gap can be as small as 10 us, so they can coalesce even on an idle loop. A1.6 is a real orphaned Chrome session, but it is bounded: Chrome detaches it when the target closes or the browser websocket closes. 'Never detached' overstates it.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "A1.5",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Reproduced on real Chromium. The test command was tab.send(cdp.page.close()) on the tab's own session, with a sibling task doing 10 ms CPU chunks. The wire log shows 'reply-ok,detach' for the session, yet the caller got ProtocolException('target session was detached') in 8/8 and 20/20 runs (p7b, p7). Rates by load: 2 ms chunks gave 1/20, an idle loop 0/20, and 10 ms chunks on Py 3.11 0/20. A command in flight on an attached OOPIF while its iframe was removed failed 1/60 on an idle loop on 3.13, after Chrome had replied value 42 (p5), and 0/60 on 3.11. Mechanism (Connection._detach_session called from _receive_message): it sets _inbox=None, cancels the session Listener and fails the pending tx. Practical harm is mostly limited to commands that end their own session, such as Page.close or detachFromTarget on the same session. For unrelated in-flight commands the target is going away, and the same exception is the legitimate result in most races (52/60). Tab.close() already avoids this by sending close_target through the browser session (commit e81b66c)."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A1.6",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Reproduced on real Chromium with Py 3.13 and 3.11 (p6). The tab came from browser.get(new_tab=True), so it was not attached. I cancelled the first evaluate while Target.attachToTarget was in browser_connection.mapper. Afterwards tab.session_id was None, and the next send attached a second session. tab.aclose() detached only that second one. The orphan session still answered a raw Runtime.evaluate with result 42 and its sessionId. Chrome detached it only when tab.close() closed the target, so the leak lasts as long as the target or the websocket, not forever. The window is the attach round trip of the first send on an unattached tab. First sends took 3-9 ms in total, including _prepare_headless. Internal timeouts (Tab.get and wait_for_ready_state, 10 s) practically never land in that window, so this needs a user-level short timeout or cancellation. I saw no functional zendriver impact. Low realism and low impact."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "B14.7",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Reproduced on real Chromium with Py 3.13 (p4/p4b). I sent the first command to an unattached OOPIF Tab right as the parent removed the iframe. It hung in 2/30, 4/60 and 1/120 trials, and 0/60 on Py 3.11. Wire evidence: the attach reply was frame 195 and detachedFromTarget for the same session was frame 197. Both were handled before _attach_session registered the session. Chrome then answered the stuck command with {'id':80,'error':{'code':-32001,'message':'Session with given id not found.'}}, which has no sessionId. The browser listener dropped it because that id is not in the browser mapper. A second send on the same tab also hung. Only browser.stop() released the two stuck tasks, with ProtocolException('target session was detached'). The websocket keepalive does not help because the connection stays healthy. Through the public API: tab.select('#nope', include_frames=True, timeout=1) on a page that replaces a cross-site iframe every 120 ms, with a CPU-busy sibling task (10 ms chunks), stayed stuck for more than 15 s in 3/20 calls (p8c). The stack showed Tab.select, then _query_selector_all_with_frames, waiting on Network.setUserAgentOverride from _prepare_headless. On an idle loop it hung in 0/90 select calls (p8b)."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "All probes are in /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/R22-refute/. Each prints zendriver.__file__, which was /home/user/zendriver/zendriver/__init__.py in every run.\n- p1_chrome_raw.py: on a raw second CDP client, a bogus sessionId and a detached sessionId both got replies with no sessionId. attachToTarget with flatten emits Target.attachedToTarget. Page.close via the session gave the reply first and the detach 4.6-6.2 ms later (5/5).\n- p2_chrome_attach_gap.py, p2b_sweep.py: attaching to a closing page gave a success reply and then a detach 3.8-7.8 ms later. With a 1 ms or longer gap after closeTarget, the attach failed with 'No target with given id found'.\n- p3_oopif_gap.py: attaching to an OOPIF while its iframe was removed gave reply-to-detach gaps of 0.01-1.6 ms.\n- p4_zd_oopif_attach_race.py, p4b_hang_details.py (B14.7): on 3.13, HANG 2/30, 4/60 and 1/120; on 3.11, 0/60. Includes the wire indexes, Chrome's error without sessionId, the second send hanging and browser.stop() releasing both tasks.\n- p5_zd_oopif_reply_detach.py (A1.5 OOPIF): on 3.13, 1/60 calls failed after a success reply; on 3.11, 0/60.\n- p7_zd_page_close.py, p7b_page_close_wire.py (A1.5 Page.close): with 10 ms busy chunks, 3.13 failed 20/20 and 8/8 with wire order reply-ok,detach. 3.12 failed 20/20. 3.11 failed 0/20. With 2 ms chunks, 1/20. Idle, 0/20.\n- p6_zd_cancel_attach.py (A1.6): the orphan session stays alive after tab.aclose() and answers a raw command; Chrome detaches it only on tab.close(). Same result on 3.11 and 3.13.\n- p8b_select_churn_count.py, p8c_select_churn_busy.py: select(include_frames=True, timeout=1) on a page with a churning cross-site iframe. Idle: 0/90 stuck across periods of 15, 50 and 120 ms. With a CPU-busy loop: 3/20 stuck for more than 15 s.\nCode (read fresh): Connection._attach_session (~L460) registers browser_connection._sessions[session_id] only after awaiting the attach reply. _receive_message (~L826) ignores detachedFromTarget for an unregistered sessionId, and routes a reply without sessionId only to the browser mapper. Listener.listener_loop (~L922) handles a reply only if its id is in self.connection.mapper. _detach_session (~L481) drops _inbox and fails pending transactions. Git history: everything comes from e81b66c (#280), where the Tab.close comment shows the reply-versus-detach race was known for close_target only. No test asserts the racy behaviour; tests/core/test_frames.py only checks session_id is None after close.",
              "refinements": "1) A1.5 and B14.7 require Python 3.12 or later. On 3.11, wait_for wraps the read in a task, so the session listener or the _attach_session continuation runs before the next read. They also require the reply and the detach to be parsed from the same socket read. That happens readily for OOPIF teardown, where the gap is 1 ms or less. For page targets the gap is about 4-6 ms, so the event loop must be blocked for at least that long when the reply arrives. 2) B14.7 only hangs if the target dies within about 1 ms after Chrome processed the attach. A slightly later destruction makes the attach fail cleanly with 'No target with given id found'. The hang ends only when the browser websocket closes, through browser.stop() or a connection drop. It defeats select/find(include_frames=True) timeouts, because those check elapsed time between iterations rather than wrapping each send. In headless mode the stuck send is the Network.setUserAgentOverride from _prepare_headless. 3) A1.5 matters mainly for commands that end their own session, such as Page.close or Target.detachFromTarget sent on the same session. For other in-flight commands on a dying target, the exception is usually what the caller would have got anyway. 4) A1.6 is low impact. Only one orphan leaks per attach attempt that is cancelled during the attach round trip, and Chrome reaps it when the target closes or the websocket closes. 5) Side observation outside this group: probes repeatedly logged 'Task exception was never retrieved ... ValueError(\"no websocket connection\")' from Listener tasks. Connection.send recreates a Listener after aopen and _prepare_headless even if the session has just been detached, and that Listener's _receive_message raises with websocket=None.",
              "chrome_claims": [
                {
                  "claim": "Chrome answers a command sent with an unknown or already-detached sessionId with an error that carries no sessionId field: {'id':N,'error':{'code':-32001,'message':'Session with given id not found.'}}",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "p1_chrome_raw.py, raw second websocket client on headless Chromium 141; also captured on zendriver's own wire in p4b for the stuck command"
                },
                {
                  "claim": "Page.close sent on a page's flat session: Chrome sends the success reply first and Target.detachedFromTarget 4-6 ms later",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "p1_chrome_raw.py, 5/5 trials"
                },
                {
                  "claim": "attachToTarget on a page that is being closed succeeds, followed by a detach 3.8-7.8 ms later; once closeTarget has been processed about 1 ms or more earlier, the attach fails with -32602 'No target with given id found'",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "p2_chrome_attach_gap.py and p2b_sweep.py on headless Chromium"
                },
                {
                  "claim": "For an OOPIF whose iframe is removed by the parent, the attachToTarget reply and Target.detachedFromTarget can arrive 10 us to 1.6 ms apart (effectively in the same read)",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "p3_oopif_gap.py with a cross-site iframe (127.0.0.1 embedding localhost)"
                },
                {
                  "claim": "A flatten attachToTarget emits Target.attachedToTarget on the browser session, and a session whose attach reply zendriver dropped stays alive in Chrome until the target closes",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "p6_zd_cancel_attach.py: the orphan answered a raw Runtime.evaluate with value 42 after tab.aclose(); it showed up in the detached list only after tab.close()"
                },
                {
                  "claim": "An idle orphan session (no domains enabled) has no observable side effects beyond Chrome-side resources and TargetInfo.attached",
                  "status": "assumed",
                  "how": "not measured; no events from the orphan were seen in the probes, but I did not look for subtler effects"
                }
              ],
              "realism": "medium",
              "severity": "hang",
              "fix_assessment": {
                "kind": "minimal-local",
                "prototype_dir": "",
                "diff_summary": "",
                "regression_test_path": "",
                "fails_on_repo": "not-run",
                "passes_on_prototype": "not-run",
                "mypy_ok": "not-run",
                "risks": "Not assessed (refute lens). Possible fixes: (a) in _receive_message, remember session ids that were detached but not registered, and have _attach_session raise for them; or register the session from a done-callback on the attach transaction; (b) deliver the detach through the session inbox, or drain it before failing pending transactions, so queued replies resolve first; (c) route a reply without sessionId whose id belongs to a session's mapper to that session (ids are already shared per websocket), which turns any remaining B14.7 case into an error instead of a hang; (d) on cancellation of the attach, add a done-callback that records the session id or detaches it."
              }
            }
          },
          {
            "lens": "fixsafety",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "R22",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "All three ordering bugs in zendriver/core/connection.py reproduce on the repo code. A1.5: a session reply that arrives in the same burst as Target.detachedFromTarget is dropped by Connection._detach_session, which sets self._inbox = None and then fails the tx. B14.7: Connection._attach_session registers the session only after its awaited send() resumes. If the detach arrives in the same burst as the attach reply, the browser listener sees an unknown session and ignores the detach. Every later send on the tab then hangs at Connection.send 'return await tx'. A1.5 and B14.7 depend on Python >= 3.12, where wait_for awaits inline and websockets returns buffered frames without suspending. Both reproduce on 3.12.3 and 3.13.16 and not on 3.11.17. A1.6 does not depend on the Python version and reproduces on real Chromium: a cancelled first command leaves the attach tx cancelled, so the session Chrome creates is never recorded or detached. A small local fix in connection.py fixes all three, and a variant of A1.6 (aclose while the attach is pending) as well.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "A1.5",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Fake peer writes the Runtime.evaluate reply (with sessionId) and Target.detachedFromTarget in one transport write. On the repo (py3.13), tab.send raises ProtocolException('target session was detached'). The same peer with a 50ms gap returns the RemoteObject value=2. On py3.11 it does not reproduce (the test passes on untouched code). Not hit on real Chromium either: 15/15 page.close sent through the tab's own session succeeded. The fix is to drain the queued inbox replies in _detach_session before _fail_pending_transactions. That alone makes the test pass."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A1.6",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Fake peer with the attach reply delayed 0.3s and the first send under wait_for(…, 0.1): attached [S1, S2], detached [S2], so S1 is orphaned. The same happens on py3.11, 3.12 and 3.13. On real Chromium (repo), first-evaluate timeouts of 0.001s and 0.003s gave 'sessions attached=2 orphaned=1', the prototype 0 orphans. Two more variants fail on the repo too: four concurrent first sends with the first one cancelled (S1 orphaned), and tab.aclose() before the late attach reply (S1 never detached). The leaked Chrome session is idle and lasts until the target closes or the browser websocket closes."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "B14.7",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Fake peer writes the attach reply and Target.detachedFromTarget(S1) in one write, and answers unknown sessions without a sessionId the way Chrome does. On the repo (py3.13), the send is still pending after 2s with session_id=S1, sessions=[S1], tab.mapper=[2], browser.mapper=[]. print_stack shows it stuck at connection.py:662 in Connection.send 'return await tx', and the second send hangs too. With a 50ms gap: ProtocolException('target session was detached'). Verified on real Chromium that an unknown-session error carries no sessionId. 15 close_target-vs-first-send races on real Chromium did not hit the hang: all 15 raised ProtocolException('target session was detached')."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "Scratch dir: /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/R22-fixsafety/. The regression tests in tests/test_r22_session_attach.py use an in-process websockets fake peer that writes frame bursts with ws.protocol.send_text + ws.send_data().\n\nRepo, no PYTHONPATH, py3.13.16, 'zendriver imported from /home/user/zendriver/zendriver/__init__.py':\n- FAILED test_reply_in_same_burst_as_detach_is_delivered (ProtocolException: target session was detached)\n- FAILED test_detach_in_same_burst_as_attach_reply_does_not_hang (TimeoutError: hang)\n- FAILED test_cancelled_first_command_does_not_orphan_a_session (['S1','S2'] != ['S2'])\n- FAILED test_aclose_while_attach_pending_does_not_orphan_a_session\n- '4 failed in 4.61s'\n\nPrototype, PYTHONPATH=pkg, 'zendriver imported from …/R22-fixsafety/pkg/zendriver/__init__.py': '8 passed' (the 4 regression tests plus the 4 edge tests in tests/test_r22_edges.py) on py3.13.16, 3.12.3 and 3.11.17.\n\nUntouched copy: py3.12.3 gives 3 failed (the first three tests; the aclose test did not exist yet). py3.11.17 gives '1 failed, 2 passed': only A1.6 fails, so A1.5 and B14.7 need Python 3.12+.\n\nFix parts checked separately:\n- Drain-only variant: A1.5 passes; B14.7 and A1.6 fail.\n- Generator registration without the shield: A1.5 and B14.7 pass; A1.6 fails.\n\nControls (probe_controls.py, repo, py3.13):\n- A1.5 burst: ProtocolException; with a 50ms gap: success.\n- B14.7 burst: hang, stack at connection.py:662 Connection.send 'return await tx'; with a 50ms gap: ProtocolException.\n\nReal Chromium (probe_real_chrome.py):\n- An unknown session returns {\"id\":1,\"error\":{\"code\":-32001,\"message\":\"Session with given id not found.\"}}.\n- Message order for an explicit attach: ['Target.attachedToTarget', 'reply id=3'].\n- A1.6, repo: orphaned=1 at timeouts of 0.001s and 0.003s. Prototype: orphaned=0 at every timeout.\n\nRepo suite tests/core -m 'not external' against the prototype with real headless Chromium: '104 passed, 1 skipped, 4 deselected', and printed ZENDRIVER USED: …/pkg/zendriver/__init__.py.\n\nmypy (cd /home/user/zendriver && uv run --no-sync mypy --config-file pyproject.toml <copy>/zendriver): path-normalized output for the prototype and an untouched copy is identical, so there are no new errors. A first draft used mapper.pop(message.get('id')) and added one arg-type error; it was rewritten to avoid that.",
              "refinements": "1. Python version matters. A1.5 and B14.7 show only on Python >= 3.12: they reproduce on 3.12.3 and 3.13.16, and the same test passes on untouched code under 3.11.17. A1.6 shows on every version.\n\n2. Real-Chrome realism differs by member:\n- A1.6 is deterministic with small wait_for timeouts on the first command of a fresh tab (browser.get(new_tab=True) does not attach).\n- A1.5 and B14.7 need Chrome to coalesce two frames into one read. 15 real trials of each did not hit it, so treat them as load-dependent.\n- Triage called the fix 'register before the reply'. Registering before the reply is impossible because the session id is unknown until then. What works is registering while the reply is parsed. Wrapping cdp.target.attach_to_target in a generator does that: Transaction.__call__ resumes the generator synchronously inside the browser listener, before it reads the next frame.\n\n3. A1.6 has two variants the finder did not list, both failing on the repo:\n- concurrent first sends where the first is cancelled, which orphans S1;\n- tab.aclose() before the late attach reply.\nShielding the attach alone would have resurrected an aclosed tab: session_id=S1, closed=False and S1 not detached. The prototype therefore also makes aclose await a pending attach.\n\n4. Adjacent pre-existing issue, not fixed here: send() and aopen() recreate a Listener for a session that was detached during the send, for example after _prepare_headless. That listener dies with ValueError('no websocket connection') and logs 'Task exception was never retrieved'. In the real-Chrome race loop this happened 15/15 times on both repo and prototype. The prototype adds a 'session_id is None -> raise ProtocolException(\"target session was detached\")' check after the attach so the B14.7 path does not trigger it.",
