              "realism": "medium",
              "severity": "hang",
              "fix_assessment": {
                "kind": "minimal-local",
                "prototype_dir": "/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/R22-fixsafety/pkg",
                "diff_summary": "Diff saved at /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/R22-fixsafety/prototype.diff. All changes are in zendriver/core/connection.py:\n(1) B14.7: Connection._attach_session sends a new generator Connection._attach_to_target(browser_connection, target_id). It does 'session_id = yield from cdp.target.attach_to_target(...)' and then registers session_id, _session_parent, _inbox, browser_connection._sessions[sid], websocket and Listener. That code runs inside Transaction.__call__ in the browser listener, before the listener reads the next frame. After the attach, _attach_session raises ProtocolException('target session was detached') if session_id is None again.\n(2) A1.6: the attach send runs as asyncio.ensure_future, stored in a new self._attaching field and awaited through asyncio.shield. A cancelled caller no longer cancels the attach tx, and a later caller awaits the same attach instead of starting a second one. aclose() first awaits a pending _attaching (shielded, exceptions ignored), so the late session is detached rather than resurrected.\n(3) A1.5: _detach_session keeps a reference to the old inbox and, before _fail_pending_transactions, delivers every queued reply whose id is in self.mapper. It calls tx(**message), and a parse error becomes tx.set_exception. Queued events are still dropped.\nNo lock is added or reordered.",
                "regression_test_path": "/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/R22-fixsafety/tests/test_r22_session_attach.py",
                "fails_on_repo": "yes",
                "passes_on_prototype": "yes",
                "mypy_ok": "yes",
                "risks": "- Session registration now runs inside Transaction.__call__ in the browser listener. Any exception there would escape tx(**message) and kill the browser listener. The body is trivial (Queue(), dict assignment, create_task), but a real PR should keep it that small.\n- A cancelled caller no longer cancels the attach. If Chrome never answers, later callers and aclose() wait on the same pending attach until the browser connection closes and the listener fails it. The repo behaved the same way, because each retry re-sent an attach that also hung. aclose already awaits a detachFromTarget reply, so the exposure matches.\n- Browser.stop calls aclose() on each target, so it can wait for a pending attach. That is bounded by the reply or by the connection closing.\n- asyncio.Lock is not re-entered: _attach_to_target takes no lock. The only lock order is the tab's _attach_lock, then the browser's _open_lock, unchanged.\n- shield marks the inner exception as retrieved when the outer is cancelled. No 'exception never retrieved' warnings appeared in any run.\n- Behaviour changes a caller would notice:\n  - A command whose reply arrives with the detach now returns its result instead of raising.\n  - A send to a target detached during the attach now raises 'target session was detached' instead of hanging.\n  - A cancelled first command no longer causes a second attach.\n- On Python <= 3.11 there is a theoretical window where wait_for's inner task has already dequeued a reply when the detach runs, and the drain does not cover it. It was not observed: the test passes on 3.11 both before and after the fix. Delivering the detach through the inbox would cover it, at the cost of depending on the session listener running.\n- The pre-existing stray-Listener ValueError noise after a detach during send() is not addressed."
              }
            }
          }
