          {
            "lens": "refute",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "R23",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "I tried to refute all five members and could not; each one reproduced against real headless Chromium 141 on Python 3.13. (1) After stop(), start() never launches Chrome. On the first run, Browser.start set self.config.host='127.0.0.1' and config.port=free_port(), so the second call sets connect_existing=True. It then waits about 2.5 s on test_connection. The stale self.info skips the 'Failed to connect' check, and the call raises ConnectionRefusedError against the dead port. (2) After Chrome dies, start() takes its dedicated branch `return await self.create(config=self.config)`. The new instance inherits the dead host/port, also skips the launch, and raises the misleading 'Failed to connect to browser ... pass sandbox=False' error. (3) Even with host/port reset by hand, the stale Tab in self.targets makes browser.get() fail with 'No target with given id found'. (4) A Browser connected via host/port reports stopped=True while it is live, so _atexit_cleanup skips _stop. (5) A second start() on a live connected browser replaces self.connection without closing the old one. The old websocket and its Listener keep running. Arguments against the bug's practical weight: the library itself never calls start() on an existing instance (create() is the only caller). Docs, README and tests never restart a Browser object; tests/conftest.py creates a new browser when stopped is True. Every restart failure is loud: an exception after about 2.5 s, no hang and no silent wrong result. Workaround: call zd.start() again. The atexit skip for connected browsers has almost no visible effect at exit, because asyncio.run cancels the listener task and the process exit closes the sockets. It even leaves the externally launched Chrome alive, which is arguably what a user who attached to it wants (explicit stop() closes it). Against that, the crash-recovery branch in start() shows the authors meant restart to work, and that branch can never succeed. So the bug is real, but its realism is low.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "A3.2",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Reproduced (p_restart.py). After stop(): host/port 127.0.0.1:42329 kept, info stale=True, 1 target kept, no chrome child. start() raised ConnectionRefusedError(111, 127.0.0.1:42329) after 2.52 s, no process launched. Crash path (kill + wait, then start()) raised 'Failed to connect to browser' after 2.51 s, with no new pid. With host/port reset by hand (p_restart_reset.py), the restart launched Chrome but tabs[0] was the stale target and browser.get() raised ProtocolException 'No target with given id found' (Target.attachToTarget); get(new_tab=True) worked. The atexit re-registration sub-claim holds by reading (stop() unregisters, only create() registers) but is moot today because restart fails earlier."
                },
