                },
                {
                  "fid": "A3.10",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Reproduced (p_remote.py) with a manually launched chromium and zd.start(host, port). stopped=True right away. A second start() gave no warning and replaced the connection: old.closed=False and the old listener kept running. The browser stayed usable. The leak is bounded: once the remote browser goes away the old listener stops (after stop() sent Browser.close: listener running=False, though old.websocket was still set). It triggers only when user code calls start() again on a live connection. For launched browsers the guard warns and no-ops, so this is a missing idempotency guard on a misuse path. Low realism; one extra websocket plus a listener task per extra call."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "B16.13",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Reproduced: a live connected browser (1 tab, evaluate works) reports stopped=True. In p_atexit.py, leaving the connected browser running at the end of asyncio.run showed that _atexit_cleanup never calls _stop (an instance-patched tracer was never hit). Practical harm at exit is negligible: the listener was already cancelled by asyncio.run's task cancellation, -X dev showed no ResourceWarning, and the external Chrome stayed alive, which arguably is desirable. The real impact is user code that relies on browser.stopped for a connected browser (e.g. `while not browser.stopped`). stopped has been process-based since the nodriver-era code (the `if not instance.stopped` atexit check predates commit a51af21), and no test covers connect-existing."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "Scripts are in /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/R23-refute/ (p_restart.py, p_restart_reset.py, p_remote.py, p_atexit.py). All ran under `timeout 120`, with asyncio.wait_for(…, 60) around start(), on /home/user/zendriver/.venv/bin/python 3.13.16 against the repo copy (zendriver.__file__=/home/user/zendriver/zendriver/__init__.py) and real Chromium 141.0.7390.37 headless. Key output: 'after stop: host/port 127.0.0.1 42329 info stale: True targets kept: 1 ... chrome children: []'; 'restart raised after 2.52s: ConnectionRefusedError(111, \"Connect call failed (127.0.0.1, 42329)\")'; 'process launched by restart? None'; 'start after crash raised after 2.51s: Exception(... Failed to connect to browser ...)'; hand-reset: 'tabs[0] stale: True', 'get raised ProtocolException No target with given id found command:Target.attachToTarget', 'get new_tab OK 4'; remote: 'stopped property: True tabs: 1', 'warnings from second start(): []', 'connection replaced: True old still open: True old listener running: True'; atexit: '_stop' tracer never printed, 'listener running: False' after asyncio.run, 'external chrome alive after loop close: True'. Code read fresh: Browser.start (browser.py ~517-541): guard `if self._process or self._process_pid`, crash branch `return await self.create(config=self.config)`, `connect_existing = host is not None and port is not None`, else it writes the auto host/port into self.config. `if not self.info:` (~587) uses info left over from the previous run. Browser.stopped (~325) is `not (self._process and self._process.poll() is None)`. _atexit_cleanup (~234) does `if not self.stopped: await self._stop()`. _stop (~874) never resets config.host/port, info, targets or connection. History is squashed: the logic is first seen in 5c1a005 (the import of the nodriver-derived code), and the atexit `if not instance.stopped` check predates a51af21. No test asserts or exercises restart or connect-existing. util.start documents host+port as 'connect to existing, will not start a local chrome'.",
              "refinements": "Only user code that reuses the same Browser object hits this: `await b.stop(); await b.start()`, or `if b.stopped: await b.start()` after a crash. No library path calls start() twice. The most plausible real trigger is crash recovery in long-running scrapers, the case the dedicated create() branch in start() was written for. Even if that branch worked, it returns a NEW Browser, so callers that ignore start()'s return value would keep using the dead object. Failures are deterministic and loud (an exception after browser_connection_max_tries * browser_connection_timeout, about 2.5 s by default), never a hang. A failed stop->start also re-adds the browser to util.get_registered_instances() (start adds it before the failing send) and leaves self.connection pointing at a never-opened Connection. Assumed, not verified: if another process re-binds the stale port between stop() and start(), the restart would silently attach to that foreign browser, and a later stop() would send it Browser.close. That needs a free_port() collision and is unlikely. A3.10 applies only to connect-existing browsers when start() is called while the old websocket is live; after the remote browser closes, the old listener ends by itself. B16.13's atexit consequence is cosmetic at exit; the meaningful part is the wrong stopped value for user code. Related but outside the claims: start() also overwrites a user-supplied port when host is None (both are reset by the else branch). Fix note: a fix must keep connect-existing semantics for user-supplied host/port (remember whether they were user-provided), clear self.info/targets/connection on _stop, and re-register atexit when start() relaunches.",
              "chrome_claims": [
                {
