          {
            "lens": "fixsafety",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "R23",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "Confirmed on real headless Chromium with Python 3.13. (1) Browser.start() saves the host and port it picks itself (127.0.0.1 plus free_port()) into self.config. Browser._stop() never resets them. So the next start() treats the browser as connect-existing and never launches Chrome. Because self.info is still stale, the 'Failed to connect' check (`if not self.info`) is skipped, and start() fails with ConnectionRefusedError after about 2.5 s. (2) The branch in start() for a dead process, `return await self.create(config=self.config)`, copies the same dead host and port into a new instance, which fails with 'Failed to connect to browser'. (3) If host and port are reset by hand, the old Tab objects are still in self.targets and browser.get() fails with 'No target with given id found'. The restarted browser is also never registered with asyncio_atexit again. (4) For a browser connected through host/port, `stopped` is always True, and a second start() opens a second websocket. The first one stays open with its Listener running, even after stop(). A minimal local fix in browser.py works and is safe. One version of it, the 'already running' guard, would regress reconnecting. Another, clearing targets too early, adds unretrieved StopIteration task exceptions. The final prototype avoids both.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "A3.2",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Repo code: stop() then start() raised ConnectionRefusedError to the old port after 2.52 s, and no process was launched. Kill plus wait, then start(), raised 'Failed to connect to browser' through create(). With host/port reset by hand (probe p9): targets=2, get() raised ProtocolException 'No target with given id found' (tabs[0] stale: True), and _atexit_cleanup was not in the asyncio_atexit callbacks after the restart. Every sub-claim reproduced."
                },
