                },
                {
                  "fid": "B16.13",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "stopped is True for a live browser connected through host/port: 1 tab, open connection, on both real Chromium and the fake peer. _atexit_cleanup therefore skips _stop for such browsers. That skip is also what keeps the external browser from being sent Browser.close when the loop exits, so a fix to `stopped` must not silently start closing external browsers at exit. My prototype keeps the atexit behaviour unchanged."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "Probes (mine) are in /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/R23-fixsafety/probes/.\nRepo, p1.py: 'after stop: host/port 127.0.0.1 53127 info stale: True targets: 1 process None' / 'restart raised after 2.52s: ConnectionRefusedError ... ('127.0.0.1', 53127)' / 'start after crash raised after 2.52s: Exception [... Failed to connect to browser ...]'.\nRepo, p2.py (real Chromium on a port): 'connected; stopped: True tabs: 1' / 'connection replaced: True old closed: False old listener running: ... [running: True]' / 'after stop: new closed True old closed False old listener running True'.\nRepo, p9: 'restarted; targets: 2 atexit registered: False' / 'get after restart raised ProtocolException No target with given id found | tabs[0] stale: True'.\nRegression test tests/test_r23_restart.py. On the repo (zendriver imported from /home/user/zendriver/zendriver/__init__.py, Python 3.13.16): '5 failed, 1 passed'. The one pass is the non-regression test for reconnecting after the remote went away and came back. On the prototype (imported from .../R23-fixsafety/pkg/zendriver/__init__.py): '6 passed in 8.59s'.\nRepo suite copy (tests/core + tests/docs, not external, headless) on the prototype: '104 passed, 1 skipped, 13 deselected', the same as the untouched copy. That includes test_browser_is_garbage_collected_after_stop.",
              "refinements": "1) Do not add a 'no-op while connected' guard for host/port browsers (my v1). Connection.closed only becomes True on aclose. After the remote browser dies, stopped stays False, so start() would refuse to reconnect. The repo reconnects in that case, by accident. My non-regression test passes on the repo, fails on v1 and passes on v2/v3. Instead, close the old connection before making the new one, so a second start() reconnects without leaking. 2) Do not clear self.targets right after closing the tabs in _stop (my v2). Events arriving during Browser.close then hit `next(filter(...))` in _handle_target_update and leave 'Task exception was never retrieved: RuntimeError coroutine raised StopIteration'. Seen in 2 of 5 runs of a stop loop on v2, and in 0 of 4 on the repo and 0 of 4 on v3. Clear the targets at the end of _stop. A tolerant `next(..., None)` in _handle_target_update would make this fully robust. 3) This group depends on Q3: if the process died and start() runs before the listener has seen the close (sync kill()+wait() with no yield), the new `await self._stop()` in start() raises ConnectionClosedError from Tab.aclose (detachFromTarget catches only ProtocolException). Calling stop() at that point raises the same way on the repo. A retry succeeds. With a Q3-style `except (ProtocolException, websockets.exceptions.ConnectionClosed)` in Connection.aclose, the first attempt succeeds. 4) Not in this group, but seen: on each start(), _extensions re-adds --load-extension to _browser_args, so a restart duplicates it (harmless).",
              "chrome_claims": [
                {
