                }
              ],
              "evidence": "Commands: run from my scratch dir /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/R24-reproduce\n1) TMPDIR=$S/tmp PATH=/usr/bin:/bin timeout 120 /home/user/zendriver/.venv/bin/python probe_r24.py\n   zendriver imported from /home/user/zendriver/zendriver/__init__.py ; python 3.13.16\n   find_executable(): FileNotFoundError (no local browser, as intended)\n   Config(host, port): RAISED FileNotFoundError  at config.py:find_executable:453 <- config.py:__init__:90\n   [remote chromium pid=11013 port=56555]\n   zd.start(host, port): RAISED FileNotFoundError: 'could not find a valid browser binary...'  raised at: config.py:find_executable:453 <- config.py:__init__:90 <- util.py:start:80\n   Browser.create(host=, port=): RAISED FileNotFoundError ... <- config.py:__init__:90 <- browser.py:create:216\n   create_from_undetected_chromedriver(driver): RAISED FileNotFoundError ... <- config.py:__init__:90 <- util.py:create_from_undetected_chromedriver:105 ; fake driver.service.stopped = False\n   zd.start(host, port, browser_executable_path='/does/not/exist'): OK ; connected; product = Chrome/141.0.7390.37 | tabs = 1 ; b._process = None\n2) Control, same calls with chromium on PATH: TMPDIR=$S/tmp PATH=$S/../../bin:/usr/bin:/bin timeout 120 .venv/bin/python control_r24.py\n   zd.start(host, port) [chromium on PATH]: OK, product Chrome/141.0.7390.37, _process None\n   create_from_undetected_chromedriver [chromium on PATH]: OK, service.stopped True\nThe fake undetected_chromedriver driver only needs options.debugger_address, browser_pid and service.stop(). All Chromium processes I started were reaped, and the temp dirs were removed.",
              "refinements": "1) The exception happens in Config.__init__, before Browser.start runs. A user-supplied Config(host=..., port=...) therefore cannot be built either, not just the start() and create() helpers. 2) The only workaround today is a dummy browser_executable_path. That works because Browser.start never touches the path when connect_existing is true: the existence check, the _start_process call and the initial-tab wait are all inside `if not connect_existing`. 3) For create_from_undetected_chromedriver the setup is less realistic: undetected_chromedriver itself launched a local Chrome, so the failure only happens when that Chrome is outside PATH and the standard locations (for example, uc was given a custom binary path). The remote or dockerised Chrome case (zd.start(host, port) from a slim client container) is the common one. 4) Side observation, out of scope: one run with a long TMPDIR made my own Chromium fail to bind its singleton socket. That is my test environment, not zendriver. Also, Browser.start's failure path calls stop() with connection=None and _process=None, so _stop returns early and the uc_* temp profile is left behind.",
