          },
          {
            "lens": "refute",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "R24",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "I could not refute this. Config.__init__ (zendriver/core/config.py, line 89-90) runs find_executable(browser) whenever browser_executable_path is falsy, and it does so before it looks at host or port. If no local Chrome, Brave or Edge is on PATH (or in the standard Windows or macOS install locations), zd.start(host, port), Browser.create(host=..., port=...), Config(host=..., port=...) and util.create_from_undetected_chromedriver all raise FileNotFoundError('could not find a valid browser binary...'). The failure happens before any connection attempt. Browser.start never needs the binary on this path: it skips the existence check under `if not connect_existing:` (browser.py, Browser.start, around line 537) and only uses `exe` in _start_process under the same guard. A bogus browser_executable_path therefore connects and navigates normally. The only thing that softens the bug is a workaround: the error message itself tells the user to pass browser_executable_path, and any string works.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "A3.9",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Reproduced on Python 3.13 with the default PATH (no chromium on it). zd.start(host='127.0.0.1', port=P) against a running headless Chrome 141 raised FileNotFoundError from config.py line 90 (Config.__init__ -> find_executable). The create_from_undetected_chromedriver sub-claim, tested with a fake driver object, raised the same error, and that path has no way to pass browser_executable_path, so the only workaround there is changing PATH. That sub-case is narrower, because uc normally implies a local Chrome that the same standard-location search would find."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A9.2",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Reproduced: start(host, port), Browser.create(host, port) and Config(host, port) all raise FileNotFoundError. With browser_executable_path='/does/not/exist' (and also with Config(..., browser_executable_path='x')), the connection succeeds against Chrome/141.0.7390.37 and Tab.get to a data: URL returns the right title. This confirms the binary is never used when connecting to an existing browser."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "B16.12",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Same root cause and same reproduction as A9.2. The docstring of util.start says that with both host and port 'zendriver will not start a local chrome browser!', yet a local binary is still required. The create_from_undetected_chromedriver sub-claim is confirmed with a fake driver (Config() raises)."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "Own probe at /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/R24-refute/probe_remote.py. It launches /opt/pw-browsers/chromium-1194/chrome-linux/chrome by absolute path with --headless=new --remote-debugging-port=P, waits for /json/version, then tries each entry point, all wrapped in asyncio.wait_for(…, 30) and run under timeout 120. With the default PATH (shutil.which('chromium') is None): '1 zd.start(host,port) raised FileNotFoundError could not find a valid browser binary...', '2 Browser.create(host,port) raised FileNotFoundError', '3 Config(host,port) raised FileNotFoundError', '4 create_from_undetected_chromedriver raised FileNotFoundError', '5 zd.start(host,port,bogus exe): connected Chrome/141.0.7390.37 tabs 1 / navigated, title: hi', '6 zd.start(Config(host,port,exe=\"x\")): connected'. In a control run with the chromium symlink on PATH, cases 1-4 all connect. The traceback shows config.py line 90 in __init__ -> find_executable -> raise FileNotFoundError at line 453. Python 3.13.16; the bug does not depend on the Python version. Refutation attempts that failed: (a) there is no upstream serialisation or guard, because Config is built before Browser.start's connect_existing branch; (b) git blame puts the line in the grafted root commit 5c1a005, so the history carries no intent, and the `if not connect_existing:` guard in Browser.start shows the intent was to skip the executable check; (c) no test asserts the current behaviour, and tests/core/test_config.py passes FAKE_BROWSER_PATH precisely to avoid autodetection on machines without a browser; (d) no timeout or close path is involved because the failure is synchronous. I cleaned up: I killed the chrome processes I started (exit 0) and removed their user-data dirs.",
              "refinements": "The bug only triggers when find_executable finds nothing: no chrome, chromium, google-chrome, brave or msedge on PATH (or in PROGRAMFILES/LOCALAPPDATA on Windows, /Applications on macOS, /opt/microsoft/msedge* on Linux). Users connecting to a Chrome on the same machine that has Chrome installed never see it. An easy workaround exists for start(), Browser.create and Config (pass any browser_executable_path; the error message hints at this). There is none for create_from_undetected_chromedriver, which calls bare Config(). The failure is an immediate exception with a clear message, not a hang or data loss.",
              "chrome_claims": [
                {
