              "group_id": "R17",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "Confirmed on real headless Chromium 141 (Python 3.13). Browser.get raises TimeoutError after the full timeout for navigations that never commit (204, Content-Disposition attachment), while Tab.get(url) returns in about 0.11s. With new_tab=True, a timeout leaves the created target alive, and the caller gets no handle to it. The leftover tab is only an orphan from the caller's point of view: it is still tracked in browser.tabs (TargetCreated adds it), it keeps loading, and browser.stop() closes it. It only piles up in retry loops. Headless PDFs are not affected because the PDF viewer commits the navigation.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "SEED-G1",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "browser.get(B+'/slow', new_tab=True, timeout=2) against a server that waits 5s raised TimeoutError after 2.01s. Target.getTargets then showed the new page target, and browser.tabs went from 1 to 2. 5s later the tab had loaded /slow. With /204 and new_tab=True, the target stays (getTargets url '/204'). With /dl and new_tab=True, Chrome closes the download-only tab itself, so no orphan. Refinement: the tab is reachable through browser.tabs and is closed by stop(). It is not a resource leak unless the caller retries in a loop, and closing it on timeout would kill a tab that is legitimately still loading. The behaviour predates the get-timeout PR (the wait was a hardcoded 10s)."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A4.5",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Raw Page.navigate(/204) returned errorText 'net::ERR_ABORTED', isDownload=False. Raw Page.navigate(/dl) returned 'net::ERR_ABORTED', isDownload=True. browser.get(/204, timeout=3) and browser.get(/dl, timeout=3) each raised TimeoutError after 3.01s, while tabs[0].get(/204) and tabs[0].get(/dl) returned in 0.11s. A headless PDF (application/pdf) commits and browser.get returns in 0.01s, so the common 'get a PDF' case is safe. Only explicit attachment downloads, non-renderable MIME types and 204/205 are affected."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "Probes in /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/BA-refute/: r17_probe.py, r17_events.py, r17_pdf.py. r17_events.py shows TargetCreated already carries the requested URL, and Browser.get only completes on the TargetInfoChanged at commit: /slow at 3.02s; for /204 no TargetInfoChanged ever arrives. Browser.get (browser.py, get / wait_for_navigation) wraps only wait_for_navigation in asyncio.wait_for and has no cleanup for the target created by Target.createTarget. git: the 10s wait for TargetInfoChanged came from upstream; e81b66c made it per-target, and b411b45 only parameterised the timeout.",
              "refinements": "The timeout effectively measures time to commit (until the response arrives), not 'navigation start' as the docstring says. An orphan needs either a server slower than timeout (the tab later loads and stays in browser.tabs) or a non-committing response (204/205). Downloads opened with new_tab are closed by Chrome. A4.5 only applies to Browser.get, and so to Tab.get(new_tab=True); Tab.get on an existing tab is fine. Page.navigate's errorText/isDownload could be used to fail fast. Browser.get is owned by the get-timeout PR, so report-only.",
