          {
            "lens": "fixsafety",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "R17",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "Confirmed on real headless Chromium 141 (Python 3.13). (1) If Browser.get(new_tab=True) times out after Target.createTarget, the created tab stays open and the caller never gets a handle to it. (2) Browser.get(url) on an existing tab raises TimeoutError after the full timeout when the navigation never commits (204 or download). Tab.get returns in about 0.1 s for the same URL, and Page.navigate already reports errorText 'net::ERR_ABORTED'. A small local prototype in Browser.get fixes both: it closes the created target when the wait fails and returns early when Page.navigate reports errorText. Browser.get belongs to the get-timeout PR, so this has to be coordinated with that PR.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "SEED-G1",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Real Chromium: browser.get(B+'/slow', new_tab=True, timeout=0.5) raised TimeoutError after 0.51 s, and page targets went from 1 to 2 with the new id left behind. The same happens for new_tab to a 204 URL with timeout=2. The leftover target is still in browser.tabs, so it can be reached but nothing returns or closes it. The prototype closes it only on Exception (TimeoutError, or RuntimeError from _get_tab). It does not close it on an outer cancellation, on purpose."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A4.5",
