                "prototype_dir": "/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/BA-fixsafety/R17/pkg",
                "diff_summary": "Browser.get (zendriver/core/browser.py), diff saved in .../BA-fixsafety/R17/browser.diff. (1) Keep created_target_id from create_target. Add `except Exception:` around the body: if a target was created, `await asyncio.wait_for(self.connection.send(cdp.target.close_target(created_target_id)), timeout)` inside try/except Exception (debug log), then re-raise. The existing finally that removes the handler stays. (2) Existing-tab path: `_, _, error_text, _ = await connection.send(cdp.page.navigate(url))`, and `if error_text: return connection`, since no document is committed and no target change follows.",
                "regression_test_path": "/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/BA-fixsafety/R17/test_r17.py",
                "fails_on_repo": "yes",
                "passes_on_prototype": "yes",
                "mypy_ok": "yes",
                "risks": "Browser.get is owned by the get-timeout PR, so this needs coordination. Cleanup only runs on Exception, not on CancelledError, so the tab is still left open when an outer timeout or cancel hits. This is deliberate: awaiting closeTarget during a cancel could turn a cancel into a hang if Chrome is unresponsive. The close itself is bounded by `timeout`, so a failing get can take up to 2x timeout. Behaviour change: callers who recover a slow tab after TimeoutError via browser.tabs[-1] will find it closed. For navigations that end in errorText but then commit an error page (e.g. connection refused), get() now returns before the error page commits, the same as Tab.get. Must not land before the R25 fix (see refinements). No locks are involved, so there is no re-entrancy or lock-ordering risk. mypy shows no new errors."
              }
            }
          }
        ]
