              "evidence": "Test /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/BA-fixsafety/R25/test_r25.py: 4 real-Chromium tests plus 1 browser-free prune and late-TargetDestroyed test. Repo (printed /home/user/zendriver/zendriver/__init__.py): '5 failed in 14.03s' (3x TimeoutError, 2x AssertionError). Prototype (printed .../BA-fixsafety/R25/pkg/zendriver/__init__.py): '5 passed in 2.94s'. The repo core suite on the prototype gave '105 passed, 1 skipped, 4 deselected', including test_start_waits_for_initial_tab with autodiscover off. The first prototype used next(filter(...), None) and added 2 mypy errors (arg-type, union-attr). Rewriting it as next((t for t in self.targets if ...), None) brings it to 0 new errors. ruff is clean.",
              "refinements": "Partial: with autodiscover off, a closed tab stays in browser.targets until the next update_targets, because no tracking handler runs. So tab.close() followed by browser.get() without update_targets still fails with 'No target with given id found' (verified on the prototype). If that should work, Tab.close should also drop itself from browser.targets by target_id (not with list.remove/==, since Tab.__eq__ compares TargetInfo). Pruning without the tolerant handler would make a late TargetDestroyed raise StopIteration inside a handler task, so both changes must ship together.",
