              "realism": "medium",
              "severity": "crash",
              "fix_assessment": {
                "kind": "minimal-local",
                "prototype_dir": "/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/BA-fixsafety/R41/pkg",
                "diff_summary": "browser.py only, diff saved in .../R41/browser.diff. Import Iterator and ProtocolException. __iter__ -> `return iter(self.tabs)` (Iterator[tab.Tab]); __reversed__ -> `return reversed(self.tabs)`; __next__ deleted. grant_all_permissions: loop over the types, send grant_permissions([p]) for each, collect the accepted ones (catch only ProtocolException with e.code set, otherwise re-raise), then a final grant_permissions(grantable). tile_windows: `with mss.mss() as m:` around the monitor read; `if not num_windows: return []`; req_cols = max_columns or math.ceil(math.sqrt(num_windows)). CookieJar.save/load use a new module-level `_cookie_matches(pattern, cookie)` that runs pattern.search on (domain, name, value).",
                "regression_test_path": "/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/BA-fixsafety/R41/test_r41.py",
                "fails_on_repo": "yes",
                "passes_on_prototype": "yes",
                "mypy_ok": "yes",
                "risks": "Behaviour changes callers could notice. Browser is no longer an Iterator: next(browser) raises TypeError (before, AttributeError unless iter() was called first), and isinstance(browser, Iterator) is False. reversed(browser) returns an iterator instead of a list, so reversed(browser)[0] or len(reversed(browser)) break. Iteration is a snapshot of the tabs, not a live view. grant_all_permissions makes about 39 deprecated grantPermissions calls (about 38 DeprecationWarnings) and silently skips types Chrome rejects (logged at debug level). tile_windows positions change for every caller. Cookie patterns that relied on matching path, flags or enum reprs no longer match, which is the documented behaviour. No locks or async ordering are involved."
