              "refinements": "A2.2 affects every auto-enabled domain, not just Fetch. For Network or Page, a manual X.disable is silently re-enabled on the next send (test_manual_disable_send checks only the lists, right after the disable). It becomes a hang only for Fetch, because a param-less Fetch.enable means pattern '*' at the Request stage. A12.2 has the same leak for Network/Page/etc.: the browser keeps emitting events after the last handler is removed. A side effect is that a later expect_request gets events with no send (this masks R30 A10.7). A12.2 also applies to remove_handlers() with no args and to remove_handlers(cdp.fetch.RequestPaused).",
