                },
                {
                  "fid": "A2.2",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Sequence: add_handler(RequestPaused), fetch.enable(patterns=[*/x]), fetch.disable(), then Page.navigate. The spy shows ('Fetch.enable', {}) sent before Page.navigate and /b gets paused. With a handler that continues only */x, the navigate raises TimeoutError. With a handler that continues everything (the examples/fetch_domain.py style), nothing hangs, but the handler now sees /b and the favicon, so the interception the user turned off is back. The intercept() sub-claim is weaker: a user RequestPaused handler registered before the block had already auto-enabled Fetch({}), so the re-enable after _teardown restores the earlier state. It only adds a new failure when the handler is added inside the block."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "Probes (headless Chromium, Python 3.13.16; each prints zendriver.__file__ = repo copy): /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/BB-refute/probe_r26.py and probe_r26_long.py in the same dir. Output excerpts: 'second nav TimeoutError', 'sent during second nav: [Page.navigate]', 'raw Fetch.requestPaused frames after remove: [.../two]', 'evaluate TimeoutError', 'Page.navigate still pending after 45s' (stack at connection.py Connection.send `return await tx`), 'sent after disable: [(Fetch.enable, {}), (Page.navigate, ...)]', 'navigate /b TimeoutError'. Refute attempts that failed: (a) No upstream serialisation or timeout ends the wait. tab.get is bounded by its own timeout (default 10s, raises TimeoutError), but tab.send / evaluate have no bound. websockets keepalive does not end it because the connection stays alive. (b) Git history: `git log -S 'self.enabled_domains.remove(ed)'` shows the remove-without-disable came in with old commit 5c1a005, so it is inherited and not a deliberate decision. PR #253 (3368e54) added manual tracking with the comment 'manual enables/disables always overwrite auto enabled domains'. test_manual_disable_send only checks state right after the disable, and no test asserts the re-enable. My reading is that the re-enable on the next send is unintended.",
              "refinements": "A12.2: hangs only for Fetch, or another domain whose enable changes browser behaviour. For Network, Page and similar domains, leaving them enabled just keeps events flowing, and R30 shows that this sometimes helps. It hits only users who relied on auto-enable, meaning they never sent Fetch.enable themselves, and who do not send Fetch.disable after removing the handler. Fetch.disable sent before or after remove_handlers avoids it. A2.2: needs a RequestPaused handler still registered after the manual Fetch.disable. It hangs only if that handler does not continue requests outside the user's original pattern; otherwise the result is silent interception of every URL. Any later zendriver call triggers the re-enable, including _is_update ones such as tab.wait(), because aopen() calls _register_handlers.",
