          {
            "lens": "fixsafety",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "R26",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "Both members reproduce, against a fake peer and in real headless Chromium. (1) Connection._register_handlers drops an auto-enabled domain from enabled_domains when its last handler goes, but never sends X.disable. For an auto-enabled Fetch (default '*' pattern, Request stage), every later request stays paused and navigation hangs. (2) A manual X.disable takes the domain out of both lists in _update_manual_domain. On the next send, _register_handlers sees the handler that is still registered and sends a param-less X.enable, so Fetch pauses every request again and Page.navigate hangs. The fix for (1) is a safe local change only if it is limited to Fetch. (2) needs a design decision: a sticky manual disable changes behaviour that zendriver itself depends on.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "A2.2",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Fake peer: after add_handler(RequestPaused), fetch.enable(patterns=['*/x']) and fetch.disable(), the next send emitted [('Fetch.enable', {}), ('Page.reload', {})]. Real Chromium: tab.send(page.navigate(c.html)) TIMED OUT after 6s, with the paused list ['http://127.0.0.1:.../c.html']. Mechanism: _update_manual_domain('disable') removes fetch from both lists, then _register_handlers re-enables it with no params. The intercept()+user-handler variant restores the earlier auto-enabled state, which may be what that user wants. So whether this is a bug depends on the intended semantics."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A12.2",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Fake peer: after remove_handlers(RequestPaused, h), the next send emitted only ['Page.reload'] (no Fetch.disable) and enabled_domains was []. Real Chromium: navigation worked while a continuing handler was registered. After remove_handlers, tab.send(page.navigate(b.html)) TIMED OUT after 6s while zendriver reported enabled_domains []."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "Scratch tests in /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/BB-fixsafety/tests/test_r26.py. Repo (zendriver from /home/user/zendriver/zendriver/__init__.py): '2 failed, 1 passed'. Prototype (zendriver from .../BB-fixsafety/R26/pkg/zendriver/__init__.py): '3 passed'. Chrome probe probes/probe_r26_chrome.py on the repo printed 'A12.2 after remove: navigate TIMED OUT; enabled []' and 'A2.2 after manual disable: navigate TIMED OUT; paused [.../c.html]'. On the prototype it printed 'A12.2 after remove: navigate OK' and 'A2.2 after manual disable: navigate OK; paused []'. Fix-safety probe: in Chrome, Network.disable followed by Network.getResponseBody raises 'No resource with given identifier found', even after a re-enable. A variant that disables every dropped domain (ed.disable()) made expect_response(...).response_body fail with that ProtocolException (probes/probe_r26_generic.py). The repo code and the Fetch-only prototype both returned the body.",
              "refinements": "The fix for A12.2 must not be generic. expect_response's three Network handlers remove themselves before response_body sends Network.getResponseBody, so disabling Network when its last handler goes deletes the body; this was demonstrated in Chrome. DOM.disable would also invalidate node ids. Only Fetch (and arguably Debugger) is harmful when left enabled. The disable only happens on the next send(), not inside remove_handlers (which is sync). A2.2 is a semantics question. My sticky-disable prototype passes the A2.2 test but breaks two behaviours that pass on the repo, shown in tests/test_r26_behaviour_change.py (repo '2 passed', prototype '2 failed'). (a) tab.xpath() sends dom.enable(_is_update=True) and then disable_dom_agent() -> DOM.disable, after which a user's DOM handler would never get DOM re-enabled. (b) A user handler that relied on auto-enabled Fetch loses it after a tab.intercept() block's teardown Fetch.disable.",
              "chrome_claims": [
                {
                  "claim": "An auto-enabled Fetch left on after the last handler is removed makes navigation hang",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "Real headless Chromium probe: navigate wait_for 6s timed out on the repo, succeeded on the prototype"
                },
                {
                  "claim": "After a manual Fetch.disable, the param-less Fetch.enable on the next send pauses the navigation request",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "Real Chromium probe: c.html paused, navigate timed out on the repo; OK on the prototype"
                },
                {
                  "claim": "Network.disable drops buffered response bodies, so a generic disable-on-drop breaks expect_response.response_body",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "Chromium: getResponseBody after Network.disable -> 'No resource with given identifier found'; the generic-variant prototype failed response_body"
                }
              ],
              "realism": "medium",
              "severity": "hang",
              "fix_assessment": {
                "kind": "design-api",
                "prototype_dir": "/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/BB-fixsafety/R26/pkg",
                "diff_summary": "connection.py: (A12.2, safe and local) in _register_handlers' drop loop, skip domains a concurrent call already removed, and when the dropped domain is cdp.fetch, await self.send(cdp.fetch.disable(), _is_update=True) inside try/except Exception with a debug log. (A2.2, design) new manually_disabled_domains list: _update_manual_domain('disable') adds to it and 'enable' removes from it; _register_handlers skips domains in it and prunes entries whose domain no longer has handlers; aclose/_detach_session clear it.",
                "regression_test_path": "/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/BB-fixsafety/tests/test_r26.py",
                "fails_on_repo": "yes",
                "passes_on_prototype": "yes",
                "mypy_ok": "yes",
                "risks": "The sticky manual-disable part changes behaviour zendriver relies on. Internal enable/disable pairs (tab.xpath/disable_dom_agent, element.py DOM/Overlay, intercept._teardown Fetch.disable) would silently stop auto-enable for user handlers on those domains (tests/test_r26_behaviour_change.py: repo 2 passed, prototype 2 failed). It needs a decision, for example: mark only user-issued disables as sticky, or have intercept restore the earlier state. A Fetch-only disable-on-drop is safe. Re-entrancy: the nested send(_is_update=True) -> aopen -> _register_handlers does not recurse, because fetch is already removed. Generalising it to every domain breaks response_body (Network) and node ids (DOM). The enable path's bare 'except:' swallows CancelledError, so the new code uses 'except Exception'. Repo tests tests/core/test_tab.py + test_connection.py: 47 passed on the prototype (same as base). No new mypy errors."
              }
            }
          }
        ]
