            "lens": "refute",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "R30",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "All four members reproduce on real headless Chromium. (1) A timed-out asyncio.wait_for on exp.value or interception.request cancels the shared stored future. The behaviour is the same on Python 3.11, 3.12 and 3.13. A later await then raises CancelledError in a task that was not cancelled, and the event handler's set_result raises InvalidStateError. (2) expect_request/expect_response only call add_handler. If the block makes no zendriver call, Network is never enabled and a page-initiated request is never seen. (3) Network.loadingFailed is never handled, so response_body, or value/response for a request blocked before any response, waits forever.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "A10.3",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "After a timed-out wait the future is cancelled (<Future cancelled>). The second wait raised CancelledError with current_task().cancelling()==0, and the loop exception handler got InvalidStateError. Intercept: after a timed-out wait_for(i.request, 0.5), continue_request() raised CancelledError and the paused navigation stayed pending inside the block. Leaving the block (Fetch.disable) released it. A pure-asyncio check gives the same result on 3.11.17, 3.12.3 and 3.13.16. It needs a retry on the same expectation after a timeout; reset() or asyncio.shield avoid it."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A10.7",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Fresh tab on a page polling every 300ms, enabled_domains == []: a pure `await shield(r.value)` saw nothing for 4s. Narrowing refinement: ANY zendriver call in the block resolves it, including tab.wait(1), which only sends an _is_update Target.getTargetInfo, because aopen() always runs _register_handlers. Also, if Network was enabled on that target earlier, for example by an earlier expect block that made a send, Chrome keeps sending Network events because zendriver never sends Network.disable, and a pure await resolves (probe_a10_7b.py)."
                },
