            "entry": {
              "group_id": "R37",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "Confirmed and deterministic. Every DOM.getDocument, deep or shallow, discards the node ids issued by the previous one. Each Tab.query_selector(_all) (tab.py:516/622) and each Element.query_selector(_all)/update() (element.py:293/821/828) calls getDocument and then uses ids from it, and nothing serialises them: Connection.send has no lock, and only _open_lock/_attach_lock exist. With N concurrent queries on one tab, exactly N-2 return None/[] silently: N=2 is rescued by the single retry, N=3 loses 1, N=10 loses 8. Results were identical on Python 3.11.17 and 3.13.16. The __last flag left set after a successful retry makes that element's next stale-id hit return None at once (shown with a control). Mitigation: the retrying high-level APIs (tab.select/select_all/find with a timeout) re-poll every 0.5s and hide the problem, except that select_all(include_frames=True) returns partial results.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "A5.2",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "20-row table: gathering row.query_selector('td') gave 18/20 None in 3 of 3 runs, with 1 __last leftover each time. Gathering row.query_selector_all gave 18/20 empty. 10x gathered tab.query_selector('#topp') gave 8 None, and 10x tab.query_selector_all('p') gave lens [0,0,0,0,0,0,0,0,2,2]. Sequential control: 0/20 None. Scaling: n=2 gives 0, n=3 gives 1, n=4 gives 2, n=5 gives 3, n=10 gives 8 None, stable over 5 repeats on both 3.11 and 3.13. __last control: after a sequential stale id, the leftover element's tab.query_selector('td', elem) returned None while 4 clean elements succeeded. Not affected: 10x gathered tab.select(timeout=3) and 2x gathered find() all succeeded."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "Probes /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/BC-refute/probe_r37.py, probe_r37_scale.py (run on 3.13.16 with the repo copy, and on 3.11.17 with a scratch copy of the package, both since removed) and probe_last.py. Chrome check: 'query on d1 after 2nd getDocument FAILS: Could not find node with given id ids NodeId(1) NodeId(71)', 'after shallow getDocument, d2 FAILS'. 'gather row.query_selector None count: 18 of 20; __last leftovers: 1' (x3), 'gather 10x tab.query_selector None: 8', 'gather 10x tab.select (retrying API): all Element', 'leftover flags [F,F,F,T,F]' -> 'leftover -> None', the clean ones -> TD. Upstream serialisation: Connection.send (connection.py:616) takes no lock. Internal multi-frame search (_search_frames, tab.py:248) is sequential and targets separate sessions, so the library never triggers this itself; only user-level concurrency on one tab does.",
              "refinements": "This only happens when user code runs 3 or more DOM queries concurrently on the same Tab, for example asyncio.gather over rows or several tasks polling one tab. Two overlapping queries are rescued by the one-shot retry. Python version does not matter: the race spans CDP round-trips, not event-loop micro-ordering. Only the no-retry APIs fail silently: tab.query_selector(_all), Element.query_selector(_all), and select_all(include_frames=True) with partial results. select/select_all/find/wait_for loop until their timeout and succeed. The leftover __last flag only matters on a later stale-id hit for that same element; the public Element.query_selector calls update() first, so sequential use afterwards is unaffected. Realism is medium, since gathering element queries is a natural speed-up. A proper fix needs a design change (serialise per tab, or avoid a getDocument per query).",
