                }
              ],
              "evidence": "Probe /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/BD-fixsafety/probe_r33.py on the repo printed: '[apply throw] OK -> None', '[apply reject] OK -> {}', '[evaluate throw] EXC ProtocolException', '[apply rbv False] -> RemoteObject object_id= None value= {}', '[raw callFunctionOn throw] ... result[1] is None? False ExceptionDetails(...)', '[clear_input textarea] OK -> None / textarea value after: some text', '[clear_input_by_deleting textarea] ... some text', '[get_js_attributes circ] EXC TypeError: the JSON object must be str...'. Regression test tests/test_r33.py (7 tests). On the repo (zendriver from /home/user/zendriver/zendriver/__init__.py): '6 failed, 1 passed in 6.89s'. Only test_apply_still_returns_values passes. On the prototype (.../BD-fixsafety/pkg): '7 passed in 7.64s'. The repo's tests/core/test_react_controlled_input.py passes on the prototype, so the React native-setter behaviour is kept.",
              "refinements": "New, related and pre-existing: clear_input_by_deleting() on <input type=email> or type=number silently does nothing. setSelectionRange throws InvalidStateError for those types, and apply swallows the error. This dates from before b506c8f, which already called setSelectionRange(0,0). It was found when test_clear_input_still_works_on_input failed on the repo with 'a@b.cd' != ''. Once apply raises, these login-form fields would start raising ProtocolException, so the fix must wrap setSelectionRange in try/catch (the prototype does). Once apply raises, clear_input_by_deleting on an element with no value property (e.g. a div) raises a TypeError from n.value.length instead of returning silently. Element.__call__ ('el(\"nonexistent\")'), record_video (captureStream on a cross-origin video) and get_js_attributes on circular objects will raise ProtocolException instead of returning None or raising a misleading TypeError. Handles returned by apply(return_by_value=False) are never released (see R46 A8.13). Element.apply has the same unserializable-value gap as Tab.evaluate (A6.9).",
