            "lens": "reproduce",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "R46",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "All 7 design-level members reproduce on real headless Chromium 141 with Python 3.13. save_to_dom corrupts the DOM in all three sub-cases. resolveNode handles stay alive and add per-call renderer memory. Element and Tab are unhashable. Nested or overlapping tab.intercept() mis-routes requests, and after the inner block exits, Fetch is re-enabled with no patterns, which pauses every request including the document. evaluate drops unserializable values. `async with await zd.start()` leaves Chrome running.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "A8.10",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "#cmt: outerHTML went from '<div id=\"cmt\"><!-- c --><i>it</i></div>' to '<div id=\"cmt\" data-x=\"1\">&lt;#comment &gt;<!--#comment--><i>it</i></div>', and innerText became '<#comment >it'. #esc: '&lt;b&gt;not bold&lt;/b&gt;' became a real <b> element (querySelectorAll('#esc b').length == 1). #q: str(el) is '<div id=\"q\" title=\"say \"hi\"\" ...>' and save_to_dom raised ProtocolException \"InvalidCharacterError 'hi\"\"' is not a valid attribute name\". The title was still damaged afterwards (title=\"say \")."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A8.13",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "2000 apply() calls gave 2000 distinct object ids. The first id still worked after all of them (callFunctionOn this.id == 'inp'), and only an explicit Runtime.releaseObject made it 'Could not find object'. The JS heap (Runtime.getHeapUsage after GC) did not grow, because the table lives in inspector native memory. Renderer RSS grew 3.6 MB over 30000 apply() calls, against 1.0 MB for 30000 resolve+call+release calls: about 90-120 bytes per call. The leak is real but small, and it is freed when the execution context goes away."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A8.17",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "hash(element) and set(await tab.select_all('input, div')) raised TypeError: unhashable type: 'Element'."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A7.11",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "hash(tab) and set(browser.tabs) raised TypeError: unhashable type: 'Tab'. Tab.__hash__ is None while Connection.__hash__ is object's. Tab.__eq__ compares the whole TargetInfo (other.target == self.target), checked by reading the code."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A10.2",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Scenario 1, nested *a.json* and *b.json* (XHR, REQUEST): a.request.url == b.request.url == /b.json. a.continue_request() continued b's request, and b.continue_request() raised ProtocolException 'Invalid state for continueInterceptedRequest'. Scenario 2, inner block exits first: 'fetch in manually_enabled_domains: False | RequestPaused handlers: 1'. tab.get(page2) was still pending after 8s, stuck at tab.py:512 on Page.navigate. The outer a.request.url was the /page2 document, and evaluate timed out. Refinement: tab.get has timeout=10 by default, so get raises TimeoutError instead of hanging forever, but the paused requests stay stuck until Fetch is disabled. Also, Chrome's Fetch domain classifies fetch() requests as XHR: ResourceType.FETCH patterns matched nothing in my run."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A6.9",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "evaluate('1/0'), evaluate('Math.max()'), evaluate('NaN'), evaluate('-0'), evaluate('2n**64n') and evaluate('10n') all returned None. The raw RemoteObject had value=None and unserializable_value 'Infinity', '-Infinity', 'NaN', '-0', '18446744073709551616n' and '10n' respectively, so the results look the same as undefined or null. evaluate('NaN', return_by_value=False) returned 'NaN'."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "B16.10",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "After `async with await zd.start(headless=True) as b: pass` and a 2s wait: b.stopped False, pid still alive, connection still open. Only an explicit await b.stop() killed it. examples/wait_for_page.py and examples/expect_download.py use this pattern."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "Commands: export PATH=.../scratchpad/bin:$PATH; cd /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/BD-reproduce && timeout 120 /home/user/zendriver/.venv/bin/python probe_r46a.py (save_to_dom, evaluate, hash, handle liveness, async with). Then timeout 120 ... probe_r46b.py (nested intercept against a local http.server). Then timeout 125 ... probe_r46d.py (renderer RSS with and without releaseObject); probe_r46c.py showed the JS heap delta is 0. Key lines: \"after outerHTML: '<div id=\\\"cmt\\\" data-x=\\\"1\\\">&lt;#comment &gt;<!--#comment--><i>it</i></div>'\", '#esc now contains <b> elements: 1', \"save_to_dom raised ProtocolException: InvalidCharacterError 'hi\\\"\\\"' is not a valid attribute name.\", \"evaluate('1/0') -> None raw.value=None unserializable_value=UnserializableValue('Infinity')\", \"[hash Tab] TypeError: unhashable type: 'Tab'\", '2000 apply() calls -> 2000 distinct object ids', 'first object id still alive after 2000 calls: this.id = inp', \"a.request.url: 'http://127.0.0.1:47745/b.json'\", 'b.continue_request(): ProtocolException: Invalid state for continueInterceptedRequest', 'tab.get(page2) still pending after 8s ... tab.py, line 512 ... Page.navigate status: pending', \"outer a.request.url: 'http://127.0.0.1:47745/page2'\", '[B16.10] after async with: stopped? False pid 12315 pid alive: True connection open: True'.",
              "refinements": "A10.2: tab.get times out at its default 10s rather than hanging forever, but the page's other requests stay paused for good. Use ResourceType.XHR to intercept fetch() calls. A8.13: the growth is about 100 bytes per call in renderer native memory (not the JS heap) and ends with the execution context, so it is low impact. All the other members reproduce exactly as claimed. These are API and design decisions: escaping or serialising for save_to_dom, a hash based on backend_node_id or target_id, pattern-aware or ref-counted Fetch interception, decoding unserializable_value, and whether __aexit__ should call stop().",
