          {
            "lens": "refute",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "R46",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "Every member reproduced against real Chromium or in pure Python. save_to_dom sends the unescaped __repr__, which corrupts comments and escaped text and fails on quotes. Handles from DOM.resolveNode are never released and keep removed (detached) nodes alive until navigation. Element and Tab are unhashable. Nested tab.intercept() blocks clobber each other, and an inner block that exits first makes Fetch re-enable with no patterns, which stalls page loads. evaluate returns None for Infinity, NaN, -0 and BigInt. `async with await zd.start()` leaves Chrome running until asyncio_atexit runs at event-loop close.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "A8.10",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Real Chromium. The #cmt outerHTML becomes '<div id=\"cmt\" data-x=\"1\">&lt;#comment &gt;<!--#comment--><i>it</i></div>' with innerText '<#comment >it'. #esc becomes '<p ...><b>not bold</b></p>', so the escaped text is now live markup. #q raises ProtocolException InvalidCharacterError, and the element is left with title=\"say \". A plain element round-trips correctly."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A8.13",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "5000 apply() calls produce 5000 distinct object ids, and the first id still resolves afterwards. Refute angle: handles to the same live node cost about 0 B/call of V8 heap (0.52MB to 0.52MB). The real cost is retention of detached nodes. Replacing a 200KB node 400 times with an apply each time grew renderer USS from 43MB to 136MB, against 21MB to 43MB for the control without apply. The first detached handle was still alive (isConnected=False, textContent length 200018). Memory was freed on reload, back to 55MB. This only matters in long-lived single-page apps that replace nodes without navigating. Realism is medium-low."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A8.17",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "set(await tab.select_all('a')) raises TypeError unhashable type: 'Element' (real Chromium). __eq__ dates from the initial import 5c1a005 and no __hash__ was ever defined. The failure is loud, not silent. backend_node_id gives a stable key."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A7.11",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "hash(browser.main_tab) and set(browser.tabs) raise TypeError unhashable type: 'Tab' (real Chromium). zendriver's own code keeps sets of TargetIDs, not Tabs, so nothing breaks internally. Only user code that hashes tabs is affected, and the failure is loud."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A10.2",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Real Chromium with a local HTTP server, fetch() reported as ResourceType.XHR. Scenario 1 (nested, '*a.json*' outside '*b.json*'): the outer a.request resolves to b.json, the inner b.request also resolves to b.json, a.continue_request continues b's request, and b.continue_request raises 'Invalid state for continueInterceptedRequest'. a.json was never paused, so the inner Fetch.enable replaced the pattern list. Scenario 2 (inner block exits first): fetch is no longer in manually_enabled_domains but 1 handler is still registered. The next send re-enables Fetch with no patterns, so a resolves with the first paused request and b.json stays paused, leaving window.__b None. A navigation inside the outer block stalls: tab.evaluate of the title timed out after 8s. It completes only after the outer block exits. A second RequestPaused event also raised an unretrieved InvalidStateError in _response_handler. The user-installed RequestPaused handler variant was reasoned, not run. The intercept docstring does not say nesting is unsupported. Realism is medium-low."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A6.9",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
