            "lens": "fixsafety",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "R46",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "All seven design-level claims reproduce on real Chromium 141 with Python 3.13. save_to_dom re-parses the unescaped __repr__: a comment becomes visible '<#comment >' text, escaped text becomes a live <b>, and a quote in an attribute value makes DOM.setOuterHTML raise while still leaving the attribute truncated in the DOM. Every apply() leaves a new, never-released DOM.resolveNode handle (500 calls gave 500 live ids, and the first still resolves). Element and Tab define __eq__ without __hash__, so both are unhashable. Nested tab.intercept() blocks hand the outer interception the inner request. If the inner block exits first, Fetch is auto-re-enabled with no patterns, every request pauses and tab.get() hangs. evaluate() returns None for Infinity, -Infinity, NaN, -0 and BigInt. `async with await zd.start()` never stops Chrome. Hashing, evaluate decoding, Browser.__aexit__ and save_to_dom have small safe fixes, which I prototyped. The handle leak and intercept nesting need design work.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "A8.10",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "(a) #cmt outerHTML became '<div id=\"cmt\" data-x=\"1\">&lt;#comment &gt;<!--#comment--><i>it</i></div>' and innerText became '<#comment >it'. (b) #esc became '<p ...><b>not bold</b></p>'. (c) #q raised ProtocolException InvalidCharacterError, and the DOM was still changed to title=\"say \". The DOM is corrupted even though the call failed (refinement)."
