          {
            "lens": "refute",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "R38",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "I tried to refute all four keys.py members and could not. Each one reproduces in real headless Chromium 141 on Python 3.13. The most serious is A10.5: send_keys(str) sends each emoji grapheme as one 'char' event, and Chrome rejects any text of 4 or more UTF-16 units. So skin-toned emoji, flags and ZWJ sequences raise ProtocolException partway through typing. The other three are wrong-result bugs with lower realism. They only appear on the DOWN_AND_UP path (from_mixed_input, SpecialKeys, or a KeyEvents object the user builds and reuses).",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "A10.5",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "In real Chromium I called send_keys('x'+s+'y') for each case. Thumbs-up (2 UTF-16 units) and e+combining acute (2 units) both work. Thumbs-up with a skin tone (4), the DE flag (4), the family ZWJ emoji (11) and a+3 combining marks (4) each raise ProtocolException \"Invalid 'text' parameter\", and the field is left holding 'x'. Nothing upstream splits graphemes: KeyEvents.from_text passes the whole grapheme to to_cdp_events(CHAR). The repo test test_visible_events types a thumbs-up emoji, so emoji support is intended."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A10.9",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "In KeyEvents.to_down_up_sequence, step 3 releases the modifiers in press order, although its comment says reverse order. It does this before step 4 (main keyup), which then carries modifiers=0. Chrome log for Ctrl+Enter: keyup Control (ctrl=false), then keyup Enter (ctrl=false). For 'A': keyup Shift, then keyup 'a' (shift=false). For Ctrl+Shift+a, Control is released before Shift. Keydown carries the correct modifiers, so shortcuts handled on keydown still work (Ctrl+A/C/V in the repo test)."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A10.10",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Offline, KeyEvents('A').to_cdp_events(DOWN_AND_UP) gives Shift plus text 'A' the first time and plain 'a' with no Shift the second time. For '!' the second call gives '1'. In real Chromium, sending the same KeyEvents('A') object twice types 'Aa'. Cause: to_cdp_events assigns self.key = _normalise_key(...). zendriver itself never reuses an instance (from_text and send_keys build new ones), so only user code that reuses the public class is affected."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A10.11",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "In real Chromium, send_keys(SpecialKeys.SPACE) and from_mixed_input([' ']) produce keydown key=' ' with code '' (empty) and keyCode 32. TAB gives code 'Tab'. A space is still inserted, and e.key and e.keyCode are correct, so only handlers that check e.code === 'Space' miss it. send_keys(' ') with a str goes through CHAR and sends no keydown at all."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "Probes: /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/BE-refute/keys_offline.py and /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/BE-refute/keys_real.py. These ran against the repo copy (zendriver.__file__ = /home/user/zendriver/zendriver/__init__.py), headless Chromium 141, Python 3.13.16, with a document-level keydown/keypress/keyup logger. Key output: \"A10.5 '<thumbs-up+skin tone>' utf16 4 -> EXC ProtocolException: Invalid 'text' parameter ... value= 'x'\"; \"A10.9 Ctrl+Enter [...keyup:Control:ctrl=false, keyup:Enter:ctrl=false]\"; \"A10.11 SPACE ['keydown:\\\" \\\"::32', ...]\" (empty code); \"A10.10 reuse value= 'Aa'\". git log -S shows the code arrived in bulk in 5c1a005 and d4d8117. Nothing in history marks the behaviour as deliberate, and no test asserts it.",
              "refinements": "A10.5: this is a Chrome limit (text must be under 4 UTF-16 units). Single-codepoint emoji and short combining sequences work; it fails on skin-tone modifiers, flags, keycaps, ZWJ families and long combining clusters. Earlier graphemes have already been typed when it raises. A10.9/A10.11: only the DOWN_AND_UP path is affected (from_mixed_input, SpecialKeys, explicit KeyEvents). Plain send_keys(str) uses CHAR, sends no keydown/keyup, and so cannot show these two bugs. A10.9 matters only to keyup handlers that read modifiers or e.key. Real keyboards also produce Shift-up before A-up during rollover, so robust pages already cope. A10.10 needs explicit reuse of a KeyEvents object.",
