            "lens": "fixsafety",
            "entry": {
              "group_id": "R40",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "Tab.set_window_state (tab.py, set_window_state) has two bugs, confirmed on headless Chromium. (1) A normal-state request with geometry while the window is maximized, minimized or fullscreen only restores the window; Chrome ignores the bounds and leaves it at the restore geometry. (2) For min, max and full it first calls set_window_state(state='normal') with the defaults 0,0,1280,720. That moves and resizes a normal window and overwrites its restore geometry. The fix is to restore with Bounds(window_state=NORMAL) and no geometry, only when the current state is not normal, and then apply the target.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "A6.6",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Headless: after maximize, minimize or fullscreen, set_window_size(200,100,900,700) gives state=normal l=0 t=0 w=1280 h=720. Headful under Xvfb (no WM) the fullscreen case reproduces the same way. The prototype ends at 200,100,900,700 in both."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A6.7",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "Headless: sized to 100,50,800,600, then tab.maximize(), then a raw restore gives 0,0,1280,720. A raw maximize as control restores to 100,50,800,600. Under Xvfb, maximize is a no-op without a WM, yet the window still jumps to 0,0,1280,720."
                }
              ],
              "evidence": "Probe /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/BE-fixsafety/probes/win_probe.py on the repo: 'set_window_size(200,100,900,700) after maximize: state=normal l=0 t=0 w=1280 h=720' (same after minimize and fullscreen); 'A plain restore: state=normal l=0 t=0 w=1280 h=720' against 'B plain restore: state=normal l=100 t=50 w=800 h=600'. Raw transitions MIN->MAX, FULL->MAX, FULL->MIN and MIN->FULL are rejected with 'restore it to normal state first'. Probe probes/win_headful.py under xvfb-run: on the repo, fullscreen then set_window_size ends at 0,0,1280,720; on the prototype it ends at 200,100,900,700. Regression test /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/BE-fixsafety/R40/tests/test_r40_window.py (browser-free fake of Chrome's SetWindowBounds state machine via an instance-patched tab.send, plus one real-Chromium test). Repo: '7 failed, 1 passed in 0.94s'; the passing test checks non-normal to non-normal transitions and is a guard. Prototype: '8 passed in 1.12s'. The repo's tests/core/test_tab.py passes on the prototype (42 passed).",
              "refinements": "The headless reset size is the window state's default (1280x720). Getting into the maximized, minimized or fullscreen states still needs the window to be normal first, so the restore step must stay; it just must not carry geometry, and it can be skipped when the window is already normal. Under Xvfb with no window manager, maximize and minimize do nothing in headful mode, but A6.7's move and resize still happens.",
              "chrome_claims": [
                {
                  "claim": "Browser.setWindowBounds(windowState=normal plus geometry) on a non-normal window restores it and ignores the geometry, without an error",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "win_probe.py headless; win_headful.py fullscreen case under Xvfb"
                },
                {
                  "claim": "Minimized/fullscreen to another non-normal state is rejected unless the window is restored first",
                  "status": "verified",
                  "how": "Raw CDP transitions in win_probe.py"
                },
                {
                  "claim": "In headful mode with a real window manager, the restore finishes before the following setWindowBounds, so the bounds apply",
                  "status": "assumed",
                  "how": "Only tested under Xvfb without a WM (fullscreen case works). A WM that restores asynchronously could still make Chrome see IsMaximized() and ignore the bounds."
                }
              ],
              "realism": "medium",
              "severity": "wrong-result",
              "fix_assessment": {
                "kind": "minimal-local",
                "prototype_dir": "/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/BE-fixsafety/R40/pkg",
                "diff_summary": "tab.py set_window_state. The recursive `await self.set_window_state(state='normal')` is replaced. If the current bounds.window_state (from get_window) is not None or NORMAL, send set_window_bounds(window_id, Bounds(window_state=NORMAL)) first, with no geometry. Then send the target: Bounds(left, top, width, height, NORMAL) for normal, or Bounds(window_state=target) otherwise.",
                "regression_test_path": "/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/BE-fixsafety/R40/tests/test_r40_window.py",
                "fails_on_repo": "yes",
                "passes_on_prototype": "yes",
                "mypy_ok": "yes",
                "risks": "No locks or re-entrancy are involved; the recursion is removed. maximize, minimize and fullscreen on a normal window no longer move or resize it first, and the restore geometry is kept. medimize() from maximized still ends at 0,0,1280,720, because it restores and then applies its default bounds. A non-normal window costs one extra round trip, as before. Residual risk: a real WM in headful mode may restore asynchronously, and then the bounds would still be ignored silently. Polling get_window until the state is normal, with a bound, would harden that, but it was not tested here. mypy: 332 errors, the same as an untouched copy. ruff is clean."
              }
            }
