              "group_id": "R31",
              "verdict": "confirmed",
              "summary": "All five download-state members reproduce against real headless Chromium. Browser.setDownloadBehavior is sent without browser_context_id or events_enabled, and the state is tracked per Tab although it applies per context. Effects: set_download_path and expect_download on a create_context tab do nothing, and ex.value hangs. download_file inside expect_download disables events, so ex.value hangs and the file is written despite 'deny'. One tab's download_file or expect_download silently redirects another tab's downloads to ./downloads or ~/Downloads.",
              "member_verdicts": [
                {
                  "fid": "A7.2",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "ctx_tab = create_context(url); set_download_path(dirCtx); clicking the download link saves no file anywhere. Inside async with ctx_tab.expect_download(), clicking gives no DownloadWillBegin within 5s, and the stuck task is at expect.py:236 DownloadExpectation.value `return await self.future`. Control: the same command with browser_context_id=ctx_tab.target.browser_context_id and events_enabled=True delivers DownloadWillBegin 'fromlink.txt' and saves dirCtxExplicit/fromlink.txt. The side effect on the default context was not tested separately."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A7.3",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "On a fresh tab, a control link click inside expect_download delivers DownloadWillBegin. With `async with tab.expect_download() as ex: await tab.download_file(base+'/docs/data.txt','x.txt')`, no DownloadWillBegin arrives within 5s (stack stuck at expect.py:236). The listing shows downloads/x.txt even though the expectation set deny. download_file -> set_download_path sends setDownloadBehavior(allow, path) without events_enabled."
                },
                {
                  "fid": "A7.4",
                  "verdict": "confirmed",
                  "note": "tab_a.set_download_path(dirA) and a link click put the file in dirA. Then tab_b.download_file(url,'b.txt') on a new tab, then another tab_a link click: the listing is {'downloads': ['b.txt','fromlink.txt']} and dirA is empty, while tab_a._download_behavior still says ['allow', dirA]."
                },
