              "evidence": "Probe /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/BE-fixsafety/probes/dl_probe.py (HOME redirected) on the repo: 'A7.3 TIMEOUT: no DownloadWillBegin', 'files in work: [downloads/x.txt]'; 'A7.4 files: [downloads/b.txt, downloads/fromA.txt, ...]'; 'A10.6 ... in HOME/Downloads: [second.txt]'; 'A7.2 after ctx set_download_path, click: saved: False', 'ctx expect_download TIMEOUT', 'with explicit browser_context_id saved: True'; 'default ctx explicit id EXC: Failed to find browser context for id'. Regression test /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-zendriver/b2021b39-94e6-5220-9e66-7f33c0314a80/scratchpad/verify/BE-fixsafety/R31/tests/test_r31_download.py (real Chromium; every test starts its own browser). Repo: '5 failed, 1 passed in 13.40s'; the passing test is the import check, and the A7.3 test fails with TimeoutError. Prototype: '6 passed in 5.73s'. The repo's tests/core/test_tab.py, test_browser.py and test_connection.py give 61 passed on the prototype, including test_expect_download.",
              "refinements": "Fix-safety finding: the obvious per-context fix is not safe on its own. Version 1 of the prototype captured the previous behaviour at __aenter__, marked the context 'deny' and restored the captured value at __aexit__. With two expectations that overlap without nesting (A enters, B enters, A exits, B exits), it left the context permanently denied: B restores 'deny', and later downloads vanish silently. The repo itself sends those to ~/Downloads. The added test test_overlapping_expectations_do_not_leave_downloads_denied caught it. The fix needs a per-context count of active expectations, restoring the current per-context state only when the last one exits. Chrome also rejects the default context's id, so browser_context_id may only be sent for ids listed by Target.getBrowserContexts.",
