              "summary": "A11.5 and A11.6 are real. ContraDict.update(), setdefault() and |= bypass __setitem__ and store plain dicts. copy.copy and pickle rebuild without __init__, so they get a separate real __dict__ that keeps deleted keys readable as attributes. A11.8 also reproduces, but it is the class's documented conversion design ('dict assignments will be converted too'), not a defect. A plain dict assigned to a ContraDict is copied too. A key finding for the fix: overriding update/setdefault alone does NOT work. __getattribute__ sends warning names such as 'update' and 'setdefault' to getattr(super(), name), which is dict's method, so the overrides are bypassed when called as c.update(...).",
